WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:21.670 --> 00:00:23.510
Good evening, everyone.

00:00:23.510 --> 00:00:25.750
Welcome to the MIT Museum.

00:00:25.750 --> 00:00:27.370
My name is Brindha Muniappan.

00:00:27.370 --> 00:00:30.160
I'm the Director of
Programs here at the museum.

00:00:30.160 --> 00:00:32.800
And I'd like to welcome
you to tonight's event,

00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:37.000
a discussion about the art
and science of solar lights.

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:40.330
We're thrilled to be able to
offer this program this evening

00:00:40.330 --> 00:00:43.930
in collaboration
with Arts at MIT.

00:00:43.930 --> 00:00:48.430
We're going to have a series
of paired discussions.

00:00:48.430 --> 00:00:53.590
Tonight, you'll get to hear from
artists, designers, architects,

00:00:53.590 --> 00:00:55.420
technologists, engineers.

00:00:55.420 --> 00:00:57.790
It's a pretty fantastic
program that we

00:00:57.790 --> 00:00:59.210
have for you this evening.

00:00:59.210 --> 00:01:00.400
I hope you'll enjoy it.

00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:02.200
And without further
ado, I'm going

00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:04.540
to allow Leila
Kinney, head of Arts

00:01:04.540 --> 00:01:09.130
at MIT to introduce the program.

00:01:09.130 --> 00:01:10.730
So am I on this or this?

00:01:10.730 --> 00:01:11.230
I'm on this.

00:01:11.230 --> 00:01:12.250
Great.

00:01:12.250 --> 00:01:13.820
So thank you all for coming.

00:01:13.820 --> 00:01:17.230
And I wanted to say a few
words about the first section

00:01:17.230 --> 00:01:18.230
of this.

00:01:18.230 --> 00:01:20.620
This is a format that
we're using tonight

00:01:20.620 --> 00:01:23.230
that I think the MIT
Museum has specialized in.

00:01:23.230 --> 00:01:26.230
Those of you who have come to
the Cambridge Science Festival

00:01:26.230 --> 00:01:29.590
know about this, Big
ideas for Busy People.

00:01:29.590 --> 00:01:33.292
So forgive are flying through
many interesting issues,

00:01:33.292 --> 00:01:34.750
but I think it's
an interesting way

00:01:34.750 --> 00:01:39.430
to make some of the research
and technology and innovation

00:01:39.430 --> 00:01:42.520
here at MIT sort of
accessible in a general

00:01:42.520 --> 00:01:44.710
and fairly efficient way.

00:01:44.710 --> 00:01:49.090
So as Brindha mentioned,
I'm the Head of Arts at MIT.

00:01:49.090 --> 00:01:52.510
And you may or may not know that
we actually have a major arts

00:01:52.510 --> 00:01:55.840
award here, the Eugene
McDermott Award in the Arts

00:01:55.840 --> 00:02:01.180
at MIT, which was created by
Margaret McDermott in honor

00:02:01.180 --> 00:02:03.700
of her husband Eugene who
was the founder of Texas

00:02:03.700 --> 00:02:04.480
Instruments.

00:02:04.480 --> 00:02:07.360
And they've been long time
benefactors and supporters

00:02:07.360 --> 00:02:08.889
of MIT.

00:02:08.889 --> 00:02:11.560
It really reflects her
vision and the vision

00:02:11.560 --> 00:02:15.520
of the 13th President at
MIT, that it's very important

00:02:15.520 --> 00:02:20.830
to integrate the arts into an
institution primarily known

00:02:20.830 --> 00:02:22.930
for science and engineering.

00:02:22.930 --> 00:02:25.480
And perhaps one of the
reasons that many of you

00:02:25.480 --> 00:02:26.472
are here this evening.

00:02:26.472 --> 00:02:27.430
Can I just get a sense?

00:02:27.430 --> 00:02:30.800
Has anybody heard of the
McDermott Award before?

00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:31.300
Great.

00:02:31.300 --> 00:02:32.110
Good.

00:02:32.110 --> 00:02:32.860
Thank you.

00:02:32.860 --> 00:02:37.690
And I think our 2014 recipient,
Olafur Eliasson, who was here,

00:02:37.690 --> 00:02:39.850
has recently become
familiar with it.

00:02:39.850 --> 00:02:43.780
A very important-- a very
important part of the award

00:02:43.780 --> 00:02:45.850
is the residency,
because we want

00:02:45.850 --> 00:02:49.300
an artist to sort of engage
with the MIT community

00:02:49.300 --> 00:02:52.030
in a way that is,
we hope, mutually

00:02:52.030 --> 00:02:54.290
enlightening and beneficial.

00:02:54.290 --> 00:02:58.010
So Olafur's practice-- and
may I ask another question?

00:02:58.010 --> 00:03:00.400
How many of you were able
to attend the public lecture

00:03:00.400 --> 00:03:02.200
last night in 10250?

00:03:02.200 --> 00:03:03.370
Oh good.

00:03:03.370 --> 00:03:03.870
Excellent.

00:03:03.870 --> 00:03:05.578
Well, for those of
you who weren't there,

00:03:05.578 --> 00:03:08.020
I'll just say a few
words about his work,

00:03:08.020 --> 00:03:10.670
not to take too long
on the introductions.

00:03:10.670 --> 00:03:13.780
Olafur's practice is
very wide ranging.

00:03:13.780 --> 00:03:17.230
And we could have
focused the residency

00:03:17.230 --> 00:03:19.040
on any number of things.

00:03:19.040 --> 00:03:23.230
He's well known for his
large scale installations,

00:03:23.230 --> 00:03:28.150
for temporary
pavilion's, architecture,

00:03:28.150 --> 00:03:33.160
experiments with sensory
perceptions based light.

00:03:33.160 --> 00:03:36.970
I think it's a shame that
artists become known only

00:03:36.970 --> 00:03:40.330
for a few works that are
mentioned over and over again.

00:03:40.330 --> 00:03:43.090
But it's really, I think,
impossible in his case,

00:03:43.090 --> 00:03:44.950
not to mention a couple.

00:03:44.950 --> 00:03:49.270
And one is a project from about
a decade ago called The Weather

00:03:49.270 --> 00:03:51.250
Project of the Tate Modern.

00:03:51.250 --> 00:03:53.890
Have people heard of
that by any chance?

00:03:53.890 --> 00:03:55.510
Some people nodding.

00:03:55.510 --> 00:03:57.320
Well, the Tate Modern,
as you may know,

00:03:57.320 --> 00:04:01.180
is a huge turbine factory
that's been converted

00:04:01.180 --> 00:04:03.340
into a contemporary art museum.

00:04:03.340 --> 00:04:07.330
And Olafur installed a
project there that, I think,

00:04:07.330 --> 00:04:11.680
took everyone and maybe
even him by surprise

00:04:11.680 --> 00:04:15.520
because of the intense
public engagement with it.

00:04:15.520 --> 00:04:22.120
He created an artificial sun
at the end of this turbine

00:04:22.120 --> 00:04:27.340
hall, a mirrored ceiling and
put some fog or mist into it.

00:04:27.340 --> 00:04:30.970
And the environment sort of
transformed people's experience

00:04:30.970 --> 00:04:33.970
of that space and
of one another.

00:04:33.970 --> 00:04:37.180
Over two million people ended
up attending the project.

00:04:37.180 --> 00:04:39.160
And it really became
a kind of marker

00:04:39.160 --> 00:04:42.100
for a kind of
participatory art that

00:04:42.100 --> 00:04:45.340
made one more aware
of one's experience

00:04:45.340 --> 00:04:50.800
in a space, which is a really
important aspect of his work.

00:04:50.800 --> 00:04:52.990
I think he'll talk a
little bit perhaps tonight

00:04:52.990 --> 00:04:56.620
about how we get from big
sun to little sun, which

00:04:56.620 --> 00:05:01.000
became the focus of the
residency here at MIT.

00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:02.860
You can see in
the window beyond,

00:05:02.860 --> 00:05:05.200
if you didn't notice
it when you came in,

00:05:05.200 --> 00:05:08.830
this portable solar
powered handheld lamp

00:05:08.830 --> 00:05:12.250
called Little Sun that
Olafur calls a work of art

00:05:12.250 --> 00:05:13.360
that works in life.

00:05:13.360 --> 00:05:16.070
I'll let him explain
more about that.

00:05:16.070 --> 00:05:20.630
But we decided to use that as a
means for engaging here with it

00:05:20.630 --> 00:05:24.520
the MIT community over the
course of this academic year.

00:05:24.520 --> 00:05:27.910
We started off by
having the Little

00:05:27.910 --> 00:05:31.000
Sun be a challenge in
a hacking arts event

00:05:31.000 --> 00:05:34.480
that occurred in the fall,
which put artists, entrepreneurs

00:05:34.480 --> 00:05:36.610
and engineers together.

00:05:36.610 --> 00:05:39.160
It was a particular
challenge and one

00:05:39.160 --> 00:05:40.840
of the members of his
studio came over--

00:05:40.840 --> 00:05:44.080
he's the CEO of Little Sun,
an architect himself came over

00:05:44.080 --> 00:05:48.280
to judge that competition
to mentor the students.

00:05:48.280 --> 00:05:50.810
Then energy night,
as you may know,

00:05:50.810 --> 00:05:53.470
is a big event annually
at the MIT Museum.

00:05:53.470 --> 00:05:55.870
It's probably one of the
most well attended events

00:05:55.870 --> 00:06:01.780
and certainly the most
populous club for MIT students.

00:06:01.780 --> 00:06:06.910
They organize a showcase of
new energy products each fall.

00:06:06.910 --> 00:06:10.220
And so in October, we
stalled this exhibition here

00:06:10.220 --> 00:06:12.470
and Frederik
Otteson, the engineer

00:06:12.470 --> 00:06:16.430
who is the co-founder
with Olafur of Little Sun

00:06:16.430 --> 00:06:18.240
came over for that event.

00:06:18.240 --> 00:06:20.030
And then this week,
we have sort of

00:06:20.030 --> 00:06:25.160
culminated this series of
interactions around Little Sun

00:06:25.160 --> 00:06:28.340
with a series of meetings
with people like [INAUDIBLE]..

00:06:28.340 --> 00:06:31.250
And this is how we
got Harald engaged

00:06:31.250 --> 00:06:35.330
and the J-PAL group
were economists

00:06:35.330 --> 00:06:39.860
to evaluate the effectiveness
of various technologies

00:06:39.860 --> 00:06:44.960
for emerging economies
through fairly rigorous policy

00:06:44.960 --> 00:06:52.640
and technical evaluations and
studies controlled trials.

00:06:52.640 --> 00:06:56.090
We've also engaged, of course,
with many other aspects

00:06:56.090 --> 00:06:59.360
of the MIT community,
the Civic Media Group.

00:06:59.360 --> 00:07:03.620
A group that's exploring open
and participatory documentary

00:07:03.620 --> 00:07:07.310
to think about how we
might bring together

00:07:07.310 --> 00:07:11.480
users, distributors
and creators,

00:07:11.480 --> 00:07:13.400
if you will of Little Sun.

00:07:13.400 --> 00:07:16.370
And of course, the programs in
art, culture, and technology

00:07:16.370 --> 00:07:17.940
and architecture.

00:07:17.940 --> 00:07:19.640
So in a way, this
is a culmination

00:07:19.640 --> 00:07:23.180
of that extended period of
an engagement with the MIT

00:07:23.180 --> 00:07:23.690
community.

00:07:23.690 --> 00:07:27.620
And I'm very grateful to Brindha
and Andrew and their team

00:07:27.620 --> 00:07:32.240
for allowing us to sort of
merge this with an opportunity

00:07:32.240 --> 00:07:35.000
to showcase some
of the innovation

00:07:35.000 --> 00:07:38.480
and new technologies
around solar power

00:07:38.480 --> 00:07:41.130
in particular here at MIT.

00:07:41.130 --> 00:07:47.060
So a brief couple of words
about Harald and then I'll

00:07:47.060 --> 00:07:51.110
hand it over to Harald
and then Olafur.

00:07:51.110 --> 00:07:53.540
Harald Quintus-Bosz
was-- well, there's

00:07:53.540 --> 00:07:55.640
a very interesting
little story behind this.

00:07:55.640 --> 00:08:00.680
When I was running around MIT
trying to sort of describe

00:08:00.680 --> 00:08:04.340
this project, the possibilities
for this resonance to people,

00:08:04.340 --> 00:08:07.260
I happened to be on the MIT
Museum advisory committee.

00:08:07.260 --> 00:08:10.790
So one of the places that
I presented this project

00:08:10.790 --> 00:08:13.250
was at the advisory committee.

00:08:13.250 --> 00:08:17.300
And Harald's partner and
co-founder of Cooper Perkins--

00:08:17.300 --> 00:08:20.120
which is a product
development company based here

00:08:20.120 --> 00:08:21.200
in Lexington--

00:08:21.200 --> 00:08:25.130
Gerhard said I know exactly
the person you need to talk to.

00:08:25.130 --> 00:08:30.710
Harald is not only himself
a graduate of MIT and also

00:08:30.710 --> 00:08:33.500
of Stanford-- we got him
first, they got him second--

00:08:33.500 --> 00:08:37.820
but had worked with
the D-Lab lab here.

00:08:37.820 --> 00:08:40.730
How many of you were familiar
with the D-Lab and its work?

00:08:40.730 --> 00:08:41.520
Great.

00:08:41.520 --> 00:08:45.070
It's a wonderful program
here at MIT that's--

00:08:45.070 --> 00:08:47.870
it's called dialogue,
design and dissemination

00:08:47.870 --> 00:08:50.750
for the developing world,
but D-Lab for short.

00:08:50.750 --> 00:08:54.560
And it works on creating sort
of effective and appropriate

00:08:54.560 --> 00:08:59.270
technologies for primarily-- is
it primarily sub-Saharan Africa

00:08:59.270 --> 00:09:00.545
or other countries as well?

00:09:00.545 --> 00:09:02.420
Harald will say a little
bit more about that.

00:09:02.420 --> 00:09:05.810
They have a wonderful
international design summit

00:09:05.810 --> 00:09:06.830
in Africa.

00:09:06.830 --> 00:09:10.520
And Harald is just not
only an instructor in D-Lab

00:09:10.520 --> 00:09:13.790
and the Chief Technical
Officer of Cooper Perkins,

00:09:13.790 --> 00:09:16.640
but has been really active
in that international design

00:09:16.640 --> 00:09:17.570
summit.

00:09:17.570 --> 00:09:20.660
He also, before
going to MIT and then

00:09:20.660 --> 00:09:24.440
to Stanford for his
graduate work, was at IDEO.

00:09:24.440 --> 00:09:26.840
So he has, I think, some
very interesting insights

00:09:26.840 --> 00:09:30.650
to share with us about
design thinking as a result.

00:09:30.650 --> 00:09:34.010
And Cooper Perkins
itself does all kinds

00:09:34.010 --> 00:09:36.270
of product development
technologies.

00:09:36.270 --> 00:09:40.100
Everything from, I think he
told me chilling technology

00:09:40.100 --> 00:09:45.620
for milk in India to increasing
the efficiency of more drilling

00:09:45.620 --> 00:09:46.460
equipment.

00:09:46.460 --> 00:09:48.450
I'm sure he'll give
you other examples.

00:09:48.450 --> 00:09:50.180
So without further
ado, let me introduce

00:09:50.180 --> 00:09:53.030
Harald, who will talk about
his solar light project.

00:09:53.030 --> 00:09:55.190
And the focus I might
say of this discussion--

00:09:55.190 --> 00:09:58.310
this segment is going to
be on design approaches

00:09:58.310 --> 00:10:00.590
to design for solar lighting.

00:10:00.590 --> 00:10:04.310
And the others will
have different foci.

00:10:04.310 --> 00:10:07.220
All right.

00:10:07.220 --> 00:10:08.330
Just a quick question.

00:10:08.330 --> 00:10:09.980
This the clicker for that?

00:10:13.160 --> 00:10:14.330
Oh, no problem.

00:10:14.330 --> 00:10:18.140
All right, so thank you, Leila,
for the great introduction.

00:10:18.140 --> 00:10:19.440
Said quite a bit.

00:10:19.440 --> 00:10:20.660
So, yes.

00:10:20.660 --> 00:10:22.100
I'm an instructor
at D-Lab, which

00:10:22.100 --> 00:10:23.720
is two floors up from here.

00:10:23.720 --> 00:10:25.580
Right up there.

00:10:25.580 --> 00:10:32.330
And not so long
ago in 2007, I was

00:10:32.330 --> 00:10:34.220
in the fourth floor
building three,

00:10:34.220 --> 00:10:40.550
which is that way, meeting
with Amy Smith and Ben Linder

00:10:40.550 --> 00:10:44.210
and Patricia and Laura
Stupin and others.

00:10:44.210 --> 00:10:46.490
And we are struggling
with the problem

00:10:46.490 --> 00:10:50.090
of a teaching tool for IDDS.

00:10:50.090 --> 00:10:52.560
And we were thinking of
this particular problem,

00:10:52.560 --> 00:10:54.440
which was lighting.

00:10:54.440 --> 00:10:58.070
We knew we wanted to target
the developing world.

00:10:58.070 --> 00:11:00.620
And we wanted to
think about addressing

00:11:00.620 --> 00:11:03.600
this particular problem, which
is lighting in the developing

00:11:03.600 --> 00:11:04.100
world.

00:11:04.100 --> 00:11:07.130
Which is important, because
people in the developing world

00:11:07.130 --> 00:11:08.780
are often lighting
with kerosene.

00:11:08.780 --> 00:11:12.410
And not only is it
expensive, but it can also

00:11:12.410 --> 00:11:15.120
be toxic to handle
the fumes, et cetera.

00:11:15.120 --> 00:11:17.990
So there was a big
opportunity for us

00:11:17.990 --> 00:11:22.460
to be able to fuse solar
lighting in a device that

00:11:22.460 --> 00:11:25.820
would help us teach
particular skills for IDDS.

00:11:25.820 --> 00:11:29.180
And the particular skills
we were looking to teach

00:11:29.180 --> 00:11:32.990
were primarily how
to use the lathe.

00:11:32.990 --> 00:11:35.240
We wanted we wanted to take
advantage of the lathe.

00:11:35.240 --> 00:11:37.130
It's a wonderful
piece of equipment,

00:11:37.130 --> 00:11:40.370
it does many operations.

00:11:40.370 --> 00:11:42.890
Hinges, all kinds of
things fit together so

00:11:42.890 --> 00:11:44.660
beautifully with a lathe.

00:11:44.660 --> 00:11:47.640
And it's great to-- it's
a great thing to use.

00:11:47.640 --> 00:11:50.190
But before I go
on with the lathe,

00:11:50.190 --> 00:11:52.910
let me talk a little
bit about IDDS

00:11:52.910 --> 00:11:54.890
because you're
probably wondering

00:11:54.890 --> 00:11:56.640
what it might be all about.

00:11:56.640 --> 00:12:00.740
So IDDS is a program
focused on teaching design,

00:12:00.740 --> 00:12:03.630
creating a network of innovators
in the developing world.

00:12:03.630 --> 00:12:06.620
So instead of teaching
students at universities,

00:12:06.620 --> 00:12:09.310
we're out there teaching
design to the participants

00:12:09.310 --> 00:12:10.310
of the developing world.

00:12:10.310 --> 00:12:12.870
And they're from
all over the place.

00:12:12.870 --> 00:12:16.340
So the idea is to take
a group like this--

00:12:16.340 --> 00:12:24.170
doctors, bike mechanics,
pastors across a broad range

00:12:24.170 --> 00:12:31.950
of countries, Pakistan, India,
many countries in Africa,

00:12:31.950 --> 00:12:36.320
South America, just everywhere--
and teach these people

00:12:36.320 --> 00:12:40.010
how to design and become
innovative to develop

00:12:40.010 --> 00:12:42.380
technologies that are
appropriate to them.

00:12:42.380 --> 00:12:44.900
And primarily, we do
that with exercises.

00:12:44.900 --> 00:12:50.380
In this case, it was
a launch a seed down

00:12:50.380 --> 00:12:52.610
the four floors
down in a building

00:12:52.610 --> 00:12:55.070
to hit a particular target.

00:12:55.070 --> 00:12:59.570
And this was over
in Ghana, Kumasi.

00:12:59.570 --> 00:13:02.180
We are also using charcoal.

00:13:02.180 --> 00:13:06.770
Amy Smith is particularly
adept at teaching

00:13:06.770 --> 00:13:11.900
the methods of making
charcoal, both the creation

00:13:11.900 --> 00:13:14.300
of the carbon itself
and then the manufacture

00:13:14.300 --> 00:13:16.100
of the briquettes.

00:13:16.100 --> 00:13:17.490
So we do that as well.

00:13:17.490 --> 00:13:20.240
And we take these people and we
teach them the design process

00:13:20.240 --> 00:13:23.330
so they end up being able
to do things like this.

00:13:23.330 --> 00:13:26.150
Follow a design
process that looks

00:13:26.150 --> 00:13:28.550
familiar to those that
studied engineering

00:13:28.550 --> 00:13:32.478
so that they can then develop
products that look like those.

00:13:32.478 --> 00:13:36.650
Those are like cassava peelers
and these are treadle pumps.

00:13:36.650 --> 00:13:38.420
So that they can design
these technologies

00:13:38.420 --> 00:13:40.790
that they need for themselves.

00:13:40.790 --> 00:13:43.370
Back to the lathe.

00:13:43.370 --> 00:13:46.340
So the lathe comes
in because we wanted

00:13:46.340 --> 00:13:50.340
to see if we can mix the lathe
and solar lighting in some way.

00:13:50.340 --> 00:13:54.230
So we did the
prerequisite sketches.

00:13:54.230 --> 00:13:58.062
Many, many sketches and
thinking bread boards of like,

00:13:58.062 --> 00:13:59.270
how might this come together?

00:13:59.270 --> 00:14:01.940
What forms might it take?

00:14:01.940 --> 00:14:05.840
It all of course had
to work with the lathe.

00:14:05.840 --> 00:14:10.770
And we came up with this,
which is right here.

00:14:10.770 --> 00:14:15.500
It's a solar light.

00:14:15.500 --> 00:14:18.680
And the idea here
was that people could

00:14:18.680 --> 00:14:21.080
machine the outside shell.

00:14:21.080 --> 00:14:25.160
They could sand the line
on this Plexiglas disk.

00:14:25.160 --> 00:14:26.840
So they would draw
a circle, and then

00:14:26.840 --> 00:14:28.880
use it use a sander
to sand to the line.

00:14:28.880 --> 00:14:32.180
They would use a hacksaw and
a form to bend the handle.

00:14:32.180 --> 00:14:36.860
They learned about soldering
both the LEDs and the wires.

00:14:36.860 --> 00:14:39.860
Rechargeable batteries,
all of that stuff

00:14:39.860 --> 00:14:43.070
was part of this
particular design.

00:14:43.070 --> 00:14:44.990
And what ended up
happening was, of course,

00:14:44.990 --> 00:14:47.930
that people like Habib--

00:14:47.930 --> 00:14:52.880
now he's, I think, in India--

00:14:52.880 --> 00:14:56.555
was shown here using the form.

00:14:56.555 --> 00:14:58.430
He was making the wire
frame around the form.

00:14:58.430 --> 00:15:00.950
There is other
participants that--

00:15:00.950 --> 00:15:03.950
I think that's
[INAUDIBLE] on the lathe.

00:15:06.620 --> 00:15:09.270
And the drill press over here
to make these holes as well.

00:15:09.270 --> 00:15:13.180
So we got everybody involved
in machining as well

00:15:13.180 --> 00:15:16.150
as soldering--
learning how to solder,

00:15:16.150 --> 00:15:18.760
which was the technology we
wanted to introduce because we

00:15:18.760 --> 00:15:21.302
knew that it was important for
people in the developing world

00:15:21.302 --> 00:15:27.010
to become aware of soldering
as a method of generating

00:15:27.010 --> 00:15:29.290
innovative ideas--

00:15:29.290 --> 00:15:32.890
using circuits and electronics.

00:15:32.890 --> 00:15:38.140
So then we, of course, used
this tool to teach design.

00:15:38.140 --> 00:15:41.620
And then went on to,
in our four-week event,

00:15:41.620 --> 00:15:46.060
to present our design challenges
and have the participants use

00:15:46.060 --> 00:15:48.520
the skills they
learned in making this

00:15:48.520 --> 00:15:53.020
to be able to design,
tackle challenges

00:15:53.020 --> 00:15:59.283
with water, energy, sanitation
and many other fields.

00:15:59.283 --> 00:16:00.700
So that's what
this light's about.

00:16:00.700 --> 00:16:06.130
And just to give
you an idea, when

00:16:06.130 --> 00:16:09.100
we got to the participants
on-- this by the way,

00:16:09.100 --> 00:16:10.570
happened all in one day.

00:16:10.570 --> 00:16:15.730
Because this is just a method
of teaching the participants how

00:16:15.730 --> 00:16:17.612
to use particular
tools, it was just

00:16:17.612 --> 00:16:18.820
the beginning of the session.

00:16:18.820 --> 00:16:22.420
We gave them a bag with a
circuit board, some wires,

00:16:22.420 --> 00:16:26.410
LEDs, some plastic plates
and a tube of aluminum.

00:16:26.410 --> 00:16:28.135
And I don't have
the handle wire,

00:16:28.135 --> 00:16:29.260
but we also gave them that.

00:16:29.260 --> 00:16:34.390
And from this, in about
two three-hour sessions--

00:16:34.390 --> 00:16:37.480
3 and 1/2 hour sessions,
they made this.

00:16:37.480 --> 00:16:39.340
Anyway, that's the solar light.

00:16:39.340 --> 00:16:41.258
Thanks for your attention.

00:16:41.258 --> 00:16:44.162
[APPLAUSE]

00:16:54.790 --> 00:16:57.700
So thank you.

00:17:08.030 --> 00:17:12.589
I started some two years
ago now in collaboration

00:17:12.589 --> 00:17:14.420
with an engineer
to think about how

00:17:14.420 --> 00:17:18.410
to bring solar energy
into a project which

00:17:18.410 --> 00:17:21.619
did several things
besides the main thing

00:17:21.619 --> 00:17:24.109
is obviously delivering
light in areas

00:17:24.109 --> 00:17:28.060
where there is no
access to energy.

00:17:28.060 --> 00:17:31.780
To jump straight into that,
what was very important to us

00:17:31.780 --> 00:17:36.580
was the language with which
we could achieve this.

00:17:36.580 --> 00:17:38.603
I had worked as an
artist for 20 years.

00:17:38.603 --> 00:17:41.020
And the engineer has actually
worked with solar airplanes.

00:17:41.020 --> 00:17:44.650
And him and his team was
the first people in '91

00:17:44.650 --> 00:17:47.150
to fly across America.

00:17:47.150 --> 00:17:48.550
So he knew a lot about--

00:17:48.550 --> 00:17:51.490
in a solar airplane-- in the
single-seated solar airplane.

00:17:51.490 --> 00:17:53.420
So he knew a lot.

00:17:53.420 --> 00:17:58.030
And what we looked at was
that there was already

00:17:58.030 --> 00:18:04.790
a number of products or projects
on a sort of out in the world.

00:18:04.790 --> 00:18:09.060
Some of which are really
quite amazing projects.

00:18:09.060 --> 00:18:11.530
But in general, what
we were interested in

00:18:11.530 --> 00:18:13.660
was to tell a
positive story or try

00:18:13.660 --> 00:18:16.942
to tell a story that was
not only aimed at the areas

00:18:16.942 --> 00:18:19.150
in the world in which one
could not have electricity,

00:18:19.150 --> 00:18:21.940
but really a kind of a
global story about sharing

00:18:21.940 --> 00:18:25.510
the responsibility about
having access to energy.

00:18:25.510 --> 00:18:28.270
So we worked a
lot on the design.

00:18:28.270 --> 00:18:30.165
And let me just
briefly show you that.

00:18:30.165 --> 00:18:31.540
You have seen it
maybe over here.

00:18:31.540 --> 00:18:34.540
This is how it looks.

00:18:34.540 --> 00:18:37.330
It has a solar panel,
obviously, on the back.

00:18:37.330 --> 00:18:40.630
And it has a LED and
rechargeable batteries

00:18:40.630 --> 00:18:41.530
on the inside.

00:18:41.530 --> 00:18:44.590
And then it has
this plastic casing.

00:18:44.590 --> 00:18:47.320
A UV resistant plastic.

00:18:47.320 --> 00:18:49.840
The point here is, we
had a benchmark which

00:18:49.840 --> 00:18:53.380
was a production price,
which is about $5.

00:18:53.380 --> 00:18:55.570
The production
price had to be low

00:18:55.570 --> 00:18:59.080
in order to create something
that would be sellable locally

00:18:59.080 --> 00:19:01.720
in areas where
people are normally

00:19:01.720 --> 00:19:03.070
spending money for kerosene.

00:19:03.070 --> 00:19:06.400
So we were competing with
the oil industry so to speak.

00:19:06.400 --> 00:19:08.865
This means $5 is
already quite expensive.

00:19:08.865 --> 00:19:10.240
And obviously, we
would very much

00:19:10.240 --> 00:19:12.250
like this to be even cheaper.

00:19:12.250 --> 00:19:14.800
But with a maximum
of $5, we went

00:19:14.800 --> 00:19:20.920
through a whole series of design
processes which led us to this.

00:19:20.920 --> 00:19:23.710
Still the ambition was to make
something which is beautiful,

00:19:23.710 --> 00:19:26.530
something that feels good,
something that a child who's

00:19:26.530 --> 00:19:27.580
our typical user--

00:19:27.580 --> 00:19:32.890
a child between 6 and 16 or
a young adult, if you want.

00:19:32.890 --> 00:19:37.090
And something that would be
easy to carry around with you.

00:19:37.090 --> 00:19:41.110
The sort of beginning
started with,

00:19:41.110 --> 00:19:43.120
how does energy actually feels?

00:19:43.120 --> 00:19:46.090
And the great
challenge we saw when

00:19:46.090 --> 00:19:47.720
talking to people--
in this case,

00:19:47.720 --> 00:19:51.550
we would study a little bit
south of [INAUDIBLE] close

00:19:51.550 --> 00:19:52.600
to [INAUDIBLE].

00:19:52.600 --> 00:19:56.170
We are starting the kind of
abstract of the confidence

00:19:56.170 --> 00:19:57.880
how to tell a story.

00:19:57.880 --> 00:20:01.810
It turned out it was much
easier to tell the story to be--

00:20:01.810 --> 00:20:03.602
it was much easier
if we were emotional

00:20:03.602 --> 00:20:04.810
rather than being functional.

00:20:04.810 --> 00:20:06.727
So instead of talking
about the practicalities

00:20:06.727 --> 00:20:10.190
for four hours of sun and one
evening of light, and so on,

00:20:10.190 --> 00:20:13.060
it became clear that
if people liked it,

00:20:13.060 --> 00:20:15.100
it was much easier to
convince them to take it.

00:20:15.100 --> 00:20:17.620
So this means the
emotional component

00:20:17.620 --> 00:20:19.870
was a much stronger
driver than, let's say,

00:20:19.870 --> 00:20:21.700
the functional component.

00:20:21.700 --> 00:20:23.330
And that inspired the design.

00:20:23.330 --> 00:20:25.218
This means that it
had to be something

00:20:25.218 --> 00:20:27.010
if it was laying around
and a child saw it,

00:20:27.010 --> 00:20:30.490
they would immediately
go and take it.

00:20:30.490 --> 00:20:32.890
We got some advice
from some engineers

00:20:32.890 --> 00:20:34.540
from different people.

00:20:34.540 --> 00:20:38.750
And their advice very
often was robust,

00:20:38.750 --> 00:20:40.300
but occasionally misleading.

00:20:40.300 --> 00:20:44.980
Such as an advice with saying,
just make sure it works.

00:20:44.980 --> 00:20:47.530
Make it high quality,
focus on the functionality

00:20:47.530 --> 00:20:49.040
and don't worry
about the design.

00:20:49.040 --> 00:20:50.480
This is Africa.

00:20:50.480 --> 00:20:52.570
It's much more
important than works.

00:20:52.570 --> 00:20:54.280
What do they care about design?

00:20:54.280 --> 00:20:57.550
Typical kind of NGO
type of standard.

00:21:00.140 --> 00:21:02.170
And obviously, we
very quickly realized

00:21:02.170 --> 00:21:04.480
this was not only wrong,
it was also condescending.

00:21:04.480 --> 00:21:07.030
And very typical for
the type of language

00:21:07.030 --> 00:21:10.510
that is somehow in this area
in which we are working where

00:21:10.510 --> 00:21:12.730
a certain part of the
world being resourceful

00:21:12.730 --> 00:21:15.430
is addressing a certain other
part of the world which is then

00:21:15.430 --> 00:21:17.890
resource weak or resourceless.

00:21:17.890 --> 00:21:21.460
And so kind of the idea of
to design, the language,

00:21:21.460 --> 00:21:25.000
the campaign, the project very
much also became about building

00:21:25.000 --> 00:21:27.340
a language which was
not stigmatizing.

00:21:27.340 --> 00:21:29.110
A language which
addressed something

00:21:29.110 --> 00:21:31.870
that we shared instead of
polarizing it into what

00:21:31.870 --> 00:21:32.660
do we not share.

00:21:32.660 --> 00:21:34.660
Say, we have, you don't have.

00:21:34.660 --> 00:21:38.740
So we always talk about what
desires do we have in common?

00:21:38.740 --> 00:21:40.787
And it's very basic
we want to be happy.

00:21:40.787 --> 00:21:42.370
We want to have
something in our lives

00:21:42.370 --> 00:21:43.780
that we like or
something we treasure.

00:21:43.780 --> 00:21:46.155
We want to care about each
other, and so on and so forth.

00:21:46.155 --> 00:21:48.460
And out of that, the
Little Sun project sort of

00:21:48.460 --> 00:21:50.860
grew its language.

00:21:50.860 --> 00:21:55.750
Should I be flipping
a few images?

00:21:55.750 --> 00:21:57.680
Yeah, maybe play the video.

00:21:57.680 --> 00:22:01.540
So we have done a number of kind
of campaign related projects.

00:22:01.540 --> 00:22:04.570
I'll talk a little bit
about the business model.

00:22:04.570 --> 00:22:07.180
But let me just show you one
sort of typical campaign.

00:22:07.180 --> 00:22:10.325
See this idea of the
aspiration to be--

00:22:10.325 --> 00:22:11.200
how should I say it--

00:22:11.200 --> 00:22:14.530
to be positive or to
address something.

00:22:14.530 --> 00:22:15.610
Does it play with sound?

00:22:19.394 --> 00:22:22.230
Anyway, so this is in
Denmark, the Royal Theater.

00:22:25.060 --> 00:22:29.157
I think it's a minute long,
so just have a look at it.

00:22:29.157 --> 00:22:32.650
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:23:27.000 --> 00:23:29.120
So as you can tell,
the idea is to suggest

00:23:29.120 --> 00:23:31.708
a degree of interdependence,
shared experience.

00:23:31.708 --> 00:23:33.500
This idea of experience
something together,

00:23:33.500 --> 00:23:34.310
being a collective.

00:23:34.310 --> 00:23:37.100
Experience the feeling that,
I went into this theater

00:23:37.100 --> 00:23:40.700
was before a show on a
different or a related matter,

00:23:40.700 --> 00:23:42.260
so I went in at the start.

00:23:42.260 --> 00:23:44.210
I told everybody,
OK, now like this.

00:23:44.210 --> 00:23:44.755
We filmed it.

00:23:44.755 --> 00:23:46.130
And then during
the show, we then

00:23:46.130 --> 00:23:48.532
edit it quickly together so
that at the end of the show,

00:23:48.532 --> 00:23:50.490
I came out again and
showed it to the audience.

00:23:50.490 --> 00:23:52.670
And then we sent it
to all of the audience

00:23:52.670 --> 00:23:54.590
and asked them to pass it on.

00:23:54.590 --> 00:23:57.470
So it became a kind
of an online project.

00:23:57.470 --> 00:24:00.530
And the theater was just
to get some sort of stuff

00:24:00.530 --> 00:24:02.972
that we could use
in social media.

00:24:02.972 --> 00:24:04.430
And this is a little
bit how we try

00:24:04.430 --> 00:24:06.440
to work with the
campaigning to address

00:24:06.440 --> 00:24:09.410
the idea of having
resources, being resourceful,

00:24:09.410 --> 00:24:10.460
doing something together.

00:24:10.460 --> 00:24:12.800
Communicating something
which is not necessarily

00:24:12.800 --> 00:24:16.795
focusing on the functionality
of it, and so on and so forth.

00:24:16.795 --> 00:24:19.170
Having said that, the principle
of this-- as you can see,

00:24:19.170 --> 00:24:22.400
you can buy it over here in
the shop-- is that we sell it,

00:24:22.400 --> 00:24:23.840
I believe, here it's around--

00:24:23.840 --> 00:24:26.210
what is it? $28 or something?

00:24:26.210 --> 00:24:28.520
And we are pushing it into
as many shops as we can.

00:24:28.520 --> 00:24:31.250
And obviously, this is turning
out to be quite a bit of work.

00:24:31.250 --> 00:24:33.060
But we are selling it
here at higher price.

00:24:33.060 --> 00:24:36.230
But we are then
delivering it gradually

00:24:36.230 --> 00:24:41.600
into sales outlets of various
sorts, private sector, NGO

00:24:41.600 --> 00:24:44.270
sector, governmental systems.

00:24:44.270 --> 00:24:47.720
And momentarily where we
only work in rural Africa

00:24:47.720 --> 00:24:49.170
in eight countries.

00:24:49.170 --> 00:24:51.290
So the profit from
selling it here

00:24:51.290 --> 00:24:53.600
goes into delivering the
light, but it is also

00:24:53.600 --> 00:24:56.060
based on a market
driven model locally.

00:24:56.060 --> 00:24:58.250
Meaning that the profit
generated locally then

00:24:58.250 --> 00:25:01.790
stays and, hopefully, pays
for ordering a new lamp.

00:25:01.790 --> 00:25:04.140
And this, of course, turns
out to be incredibly complex,

00:25:04.140 --> 00:25:09.500
but still it's working
relatively well.

00:25:09.500 --> 00:25:11.240
Currently we are--
I believe we are

00:25:11.240 --> 00:25:14.750
12 people working,
a few in Africa

00:25:14.750 --> 00:25:17.630
and a handful of people
doing the office in Berlin.

00:25:17.630 --> 00:25:21.060
And it's somehow-- we are sort
of breaking even right now.

00:25:21.060 --> 00:25:22.340
So we are quite happy.

00:25:22.340 --> 00:25:27.567
We made 250,000 lamps out
of which approximately 200

00:25:27.567 --> 00:25:28.400
have been delivered.

00:25:28.400 --> 00:25:30.830
One third of those have
been delivered off-grid.

00:25:30.830 --> 00:25:32.900
And looking at the
sort of curves,

00:25:32.900 --> 00:25:36.860
the off-grid is increasing
a much steeper curve

00:25:36.860 --> 00:25:39.590
than the on-grid.

00:25:39.590 --> 00:25:42.710
And we only just started
selling them in America online.

00:25:42.710 --> 00:25:46.070
And next week, the MoMA store--

00:25:46.070 --> 00:25:48.420
of course, the MIT
Museum store comes first,

00:25:48.420 --> 00:25:51.718
and then the MoMA store
in New York comes second.

00:25:51.718 --> 00:25:54.260
But it's a big deal because then
they send out their catalog.

00:25:54.260 --> 00:25:57.025
And it's on the cover and
the back of the MoMA store

00:25:57.025 --> 00:25:59.150
catalog-- the Spring Catalog,
I think they call it.

00:25:59.150 --> 00:26:00.108
An online and all that.

00:26:00.108 --> 00:26:02.090
So slowly, we are
getting it out.

00:26:02.090 --> 00:26:05.630
We are just about to also
make a phone chatter which

00:26:05.630 --> 00:26:07.010
is going into communication.

00:26:07.010 --> 00:26:09.600
We're trying to essentially--
it's just all about money--

00:26:09.600 --> 00:26:10.880
getting money.

00:26:10.880 --> 00:26:14.510
Sort of collecting money to get
a slightly bigger lamp going--

00:26:14.510 --> 00:26:15.980
like a system lamp
going with two

00:26:15.980 --> 00:26:18.750
plugs and a lamp and a
ceiling system, and so on.

00:26:18.750 --> 00:26:20.803
And this is further
down the line.

00:26:20.803 --> 00:26:22.970
So I told you a little bit
about the business model,

00:26:22.970 --> 00:26:25.430
about our sort of
campaigning idea.

00:26:25.430 --> 00:26:28.700
The idea of awareness, building
and policymaking and addressing

00:26:28.700 --> 00:26:30.560
the foot in the door
with solar energy.

00:26:30.560 --> 00:26:32.220
And then this role
of the design.

00:26:32.220 --> 00:26:35.300
So that was like Little
Sun in a nutshell, I hope.

00:26:35.300 --> 00:26:36.804
Thank you.

00:26:36.804 --> 00:26:40.290
[APPLAUSE]

00:26:40.290 --> 00:26:42.861
So now I think we come up here.

00:26:42.861 --> 00:26:46.325
These are a little
high, these stools.

00:26:46.325 --> 00:26:49.540
I feel like I'm at the
kitchen counter or something.

00:26:49.540 --> 00:26:52.860
So I think that one of the
most interesting things

00:26:52.860 --> 00:26:54.780
about this pairing
is that you're

00:26:54.780 --> 00:26:57.070
both very interested
in empowering people.

00:26:57.070 --> 00:26:58.920
But you're coming
at it with very--

00:26:58.920 --> 00:27:01.410
from very different directions.

00:27:01.410 --> 00:27:04.050
I guess we would call
it the DIY model.

00:27:04.050 --> 00:27:06.570
And then this sort
of larger sense.

00:27:06.570 --> 00:27:10.230
The significance of
having access to energy,

00:27:10.230 --> 00:27:11.250
feeling like you have--

00:27:11.250 --> 00:27:14.410
I heard you say a power
station in your hand.

00:27:14.410 --> 00:27:17.280
So I'm wondering if we
could expand a little bit

00:27:17.280 --> 00:27:20.190
and contrast and
also have you react

00:27:20.190 --> 00:27:22.920
to the others model
of approaching

00:27:22.920 --> 00:27:26.430
that issue of empowering people.

00:27:26.430 --> 00:27:28.140
All right.

00:27:28.140 --> 00:27:32.710
Yeah, I would say instead
of maybe DIY, maybe IIY.

00:27:32.710 --> 00:27:35.040
OK.

00:27:35.040 --> 00:27:37.080
Innovate it yourself.

00:27:37.080 --> 00:27:39.210
Because I think what
we're trying to do

00:27:39.210 --> 00:27:43.460
is we're trying to make
sure that people receive

00:27:43.460 --> 00:27:45.990
a bit of inspiration
and education

00:27:45.990 --> 00:27:49.850
in order to be able to
develop technologies

00:27:49.850 --> 00:27:51.600
that are appropriate
for their situations.

00:27:51.600 --> 00:27:54.540
And that's the way
we approach it.

00:27:54.540 --> 00:27:59.538
And we're across many
different fields geographically

00:27:59.538 --> 00:28:01.080
and many different
kinds of problems,

00:28:01.080 --> 00:28:03.390
as I alluded to before.

00:28:03.390 --> 00:28:07.470
So this particular solar,
again, was just a tool for us

00:28:07.470 --> 00:28:08.700
to inspire people.

00:28:08.700 --> 00:28:11.250
And it worked fabulously well.

00:28:11.250 --> 00:28:14.120
We had several
people really wanting

00:28:14.120 --> 00:28:16.560
to take off with the light and.

00:28:16.560 --> 00:28:18.358
I had to convince
them not to start

00:28:18.358 --> 00:28:20.650
focusing on telling that
because it's really expensive.

00:28:20.650 --> 00:28:22.740
I said, no, no, no.

00:28:22.740 --> 00:28:24.390
Take this inspiration
and rechannel it.

00:28:24.390 --> 00:28:27.090
Maybe design your own
light or do something else.

00:28:27.090 --> 00:28:29.790
But don't make
copies of this one.

00:28:29.790 --> 00:28:31.800
It's a teaching tool.

00:28:31.800 --> 00:28:34.500
And do you have actually
information on follow up

00:28:34.500 --> 00:28:38.280
or what people did with
their soldering and circuit

00:28:38.280 --> 00:28:41.460
making experience thereafter?

00:28:41.460 --> 00:28:43.020
So the one individual
that I know

00:28:43.020 --> 00:28:48.240
is [INAUDIBLE] He's selling
lights over in Ghana.

00:28:48.240 --> 00:28:49.740
And there are other
people that have

00:28:49.740 --> 00:28:51.370
done many different things.

00:28:51.370 --> 00:28:54.630
Not everybody then follows
through with light making,

00:28:54.630 --> 00:28:55.800
because it's not--

00:28:55.800 --> 00:28:59.020
the IDDS wasn't
about making lights.

00:28:59.020 --> 00:29:00.820
Again, the lights was a tool.

00:29:00.820 --> 00:29:04.470
So some people were working
on different technologies

00:29:04.470 --> 00:29:09.300
of generating power from water
or other medical challenges.

00:29:09.300 --> 00:29:12.105
Trying to solve AIDS
transmission from breast

00:29:12.105 --> 00:29:13.980
milk and those kinds of areas.

00:29:13.980 --> 00:29:16.755
And yes, some of those
people have continued.

00:29:16.755 --> 00:29:18.630
When I was putting the
presentation together,

00:29:18.630 --> 00:29:22.560
I was looking at the images
that have taken a long time--

00:29:22.560 --> 00:29:24.155
along the way of IDDS.

00:29:24.155 --> 00:29:25.530
And I was recognizing
the people.

00:29:25.530 --> 00:29:27.570
It's like oh, and they're
doing a water filter.

00:29:27.570 --> 00:29:30.055
And they're doing
the birthing kit.

00:29:30.055 --> 00:29:32.430
So there's a whole bunch of
innovators out there already.

00:29:32.430 --> 00:29:33.810
So it's working.

00:29:33.810 --> 00:29:35.730
But I don't have the
numbers in my head

00:29:35.730 --> 00:29:39.450
on who has done exactly what.

00:29:39.450 --> 00:29:41.650
So Olafur, what do you
think about this idea

00:29:41.650 --> 00:29:44.490
of giving people
the tools as opposed

00:29:44.490 --> 00:29:50.460
to giving them a sort
of emotional empowerment

00:29:50.460 --> 00:29:54.720
and a sense of connectivity, I
think, which is also important?

00:29:54.720 --> 00:29:59.160
Yeah, I think the challenge
is to do it in a way

00:29:59.160 --> 00:30:02.970
where you don't come across
as the teacher who is giving.

00:30:05.670 --> 00:30:08.820
I mean, this of course,
is a little bit complex.

00:30:08.820 --> 00:30:13.500
I think what I have learned
from traveling around

00:30:13.500 --> 00:30:17.520
in East Africa has been
very much that I'm always

00:30:17.520 --> 00:30:20.250
much easier with regards
to being integrated

00:30:20.250 --> 00:30:22.350
into a dialogue or working
together on something

00:30:22.350 --> 00:30:26.100
when I come to take instead
of when I come to give.

00:30:26.100 --> 00:30:30.180
This means if I'm there to
suggest that I know something

00:30:30.180 --> 00:30:32.790
they don't and then I
go, so let me teach you,

00:30:32.790 --> 00:30:34.920
it's just a little complex.

00:30:34.920 --> 00:30:36.300
And obviously, it's--

00:30:36.300 --> 00:30:38.580
I mean, coming from a
university like MIT,

00:30:38.580 --> 00:30:42.060
MIT is sort of difficult
to go and say that.

00:30:42.060 --> 00:30:46.650
So the way we work, we
really go and we base it--

00:30:46.650 --> 00:30:49.530
we base the principles
on that we are coming

00:30:49.530 --> 00:30:51.720
and we think there is an
opportunity for which we

00:30:51.720 --> 00:30:53.512
can learn something,
we can take something.

00:30:53.512 --> 00:30:54.630
And we almost--

00:30:54.630 --> 00:30:57.942
I wouldn't say we--

00:30:57.942 --> 00:31:00.430
it has worked better the
moments where we are--

00:31:00.430 --> 00:31:02.430
where we were very clear
to say, we are not here

00:31:02.430 --> 00:31:03.347
to teach you anything.

00:31:03.347 --> 00:31:05.620
You are great already,
very creative.

00:31:05.620 --> 00:31:06.900
We are coming to take.

00:31:06.900 --> 00:31:09.840
And this is why we
are asking people

00:31:09.840 --> 00:31:12.240
to make their own businesses,
develop their own systems

00:31:12.240 --> 00:31:12.740
and so on.

00:31:12.740 --> 00:31:15.540
And sometimes, I mean, it's
hard to generalize about it.

00:31:15.540 --> 00:31:20.310
But it's also because I work
as a teacher at the [INAUDIBLE]

00:31:20.310 --> 00:31:26.070
University and I've been
very interested in the need

00:31:26.070 --> 00:31:29.130
to be a little bit sensitive
to the colonizing language

00:31:29.130 --> 00:31:30.810
that one, without
even think about,

00:31:30.810 --> 00:31:35.910
it brings about into the sort
of teacher-student situations.

00:31:35.910 --> 00:31:38.940
And this is where I think the
campaigning of the language

00:31:38.940 --> 00:31:42.190
around Little Sun,
we have almost

00:31:42.190 --> 00:31:44.620
stopped also when
working in Africa to talk

00:31:44.620 --> 00:31:47.320
about off-grid versus on-grid.

00:31:47.320 --> 00:31:50.200
Or this idea instead of--

00:31:50.200 --> 00:31:53.380
we talk about the right to
power, the right to life.

00:31:53.380 --> 00:31:56.505
The right to light, it's
like having a right to life.

00:31:56.505 --> 00:31:57.880
And in that sense,
it's something

00:31:57.880 --> 00:31:59.620
which is both off and on grid.

00:31:59.620 --> 00:32:02.243
I mean, clearly, then
having an education

00:32:02.243 --> 00:32:03.910
is different than
having a no education.

00:32:03.910 --> 00:32:04.827
Somebody has to teach.

00:32:04.827 --> 00:32:06.790
So it's a little more
complex, I can see that.

00:32:06.790 --> 00:32:07.570
But I just think--

00:32:10.270 --> 00:32:11.980
I think a degree
of sensitivity is

00:32:11.980 --> 00:32:18.320
needed when moving into areas
and without being clumsy.

00:32:18.320 --> 00:32:21.290
So it strikes me that
one of the huge issues

00:32:21.290 --> 00:32:25.430
here is just the
size of this problem.

00:32:25.430 --> 00:32:28.580
You often used the
1.6 million people

00:32:28.580 --> 00:32:31.370
who don't have access
to electricity.

00:32:31.370 --> 00:32:33.590
Creating innovators
one at a time.

00:32:33.590 --> 00:32:36.210
It's hard to scale
these things up.

00:32:36.210 --> 00:32:37.640
So I'm just wondering
if you could

00:32:37.640 --> 00:32:41.720
speak a little bit to that
challenge of scaling up

00:32:41.720 --> 00:32:42.900
these solutions.

00:32:42.900 --> 00:32:46.070
How does your approach
or D-Lab's approach

00:32:46.070 --> 00:32:46.970
deal with that.

00:32:46.970 --> 00:32:50.690
Yeah, so that's actually
a very tough challenge

00:32:50.690 --> 00:32:51.950
because of the scale.

00:32:51.950 --> 00:32:53.690
Also because as we
were talking before,

00:32:53.690 --> 00:32:58.050
there isn't a copy and paste
answer to these problems.

00:32:58.050 --> 00:33:00.650
So you can't develop a
solution in Guatemala

00:33:00.650 --> 00:33:03.290
and expect to run over
to sub-Saharan Africa

00:33:03.290 --> 00:33:05.240
and have it take off.

00:33:05.240 --> 00:33:07.670
You need to be quite
knowledgeable about the space

00:33:07.670 --> 00:33:08.670
where you're going into.

00:33:08.670 --> 00:33:11.032
So, yeah, at D-Lab,
we're particularly

00:33:11.032 --> 00:33:12.740
focused-- one of the
classes I'm teaching

00:33:12.740 --> 00:33:14.990
is design for scale,
which is an effort

00:33:14.990 --> 00:33:18.920
to continue that educational
process for the students at MIT

00:33:18.920 --> 00:33:22.970
on how to design their
technologies for higher volume

00:33:22.970 --> 00:33:25.010
manufacturers so they
can be impactful.

00:33:25.010 --> 00:33:28.760
We also broaden in the
students the point of view

00:33:28.760 --> 00:33:33.930
what impact is so then it's
not head counting or dollar

00:33:33.930 --> 00:33:34.430
counting.

00:33:34.430 --> 00:33:36.830
But it can be broader
in terms of who benefits

00:33:36.830 --> 00:33:39.010
and how they benefit.

00:33:39.010 --> 00:33:41.190
So you can think about
scaling in different ways.

00:33:41.190 --> 00:33:42.710
So yes, it is a challenge.

00:33:42.710 --> 00:33:46.455
And we spend time instructing
students on how to do it.

00:33:46.455 --> 00:33:48.080
In the developing
world, it's different

00:33:48.080 --> 00:33:50.413
because the resources and the
education, everything else

00:33:50.413 --> 00:33:52.550
is slightly different.

00:33:52.550 --> 00:33:55.100
So there's more of an
organic growth model

00:33:55.100 --> 00:33:57.358
happening in the
developing world.

00:33:57.358 --> 00:33:58.400
And we've seen that, too.

00:33:58.400 --> 00:33:59.850
So yes, it's hard.

00:33:59.850 --> 00:34:02.190
And we do focus on it.

00:34:02.190 --> 00:34:05.240
So I think in your case,
Olafur, the scaling up is also

00:34:05.240 --> 00:34:06.620
a question of distribution.

00:34:06.620 --> 00:34:11.360
And that is a huge part
of the challenge, right?

00:34:11.360 --> 00:34:14.989
Again, beyond the
functional, the distribution.

00:34:14.989 --> 00:34:18.060
Yes, and it's a question of--

00:34:18.060 --> 00:34:21.110
we chose them to be
primarily market driven,

00:34:21.110 --> 00:34:22.969
even though a lot
of the market driven

00:34:22.969 --> 00:34:24.739
projects we're working
on, I-- in fact,

00:34:24.739 --> 00:34:28.219
NGO projects has a lot
of NGOs are leaning

00:34:28.219 --> 00:34:32.000
towards using market
systems for, let's say,

00:34:32.000 --> 00:34:34.820
resource optimization.

00:34:34.820 --> 00:34:37.820
The scalability I think--

00:34:37.820 --> 00:34:40.422
there was, I think,
what's this story?

00:34:40.422 --> 00:34:43.340
The story about the
condoms and HIV.

00:34:43.340 --> 00:34:45.113
They were trying
to push out condoms

00:34:45.113 --> 00:34:47.780
and it was really difficult. And
they went into the rural areas.

00:34:47.780 --> 00:34:49.699
And every time they
brought the condoms, well,

00:34:49.699 --> 00:34:51.199
somebody got there before them.

00:34:51.199 --> 00:34:53.150
And that was Coca-Cola.

00:34:53.150 --> 00:34:56.150
[INAUDIBLE] sort of
makeup and stuff.

00:34:56.150 --> 00:34:59.270
How come the makeup got
there before the condoms?

00:34:59.270 --> 00:35:02.060
And obviously, it has to do
with this idea of push and pull.

00:35:02.060 --> 00:35:04.810
And people who know about market
economy, which I don't, would

00:35:04.810 --> 00:35:08.150
know how to facilitate
the push versus the pull.

00:35:08.150 --> 00:35:12.830
So pull it like the desires
pulling the Coca-Cola

00:35:12.830 --> 00:35:16.130
from the company all the
way out into the areas

00:35:16.130 --> 00:35:18.050
where there is not even a road.

00:35:18.050 --> 00:35:21.212
But there's plenty of Coca-Cola.

00:35:21.212 --> 00:35:25.880
And so the idea is
that the desire locally

00:35:25.880 --> 00:35:27.610
seems to be an
incredibly strong force.

00:35:27.610 --> 00:35:30.110
And this is why then they change
the campaigning for condoms

00:35:30.110 --> 00:35:33.050
from being about
addressing a problem--

00:35:33.050 --> 00:35:36.450
the problem of getting
sick and HIV, and so on,

00:35:36.450 --> 00:35:38.450
to talk campaigning for--

00:35:38.450 --> 00:35:41.570
well, almost campaigning
for promiscuity.

00:35:41.570 --> 00:35:43.400
Kind of having fun and more sex.

00:35:43.400 --> 00:35:46.580
And then the campaign-- the
kind of a condom campaign

00:35:46.580 --> 00:35:49.460
became more successful
like, let's have more sex.

00:35:49.460 --> 00:35:50.420
It's great.

00:35:50.420 --> 00:35:53.780
And in that sense, we have
learned a lot, I think,

00:35:53.780 --> 00:35:57.260
to if we want to go down the
market driven principles.

00:35:57.260 --> 00:36:00.170
Because one could also
say, well, maybe thinking

00:36:00.170 --> 00:36:02.007
about every fifth
person in this room

00:36:02.007 --> 00:36:03.340
would not have access to energy.

00:36:03.340 --> 00:36:06.050
Just like all the people
over there and these.

00:36:06.050 --> 00:36:08.327
So you have energy,
you don't have energy.

00:36:08.327 --> 00:36:10.160
What about just say,
OK, let's just quit it.

00:36:10.160 --> 00:36:13.610
Because let's see, isn't it
so that the war in Afghanistan

00:36:13.610 --> 00:36:15.650
is like a billion dollar
a week or whatever.

00:36:15.650 --> 00:36:17.540
Billion dollar every two
weeks or something like this.

00:36:17.540 --> 00:36:18.040
Incredible.

00:36:18.040 --> 00:36:20.900
So that's $2 billion, then
everybody-- all these people

00:36:20.900 --> 00:36:22.620
have one of these.

00:36:22.620 --> 00:36:24.380
So maybe we should
instead of going--

00:36:24.380 --> 00:36:25.880
it's going to take
a long time, as I

00:36:25.880 --> 00:36:28.990
said, delivering on the lamp's
through the market economy.

00:36:28.990 --> 00:36:32.200
And we know market economy can
be pretty dodgy also, right?

00:36:32.200 --> 00:36:35.210
It's exploitive
[INAUDIBLE] because--

00:36:35.210 --> 00:36:36.380
so, anyway.

00:36:36.380 --> 00:36:38.240
So maybe there are other ways.

00:36:38.240 --> 00:36:40.700
But for the time being, we
don't have any other ways.

00:36:40.700 --> 00:36:43.610
And it does seem to--

00:36:43.610 --> 00:36:45.620
there is something
in it when we have

00:36:45.620 --> 00:36:52.190
a young group of 30 boys who are
former alumnae program by plan

00:36:52.190 --> 00:36:57.590
in Zimbabwe in a city,
which I don't remember now.

00:36:57.590 --> 00:37:01.040
And it's a door to
door sales system.

00:37:01.040 --> 00:37:03.320
These 30 boys,
they are up to me--

00:37:03.320 --> 00:37:06.920
they are making on an
average between $40 and $60

00:37:06.920 --> 00:37:08.810
a week each selling this.

00:37:08.810 --> 00:37:11.660
So this is by far, the
most successful business

00:37:11.660 --> 00:37:13.760
opportunity there have been.

00:37:13.760 --> 00:37:15.500
And the funny
thing is, they kind

00:37:15.500 --> 00:37:18.300
of importer the wholesale--
the person who imports,

00:37:18.300 --> 00:37:19.527
he makes a ton of money.

00:37:19.527 --> 00:37:21.360
And we go, OK, can't
you share a little bit?

00:37:21.360 --> 00:37:22.080
But, no, no.

00:37:22.080 --> 00:37:25.980
We realize we can't start
telling them how to--

00:37:25.980 --> 00:37:28.020
what to do and how to
do the business better.

00:37:28.020 --> 00:37:29.080
It was very aggressive.

00:37:29.080 --> 00:37:31.110
So the market system
proves efficient,

00:37:31.110 --> 00:37:36.120
but also a little bit unfair,
and so on and so forth.

00:37:36.120 --> 00:37:39.790
This has facilitated
a growing model.

00:37:39.790 --> 00:37:43.890
Now there's six other
groups, each with 30

00:37:43.890 --> 00:37:46.710
boys between 20 to 25, I think.

00:37:46.710 --> 00:37:48.300
Now they are testing
and taking on.

00:37:48.300 --> 00:37:49.540
They need a little loan.

00:37:49.540 --> 00:37:52.118
We seeds the first lamps,
half the production price.

00:37:52.118 --> 00:37:53.160
They send half the money.

00:37:53.160 --> 00:37:54.120
A little bit of money.

00:37:54.120 --> 00:37:57.720
And the road is
still bumpy but we

00:37:57.720 --> 00:37:59.520
are finding successful models.

00:37:59.520 --> 00:38:02.957
And unfortunately, we cannot
take that model from Zimbabwe

00:38:02.957 --> 00:38:04.290
and apply it on another country.

00:38:04.290 --> 00:38:06.360
We have to in a
different country totally

00:38:06.360 --> 00:38:10.290
different government, different
system, different tax system.

00:38:10.290 --> 00:38:13.230
And you would think that you
could take Zimbabwe and put it

00:38:13.230 --> 00:38:15.300
into Zambia.

00:38:15.300 --> 00:38:17.490
But you can't.

00:38:17.490 --> 00:38:20.700
We haven't been able
to so far, at least.

00:38:20.700 --> 00:38:22.620
So yes, the scalability--

00:38:22.620 --> 00:38:25.620
this group over there
is a really big--

00:38:25.620 --> 00:38:27.900
it's like a close to
impossible challenge

00:38:27.900 --> 00:38:29.640
within a short period.

00:38:29.640 --> 00:38:33.103
It would be easier to drop them
off with an airplane, which

00:38:33.103 --> 00:38:35.020
of course, would have a
lot of other problems.

00:38:35.020 --> 00:38:37.860
But basically,
coming to shut down

00:38:37.860 --> 00:38:42.760
the [? war ?] one week
before and proceed and so on.

00:38:42.760 --> 00:38:45.180
So I want to open the
floor up very soon to you.

00:38:45.180 --> 00:38:46.770
But I have one final question.

00:38:46.770 --> 00:38:49.710
I think we would be remiss
with the focus of this pairing

00:38:49.710 --> 00:38:51.840
without talking a
little bit about design.

00:38:51.840 --> 00:38:55.830
And I knew that I was--

00:38:55.830 --> 00:38:57.870
contrasting
approaches to design.

00:38:57.870 --> 00:38:59.970
And I know Harald
and I were just

00:38:59.970 --> 00:39:02.940
talking because he has this
background at Stanford where

00:39:02.940 --> 00:39:04.240
he got his--

00:39:04.240 --> 00:39:04.740
Masters.

00:39:04.740 --> 00:39:07.440
--master's degree in
mechanical engineering.

00:39:07.440 --> 00:39:09.300
He also was an IDEO before.

00:39:09.300 --> 00:39:11.040
I happened just
a little anecdote

00:39:11.040 --> 00:39:13.230
to take three MIT
students, three art

00:39:13.230 --> 00:39:15.600
scholars out to Stanford.

00:39:15.600 --> 00:39:19.470
We had a big national hackathon
in the D-School there.

00:39:19.470 --> 00:39:22.230
The MIT students were so
struck by how different

00:39:22.230 --> 00:39:23.670
the approach was.

00:39:23.670 --> 00:39:28.100
In Harald we had the MIT
team versus the IDEO approach

00:39:28.100 --> 00:39:30.350
and the Stanford approach,
which is kind of different.

00:39:30.350 --> 00:39:31.050
All mixed.

00:39:31.050 --> 00:39:32.640
Yeah, it's a little mixed.

00:39:32.640 --> 00:39:33.240
Right.

00:39:33.240 --> 00:39:36.300
And then Olafur,
as an artist, has

00:39:36.300 --> 00:39:40.110
thought so much about how
to activate participation

00:39:40.110 --> 00:39:41.320
in his works.

00:39:41.320 --> 00:39:44.970
And I know the experience of
individuality and collectivity

00:39:44.970 --> 00:39:48.340
at the same time is something
that his work in particular,

00:39:48.340 --> 00:39:51.310
I think, is really
masterful at creating.

00:39:51.310 --> 00:39:53.850
So I just want to see if I
can get you both to address

00:39:53.850 --> 00:39:56.110
this approach to design.

00:39:56.110 --> 00:39:56.610
Yeah.

00:39:56.610 --> 00:39:59.710
Regardless, maybe
even into the product.

00:39:59.710 --> 00:40:03.210
But maybe how it's also
affected how you've

00:40:03.210 --> 00:40:04.770
created these solar products.

00:40:04.770 --> 00:40:10.470
Yeah, so it is true that when
I went to MIT and Stanford

00:40:10.470 --> 00:40:13.320
the approaches to teaching
engineering and design

00:40:13.320 --> 00:40:15.120
were different.

00:40:15.120 --> 00:40:16.260
And I can appreciate that.

00:40:16.260 --> 00:40:20.790
So now, at Cooper Perkins,
I'm encouraging our group

00:40:20.790 --> 00:40:24.240
to think creatively by
teaching them skills--

00:40:24.240 --> 00:40:27.780
drawing skills on the right
hand side of the brain.

00:40:27.780 --> 00:40:31.470
And that is helping to think
more laterally rather than

00:40:31.470 --> 00:40:32.460
linearly.

00:40:32.460 --> 00:40:35.520
Education, especially at MIT and
other universities like that,

00:40:35.520 --> 00:40:37.540
tends to be a linear process.

00:40:37.540 --> 00:40:40.230
So you can be channeled into
a particular linear approach.

00:40:40.230 --> 00:40:42.250
And the broader thinking,
the brainstorming,

00:40:42.250 --> 00:40:43.770
all that process,
I think, is what

00:40:43.770 --> 00:40:46.890
generates the richness
of ideas in combination

00:40:46.890 --> 00:40:49.980
with getting a different
group of people.

00:40:49.980 --> 00:40:54.720
Not 20 people all the same,
but 20 people all different

00:40:54.720 --> 00:40:57.930
participating in a brainstorming
process and selecting ideas,

00:40:57.930 --> 00:41:02.580
I think, is what makes the
design process in that style

00:41:02.580 --> 00:41:03.730
much more powerful.

00:41:03.730 --> 00:41:05.300
We used that at IDDS.

00:41:05.300 --> 00:41:07.620
It's an important ingredient.

00:41:07.620 --> 00:41:09.510
We always, when we're
selecting participants,

00:41:09.510 --> 00:41:11.468
try to select participants
from different parts

00:41:11.468 --> 00:41:13.560
of the world but also
different occupations

00:41:13.560 --> 00:41:16.080
so we can put pastors
together with mechanics

00:41:16.080 --> 00:41:18.210
and get different
solutions to the problems.

00:41:18.210 --> 00:41:21.570
So that kind of lateral
thinking approach

00:41:21.570 --> 00:41:23.770
was very heavily
taught at Stanford.

00:41:23.770 --> 00:41:25.830
A little bit of
design over there,

00:41:25.830 --> 00:41:27.960
as well-- product design.

00:41:27.960 --> 00:41:31.290
And as I showed in the
images in the presentation,

00:41:31.290 --> 00:41:34.620
it was part of the process
for designing the lamp.

00:41:34.620 --> 00:41:36.960
Where we brainstormed and we
came up with configurations

00:41:36.960 --> 00:41:37.502
of the light.

00:41:37.502 --> 00:41:41.190
And we made little models
of how it might work.

00:41:41.190 --> 00:41:44.790
And then decided on the
final form that you see.

00:41:44.790 --> 00:41:48.840
And we actually show that
process to the participants

00:41:48.840 --> 00:41:51.270
so they can run that
process themselves.

00:41:51.270 --> 00:41:51.810
Great.

00:41:51.810 --> 00:41:52.590
Thanks.

00:41:52.590 --> 00:41:54.480
Olafur.

00:41:54.480 --> 00:42:01.200
Yeah, I think for
me, the design is--

00:42:01.200 --> 00:42:03.510
how should I say--
applying how onto why.

00:42:03.510 --> 00:42:06.570
So if I have sorted out what
I want to do as an idea,

00:42:06.570 --> 00:42:10.570
I need to sort of start applying
sort of language onto the idea

00:42:10.570 --> 00:42:13.740
so the idea takes
shape or gets a body,

00:42:13.740 --> 00:42:16.230
but sort of takes space.

00:42:16.230 --> 00:42:18.650
And design is obviously
more than just

00:42:18.650 --> 00:42:21.260
wrapping it with some
material and deciding.

00:42:21.260 --> 00:42:24.140
But essentially, if the
idea contains this idea

00:42:24.140 --> 00:42:28.110
that it has to touch somebody
in one way or the other,

00:42:28.110 --> 00:42:31.280
the design would need to
amplify that in a body.

00:42:31.280 --> 00:42:34.790
So the design you know
is, I believe, almost

00:42:34.790 --> 00:42:38.120
like an amplifier of the
intentionality of the idea.

00:42:38.120 --> 00:42:41.300
But having said that, the design
obviously is also more complex

00:42:41.300 --> 00:42:42.860
because it's a
part of an economy,

00:42:42.860 --> 00:42:44.522
it's a part of the
environment, it's

00:42:44.522 --> 00:42:46.730
a part of a lot of other
structures which is outside.

00:42:46.730 --> 00:42:48.890
The labor involved
with assembling this,

00:42:48.890 --> 00:42:51.980
is that labor done at the
source or can it be applied

00:42:51.980 --> 00:42:53.460
later on in the design process?

00:42:53.460 --> 00:42:56.040
Could one send the components
and then make a factory

00:42:56.040 --> 00:42:57.450
and then at the assembly?

00:42:57.450 --> 00:42:59.150
So the design is
also the economy

00:42:59.150 --> 00:43:02.160
of the production, both
the economy financially,

00:43:02.160 --> 00:43:04.220
but also the economy
of labor and time

00:43:04.220 --> 00:43:07.880
and how people devised this.

00:43:11.320 --> 00:43:16.130
So I think of design often like
the quality of the language.

00:43:16.130 --> 00:43:19.010
And the design's
purpose is to make sure

00:43:19.010 --> 00:43:21.170
that what was said
at the beginning

00:43:21.170 --> 00:43:23.190
actually comes out at the end.

00:43:23.190 --> 00:43:27.260
So the design doesn't sort
of deroot the why on the--

00:43:27.260 --> 00:43:30.570
sort of halfway through.

00:43:30.570 --> 00:43:32.570
I say this because
I think this also

00:43:32.570 --> 00:43:37.490
applies on how I work
when I work as an artist.

00:43:37.490 --> 00:43:41.240
And as an artist, I'm obviously
occasionally celebrating

00:43:41.240 --> 00:43:44.480
the idea of doing
no good or doing

00:43:44.480 --> 00:43:49.160
nonsense or unpredictable
successes, such as improvising

00:43:49.160 --> 00:43:50.417
and just--

00:43:50.417 --> 00:43:52.250
so I just want to keep
it a little bit open.

00:43:52.250 --> 00:43:55.640
The way doesn't have to be
a very sort of pointed why.

00:43:55.640 --> 00:43:57.680
It can also be,
OK, let's just not

00:43:57.680 --> 00:44:02.000
do something reasonable today.

00:44:02.000 --> 00:44:02.840
[INAUDIBLE]

00:44:02.840 --> 00:44:05.630
And sometimes there is a lovely
work of art right in there

00:44:05.630 --> 00:44:08.270
as well.

00:44:08.270 --> 00:44:10.530
If you don't mind me
adding that detail?

00:44:10.530 --> 00:44:11.030
No.

00:44:11.030 --> 00:44:12.090
I'm glad you did.

00:44:12.090 --> 00:44:15.300
So we want to take some
questions from you now.

00:44:15.300 --> 00:44:16.060
Please.

00:44:16.060 --> 00:44:20.660
Are you trying to see production
of these in the places

00:44:20.660 --> 00:44:23.320
where they will be used?

00:44:23.320 --> 00:44:24.070
Did you hear that?

00:44:24.070 --> 00:44:27.530
So whether I am trying to put
the production into the places

00:44:27.530 --> 00:44:32.870
where they use. , Yes we'd very
much like to produce them--

00:44:32.870 --> 00:44:35.570
I mean, right now, the solar
panels from California.

00:44:35.570 --> 00:44:37.070
The LED is German.

00:44:37.070 --> 00:44:38.720
The plastic is also German.

00:44:38.720 --> 00:44:41.300
The inside is from Taiwan.

00:44:41.300 --> 00:44:44.580
The batteries are Chinese,
and so on and so forth.

00:44:44.580 --> 00:44:47.130
But essentially, the
first step, which

00:44:47.130 --> 00:44:48.590
we have been looking
into, would be

00:44:48.590 --> 00:44:52.820
to do an assembly factory
or sort of assembly place

00:44:52.820 --> 00:44:54.920
so we can sort of
ship the components.

00:44:54.920 --> 00:45:00.890
And for our next scale,
it would take 15 people

00:45:00.890 --> 00:45:04.700
on a sort of assembly type
sort of one space factory.

00:45:04.700 --> 00:45:06.410
Very basic.

00:45:06.410 --> 00:45:10.160
The challenge here is
that it's not actually

00:45:10.160 --> 00:45:11.660
the salary in the
economy and so on.

00:45:11.660 --> 00:45:13.827
It would probably make it
even a little bit cheaper,

00:45:13.827 --> 00:45:15.300
which is the upside.

00:45:15.300 --> 00:45:19.760
The downside is that shipping
green environmentally

00:45:19.760 --> 00:45:22.280
friendly certified
product very often

00:45:22.280 --> 00:45:24.590
has some tax benefits in it.

00:45:24.590 --> 00:45:28.010
This means we can send this
into Nigeria, for instance,

00:45:28.010 --> 00:45:31.580
tax free because it goes in
as a sort of green World Bank

00:45:31.580 --> 00:45:33.380
certified green
Lighting Africa program,

00:45:33.380 --> 00:45:34.340
and so on and so forth.

00:45:34.340 --> 00:45:36.350
If we send it as
electronic components,

00:45:36.350 --> 00:45:39.740
we have to pay 45% tax on some
of it 25% on some other, 10%

00:45:39.740 --> 00:45:40.426
on other.

00:45:40.426 --> 00:45:44.023
And this means that that
actually takes the upside away.

00:45:44.023 --> 00:45:45.440
It's a very puzzling
how this sort

00:45:45.440 --> 00:45:49.310
of environmentally industry
being the good industry

00:45:49.310 --> 00:45:51.610
is also having--

00:45:51.610 --> 00:45:53.420
but once you hit
a certain scale,

00:45:53.420 --> 00:45:55.170
that then turns over again.

00:45:55.170 --> 00:45:58.520
And so it's just
a question of time

00:45:58.520 --> 00:46:00.500
before it actually
pays off again.

00:46:03.680 --> 00:46:04.850
There's a microphone here.

00:46:04.850 --> 00:46:06.050
Do we have other questions?

00:46:06.050 --> 00:46:07.633
There's one on the
other side as well.

00:46:11.480 --> 00:46:11.980
One more.

00:46:11.980 --> 00:46:12.480
Yeah.

00:46:17.950 --> 00:46:20.700
I haven't had a chance to
look at this very closely,

00:46:20.700 --> 00:46:25.235
but what I see here, it looks
like the case is actually

00:46:25.235 --> 00:46:28.010
a fairly complex shape.

00:46:28.010 --> 00:46:30.440
[INAUDIBLE] plastic
outside case.

00:46:30.440 --> 00:46:35.180
And I'm wondering,
what challenges

00:46:35.180 --> 00:46:39.320
there were to design
that and keeping

00:46:39.320 --> 00:46:42.650
with the [INAUDIBLE] possible?

00:46:42.650 --> 00:46:50.103
And whether that's an area where
the injection molding process

00:46:50.103 --> 00:46:52.320
[INAUDIBLE]

00:46:52.320 --> 00:46:54.026
Yeah.

00:46:54.026 --> 00:46:55.610
It sort of looks complex.

00:46:55.610 --> 00:46:58.920
But it's actually-- it's a
two-part injection mold system.

00:46:58.920 --> 00:47:02.750
So there's a lid and a base.

00:47:02.750 --> 00:47:05.660
And then of course,
I made some effort in

00:47:05.660 --> 00:47:08.510
to make it a wearable.

00:47:08.510 --> 00:47:11.230
Because it does represent
a certain value.

00:47:11.230 --> 00:47:13.760
So it's not something you're
likely-- during the charging

00:47:13.760 --> 00:47:15.590
in the day, you're not
likely to put it out

00:47:15.590 --> 00:47:19.400
if it represents what you would
spend in two months on kerosene

00:47:19.400 --> 00:47:22.460
for lighting for a family.

00:47:22.460 --> 00:47:24.650
You don't see very often
that you sort of lay it

00:47:24.650 --> 00:47:27.890
in the garden and then go
to work and come back and--

00:47:27.890 --> 00:47:32.300
so that was one of the
sort of user modes.

00:47:32.300 --> 00:47:36.810
The thing is that it actually
has a ventilated system inside.

00:47:36.810 --> 00:47:41.000
So some work went into making
sure that when it's in the sun,

00:47:41.000 --> 00:47:43.680
it heats up immensely,
especially the black part here.

00:47:43.680 --> 00:47:45.982
And this means it has to
take-- it has an air intake.

00:47:45.982 --> 00:47:46.940
You can kind of see it.

00:47:46.940 --> 00:47:47.910
And an air outtake.

00:47:47.910 --> 00:47:50.078
And the flow sort of
wraps around the batteries

00:47:50.078 --> 00:47:50.870
and goes out again.

00:47:50.870 --> 00:47:53.480
We engineer that quite a
while to keep the battery

00:47:53.480 --> 00:47:54.260
from overheating.

00:47:54.260 --> 00:47:56.630
Because they don't like--

00:47:56.630 --> 00:47:59.510
the batteries are not the
worst batteries you can get,

00:47:59.510 --> 00:48:01.010
but these are not
the best batteries

00:48:01.010 --> 00:48:03.640
also because they had to be
in a reasonable price range.

00:48:07.200 --> 00:48:09.080
The LEDs can't [INAUDIBLE]?

00:48:09.080 --> 00:48:09.680
No.

00:48:09.680 --> 00:48:12.740
But I mean, we don't
really get up in the area

00:48:12.740 --> 00:48:14.990
where the LED-- it's just
because the batteries have--

00:48:14.990 --> 00:48:21.680
they have 900 or about
900 recharges to full.

00:48:21.680 --> 00:48:24.140
And we would want
to-- heating up

00:48:24.140 --> 00:48:27.240
900 times is also quite
a challenge for them.

00:48:27.240 --> 00:48:31.160
So we worked a lot on
this little cooling sort

00:48:31.160 --> 00:48:33.560
of from Arabic architecture
cooling principle

00:48:33.560 --> 00:48:39.890
that the rise of hot air
draws in cooler air and so on.

00:48:39.890 --> 00:48:42.283
It only took 3% of--

00:48:42.283 --> 00:48:42.950
we did the test.

00:48:42.950 --> 00:48:47.630
It only took the heat down 3%,
but just enough for kind of--

00:48:47.630 --> 00:48:51.138
at the top of the heat,
that's quite a bit.

00:48:51.138 --> 00:48:53.460
[INAUDIBLE]

00:48:53.460 --> 00:48:55.140
Well, typically,
when charging, yes.

00:48:55.140 --> 00:48:59.850
I mean, we encourage on the--

00:48:59.850 --> 00:49:01.140
if we have any input.

00:49:01.140 --> 00:49:05.130
We very often actually charges
sort of standing or leaning.

00:49:05.130 --> 00:49:07.920
And this means that there
is a bigger likelihood

00:49:07.920 --> 00:49:09.540
that it actually has an uptake.

00:49:09.540 --> 00:49:11.190
This is also not so bad.

00:49:11.190 --> 00:49:13.380
It should also heat like this.

00:49:13.380 --> 00:49:14.070
Yeah.

00:49:14.070 --> 00:49:18.200
I would add that
the biggest price--

00:49:18.200 --> 00:49:21.660
the costly parts of the
light are the internals,

00:49:21.660 --> 00:49:25.440
the batteries, the solar cell,
the LED and the circuit board.

00:49:25.440 --> 00:49:28.230
And the plastics
are a smaller part.

00:49:28.230 --> 00:49:31.950
So you can focus your design
effort on getting the features,

00:49:31.950 --> 00:49:34.320
making that product
of high quality.

00:49:34.320 --> 00:49:38.040
And it's not that big a penalty
relative to everything else.

00:49:38.040 --> 00:49:41.850
So you wouldn't take this
and cut off all of its wings

00:49:41.850 --> 00:49:44.700
and come up with a
significantly cheaper product.

00:49:44.700 --> 00:49:47.310
It's a good design.

00:49:47.310 --> 00:49:48.480
I think we're done.

00:49:48.480 --> 00:49:50.227
So onto the next panel.

00:49:50.227 --> 00:49:51.060
Thank you very much.

00:49:51.060 --> 00:49:51.978
Thank you.

00:49:51.978 --> 00:49:56.958
[APPLAUSE]

00:51:07.700 --> 00:51:09.460
Good evening, everybody.

00:51:09.460 --> 00:51:12.140
We are going to start the
second discussion now.

00:51:12.140 --> 00:51:14.920
And now we are going
to take a step back.

00:51:14.920 --> 00:51:17.620
Because apart from design
challenges and delivery

00:51:17.620 --> 00:51:20.290
challenges, there
are other challenges

00:51:20.290 --> 00:51:23.510
behind this technology, which
is the technology itself.

00:51:23.510 --> 00:51:25.780
So you have to
design them first.

00:51:25.780 --> 00:51:28.370
And there is a lot of
science behind that.

00:51:28.370 --> 00:51:30.400
So in the next 40
minutes or so, you'll

00:51:30.400 --> 00:51:33.700
probably learn more about
the science of solar cells

00:51:33.700 --> 00:51:35.950
than you ever wanted to know.

00:51:35.950 --> 00:51:38.371
But we'll try to
make it interesting.

00:51:40.960 --> 00:51:45.220
And when I try to
describe the Little Sun

00:51:45.220 --> 00:51:47.770
project to my daughter
who is only five,

00:51:47.770 --> 00:51:49.600
her first reaction
was, yeah, I know

00:51:49.600 --> 00:51:50.800
what you are talking about.

00:51:50.800 --> 00:51:52.780
It's like [INAUDIBLE].

00:51:52.780 --> 00:51:53.860
First, I laughed.

00:51:53.860 --> 00:51:56.570
And then I started thinking
a little bit more about it.

00:51:56.570 --> 00:51:58.480
And I thought, that's
a perfect test system

00:51:58.480 --> 00:52:02.530
to actually look at different
aspects of any technology.

00:52:02.530 --> 00:52:04.220
Now this technology is magic.

00:52:04.220 --> 00:52:08.230
We don't really know what kind
of innovation went into it.

00:52:08.230 --> 00:52:10.000
But there are
aspects of it which

00:52:10.000 --> 00:52:13.300
can be applicable to
any kind of technology.

00:52:13.300 --> 00:52:14.660
Now just look at them.

00:52:14.660 --> 00:52:17.110
So first one is functionality.

00:52:17.110 --> 00:52:20.590
Obviously, you want the
device to work as intended.

00:52:20.590 --> 00:52:22.180
Like in this
particular case, it's

00:52:22.180 --> 00:52:25.570
supposed to give you a
light when you need it.

00:52:25.570 --> 00:52:27.400
Another aspect is stability.

00:52:27.400 --> 00:52:30.400
So you want it to
work in the cave,

00:52:30.400 --> 00:52:33.470
outside, when it's raining,
when it's not raining,

00:52:33.470 --> 00:52:35.440
when it's cold a hot.

00:52:35.440 --> 00:52:37.660
So that's stability
of [INAUDIBLE]..

00:52:37.660 --> 00:52:40.360
Portability,
obviously, it's nice

00:52:40.360 --> 00:52:42.790
if you can put it in
your pocket and take it

00:52:42.790 --> 00:52:45.070
with you where you need it.

00:52:45.070 --> 00:52:49.900
Efficiency, it's how much energy
you can get out of your device

00:52:49.900 --> 00:52:53.290
and how much useless
energy you get.

00:52:53.290 --> 00:52:54.700
Like in this
particular case, you

00:52:54.700 --> 00:52:57.580
get light, which is
what Frodo needs.

00:52:57.580 --> 00:53:00.430
And it doesn't seem that
this device gets really hot

00:53:00.430 --> 00:53:02.770
because that would be
unwanted energy, which

00:53:02.770 --> 00:53:04.900
would drive efficiency down.

00:53:04.900 --> 00:53:07.030
And also it doesn't
have to be beautiful,

00:53:07.030 --> 00:53:09.430
but it doesn't hurt
if it's beautiful.

00:53:09.430 --> 00:53:12.520
It's all this nice to use
something that looks nice.

00:53:12.520 --> 00:53:14.710
So now, let's look
at our technology,

00:53:14.710 --> 00:53:17.170
which is photovoltaics.

00:53:17.170 --> 00:53:21.460
So the first aspect
is functionality.

00:53:21.460 --> 00:53:22.760
So it's supposed to work.

00:53:22.760 --> 00:53:24.100
So what does it do?

00:53:24.100 --> 00:53:28.040
It converts solar
energy into electricity,

00:53:28.040 --> 00:53:31.210
which in turn can be
used to create light

00:53:31.210 --> 00:53:34.750
or to make work our electronic
devices which we love so much

00:53:34.750 --> 00:53:37.000
and depend so much upon.

00:53:37.000 --> 00:53:40.090
So the functionality is
based on sound science.

00:53:40.090 --> 00:53:41.757
So how does it work?

00:53:41.757 --> 00:53:43.090
That's where it gets scientific.

00:53:43.090 --> 00:53:45.170
But don't turn away please.

00:53:45.170 --> 00:53:49.000
So although, we usually
talk about light as waves,

00:53:49.000 --> 00:53:51.490
actually when a light
interacts with matter--

00:53:51.490 --> 00:53:53.230
in this case, it's solar cell--

00:53:53.230 --> 00:53:55.090
it reveals its quantum nature.

00:53:55.090 --> 00:53:57.490
And what it means,
it actually consists

00:53:57.490 --> 00:54:01.060
of a lot of little
particles called photons.

00:54:01.060 --> 00:54:02.870
And when those
particles interact

00:54:02.870 --> 00:54:05.500
with the material
of the solar cell,

00:54:05.500 --> 00:54:09.280
they set free and another type
of particle, which is electron.

00:54:09.280 --> 00:54:11.840
So it was sitting quietly
and it's energetically

00:54:11.840 --> 00:54:16.180
preferred place in the solar
cell, but now it breaks free

00:54:16.180 --> 00:54:20.020
and it can move and it
leaves a hole behind.

00:54:20.020 --> 00:54:23.380
And then if we can make
those particles move,

00:54:23.380 --> 00:54:26.050
we can collect them into
the electrical circuit

00:54:26.050 --> 00:54:27.830
and they will make work.

00:54:27.830 --> 00:54:31.960
And work is needed to
make this device work.

00:54:31.960 --> 00:54:34.600
OK, so that's how it works.

00:54:34.600 --> 00:54:37.300
It obviously, works when
the sun is up and there

00:54:37.300 --> 00:54:41.950
is a lot of light in the
summer on a nice cloudless day.

00:54:41.950 --> 00:54:43.690
But we also would
like this to work

00:54:43.690 --> 00:54:46.270
when the sun is going down,
because you still need

00:54:46.270 --> 00:54:48.010
your cell phone then, right?

00:54:48.010 --> 00:54:50.350
And also you would like
it to work when it rains

00:54:50.350 --> 00:54:56.110
or when it's windy or in any
kind of ambient condition.

00:54:56.110 --> 00:54:58.330
In that aspect is portability.

00:54:58.330 --> 00:55:01.120
It's also nice if that
is kind of a device

00:55:01.120 --> 00:55:03.520
that you can actually
put in your pocket

00:55:03.520 --> 00:55:06.820
and take it places where
there is no [INAUDIBLE]..

00:55:06.820 --> 00:55:10.420
And as we've heard in the
previous conversation,

00:55:10.420 --> 00:55:13.600
that's always that
possibility goes hand in hand

00:55:13.600 --> 00:55:14.980
with aesthetics.

00:55:14.980 --> 00:55:17.950
Because if you have a huge power
plug somewhere in a desert,

00:55:17.950 --> 00:55:20.230
probably people don't really
care how it looks like.

00:55:20.230 --> 00:55:21.998
Nobody's going to see it.

00:55:21.998 --> 00:55:24.040
But if it's something you
want people to actually

00:55:24.040 --> 00:55:27.460
take with them around, you
better make it look nice.

00:55:27.460 --> 00:55:30.720
So that's important.

00:55:30.720 --> 00:55:32.950
And then we go to
portability also

00:55:32.950 --> 00:55:36.370
goes with efficiency, because
the requirements to efficiency

00:55:36.370 --> 00:55:38.200
are now more strict.

00:55:38.200 --> 00:55:41.980
Because if it's small, you
also want it to be light.

00:55:41.980 --> 00:55:45.010
If it's light, that means you
cannot use too much material.

00:55:45.010 --> 00:55:46.120
So that's a challenge.

00:55:46.120 --> 00:55:49.120
But still, you want to squeeze
as much energy out of it

00:55:49.120 --> 00:55:50.600
as possible.

00:55:50.600 --> 00:55:53.620
So it's requirements to
efficiency become even stricter

00:55:53.620 --> 00:55:56.050
if you are trying to
make something portable.

00:55:56.050 --> 00:55:58.810
OK, so what does it
problems with efficiency.

00:55:58.810 --> 00:56:01.660
It seems there is so much
sunlight, if it just collect

00:56:01.660 --> 00:56:04.430
it and converted into
electrical current,

00:56:04.430 --> 00:56:06.180
it shouldn't be a problem.

00:56:06.180 --> 00:56:10.460
Well, the problem is not only
light is made out of particles.

00:56:10.460 --> 00:56:13.760
It's actually made of
different kinds of particles.

00:56:13.760 --> 00:56:16.370
As you know, after rain,
when there is a rainbow,

00:56:16.370 --> 00:56:18.680
there are different
colors in light.

00:56:18.680 --> 00:56:20.690
And those different
colors are actually

00:56:20.690 --> 00:56:22.640
different types of particles--

00:56:22.640 --> 00:56:23.570
those photons.

00:56:23.570 --> 00:56:26.060
And what is different
is they carry

00:56:26.060 --> 00:56:28.700
a different amount of energy.

00:56:28.700 --> 00:56:30.380
But there is only
a certain amount

00:56:30.380 --> 00:56:32.870
of energy-- quantum
of energy which is

00:56:32.870 --> 00:56:35.660
needed to set free an electron.

00:56:35.660 --> 00:56:39.110
And every photon can only
set free one electron.

00:56:39.110 --> 00:56:42.740
And so then, if you just
look at how much energy

00:56:42.740 --> 00:56:45.500
different photons have
different color [INAUDIBLE],,

00:56:45.500 --> 00:56:48.140
you see that some of them
actually don't have enough.

00:56:48.140 --> 00:56:49.520
So they are totally useless.

00:56:49.520 --> 00:56:51.980
They cannot do work for us.

00:56:51.980 --> 00:56:54.050
Some of them have too much.

00:56:54.050 --> 00:56:56.240
So they do the
work, but they have

00:56:56.240 --> 00:57:00.410
excess of energy, which just
ends up heating our solar cell.

00:57:00.410 --> 00:57:01.850
And again, it's lost.

00:57:01.850 --> 00:57:04.980
So the efficiency is going down.

00:57:04.980 --> 00:57:06.240
So that's a challenge.

00:57:06.240 --> 00:57:09.220
So what are you
going to do with it?

00:57:09.220 --> 00:57:13.050
So it calls for some
material innovations.

00:57:13.050 --> 00:57:15.920
So what kind of innovations
you can think of?

00:57:15.920 --> 00:57:20.390
So the obvious one is you want
to change your solar cell.

00:57:20.390 --> 00:57:22.730
So if you can come up
with solar cell made

00:57:22.730 --> 00:57:25.940
of some kind of material
or different materials

00:57:25.940 --> 00:57:29.090
or an artificial material
that actually makes

00:57:29.090 --> 00:57:32.630
use of all the energy of all
the photons very efficiently,

00:57:32.630 --> 00:57:35.030
that would be one solution.

00:57:35.030 --> 00:57:39.200
And then another solution
which may sound crazy, but you

00:57:39.200 --> 00:57:41.780
can actually change the sun.

00:57:41.780 --> 00:57:44.010
Because this sun we have--

00:57:44.010 --> 00:57:45.720
that's the only the
one we have, right?

00:57:45.720 --> 00:57:47.130
We don't have a choice.

00:57:47.130 --> 00:57:49.610
But it generates
all those photons

00:57:49.610 --> 00:57:52.910
of different energy, which
seem not to work nicely

00:57:52.910 --> 00:57:56.120
with our technology,
which is a PV cell.

00:57:56.120 --> 00:57:57.180
So we don't like that.

00:57:57.180 --> 00:57:58.920
So what can we do about it?

00:57:58.920 --> 00:58:00.620
How can we change sun?

00:58:00.620 --> 00:58:03.830
Well, we just need to remember
that heat is actually light

00:58:03.830 --> 00:58:05.660
and light is heat.

00:58:05.660 --> 00:58:08.465
So we saw that those
unwanted photons,

00:58:08.465 --> 00:58:11.420
they generate a lot of
heat, which was a problem.

00:58:11.420 --> 00:58:14.000
But we can actually turn
it to our advantage.

00:58:14.000 --> 00:58:16.310
Because if you
think of it, our sun

00:58:16.310 --> 00:58:21.110
is just a giant ball of burning
gas, which is just heat.

00:58:21.110 --> 00:58:24.480
And it generates all those
photons of different energies.

00:58:24.480 --> 00:58:27.840
So what if it just absorbs
them into a little sun?

00:58:27.840 --> 00:58:30.890
We just make a
tiny artificial sun

00:58:30.890 --> 00:58:34.490
which just absorbs the whole
energy of all those photons

00:58:34.490 --> 00:58:36.020
and heats up.

00:58:36.020 --> 00:58:39.230
But then we design
the sun in such a way

00:58:39.230 --> 00:58:43.530
that it only emits good kind
of atoms, just the right size,

00:58:43.530 --> 00:58:46.910
which can be used by our
solar cell very efficiently.

00:58:46.910 --> 00:58:49.730
Then obviously we have
another material challenge.

00:58:49.730 --> 00:58:53.540
Because this sun appears to be
made of some kind of material

00:58:53.540 --> 00:58:56.000
which only made those photons.

00:58:56.000 --> 00:58:59.660
And those materials have to
be stable at high temperatures

00:58:59.660 --> 00:59:01.880
because it's going to be hot.

00:59:01.880 --> 00:59:03.950
So there are a lot of
different challenges

00:59:03.950 --> 00:59:06.800
in the design of solar cells.

00:59:06.800 --> 00:59:09.500
And the next speakers,
Evelyn and Jeffrey,

00:59:09.500 --> 00:59:12.140
are going to tell you
about different aspects

00:59:12.140 --> 00:59:15.360
of this technology and
challenges they're facing

00:59:15.360 --> 00:59:18.680
and what kind of innovations
they come up with to overcome

00:59:18.680 --> 00:59:20.430
those challenges.

00:59:20.430 --> 00:59:21.380
So I give it to them.

00:59:38.220 --> 00:59:40.630
Thanks, [? Sveda, ?] for
the great introduction.

00:59:40.630 --> 00:59:44.110
And both Jeff and
my lives easier.

00:59:44.110 --> 00:59:46.080
So today, what I'd
like to share with you

00:59:46.080 --> 00:59:48.205
is a technology that
we've been working on.

00:59:48.205 --> 00:59:49.830
And it's collaborative,
so of course, I

00:59:49.830 --> 00:59:53.010
wanted to acknowledge the people
that actually really do it.

00:59:53.010 --> 00:59:55.380
My students, Andrej
Lenert and David Bierman,

00:59:55.380 --> 00:59:57.810
and my former
postdoc Youngsuk Nam.

00:59:57.810 --> 00:59:59.890
As well as our
collaborators who also

00:59:59.890 --> 01:00:01.770
a student, Walker
Chan, who works

01:00:01.770 --> 01:00:05.700
with Ivan Celanovic, as well
as Marin Soljacic in physics.

01:00:05.700 --> 01:00:07.350
And what we've
been working on is

01:00:07.350 --> 01:00:10.830
thinking about these kind
of solar thermophotovoltaic

01:00:10.830 --> 01:00:11.630
devices.

01:00:11.630 --> 01:00:13.630
And I'll talk a little
bit about how this works.

01:00:13.630 --> 01:00:15.617
But I think hopefully
from [? Sveda's ?] intro,

01:00:15.617 --> 01:00:16.200
you'll see it.

01:00:16.200 --> 01:00:19.320
We're harnessing the
sun in the form of heat

01:00:19.320 --> 01:00:22.500
to eventually
generate electricity.

01:00:22.500 --> 01:00:25.080
So when you typically
think about the sun

01:00:25.080 --> 01:00:27.120
in the form of heat and
how you harness that,

01:00:27.120 --> 01:00:31.320
you think of solar thermal
approaches such as this power--

01:00:31.320 --> 01:00:34.020
these kind of steam
power plant designs.

01:00:34.020 --> 01:00:36.270
What you may be familiar
with and you've seen around

01:00:36.270 --> 01:00:40.290
are these parabolic
trough type configurations

01:00:40.290 --> 01:00:43.050
where you concentrate
the sun, again,

01:00:43.050 --> 01:00:45.810
like in the form of heat
to heat up this working

01:00:45.810 --> 01:00:47.310
fluid in this tube.

01:00:47.310 --> 01:00:48.970
It's usually of oil.

01:00:48.970 --> 01:00:53.323
And this oil then drives
the steam cycle, right?

01:00:53.323 --> 01:00:54.990
And then the advantage
of this approach,

01:00:54.990 --> 01:00:56.448
as [? Sveda ?]
already said, is you

01:00:56.448 --> 01:00:59.700
can now collect all of
the different wavelengths

01:00:59.700 --> 01:01:04.500
from the sun and use it as
heat to drive the steam engine

01:01:04.500 --> 01:01:06.900
and generate electricity.

01:01:06.900 --> 01:01:08.760
But typically, these
are much more efficient

01:01:08.760 --> 01:01:10.630
at the utility scale.

01:01:10.630 --> 01:01:14.130
And there are challenges with
maintenance costs in terms

01:01:14.130 --> 01:01:16.020
of these large scale systems.

01:01:16.020 --> 01:01:17.640
But there could be
an added advantage,

01:01:17.640 --> 01:01:20.640
because one of the challenges
is that you also want to-- you

01:01:20.640 --> 01:01:22.440
don't have sun all the time.

01:01:22.440 --> 01:01:26.190
And you might want to store some
of this via a thermal method,

01:01:26.190 --> 01:01:28.170
such as a thermal
storage, mechanism

01:01:28.170 --> 01:01:30.360
such that you can have
continuous electricity

01:01:30.360 --> 01:01:33.380
generation-- or
power generation.

01:01:33.380 --> 01:01:35.130
So, as [? Sveda ?]
already said, the other

01:01:35.130 --> 01:01:37.830
approaches the using
these photovoltaics.

01:01:37.830 --> 01:01:39.780
However, only
certain wavelengths

01:01:39.780 --> 01:01:42.450
are useful to excite
these charged carriers

01:01:42.450 --> 01:01:44.183
to generate electricity.

01:01:44.183 --> 01:01:45.600
But the advantage
of this approach

01:01:45.600 --> 01:01:48.510
is extremely scalable
for your rooftop

01:01:48.510 --> 01:01:50.250
or to larger scale systems.

01:01:50.250 --> 01:01:53.280
And they're solid state where
there's no moving parts at all.

01:01:53.280 --> 01:01:56.250
And the challenge however,
like I said already,

01:01:56.250 --> 01:01:59.910
is that it's very
hard to store energy--

01:01:59.910 --> 01:02:01.730
electrical energy.

01:02:01.730 --> 01:02:04.970
So when you look at these
two different approaches,

01:02:04.970 --> 01:02:07.210
you'd like to think that,
maybe we can combine them

01:02:07.210 --> 01:02:09.190
in some way such
that you could take

01:02:09.190 --> 01:02:12.470
advantage of the great things
about these technologies.

01:02:12.470 --> 01:02:15.430
So if we can try to
harness the full spectrum

01:02:15.430 --> 01:02:17.650
of the sun in the
form of heat and we

01:02:17.650 --> 01:02:19.420
can make it scalable
and solid-state

01:02:19.420 --> 01:02:23.320
and also offer this continuous
opportunity to generate power,

01:02:23.320 --> 01:02:24.730
this would be great.

01:02:24.730 --> 01:02:27.370
And the opportunity
is using what we call

01:02:27.370 --> 01:02:29.490
solar thermal photovoltaics.

01:02:29.490 --> 01:02:33.270
And so the idea is you take
a typical configuration

01:02:33.270 --> 01:02:36.870
of a photovoltaic cell,
where you collect all of--

01:02:36.870 --> 01:02:39.870
you have the sun shining
on this photovoltaic cell,

01:02:39.870 --> 01:02:42.720
and instead of
this, in fact, you

01:02:42.720 --> 01:02:46.110
have an intermediate
conversion process

01:02:46.110 --> 01:02:51.990
where we have this materials of
a selective absorber emitter.

01:02:51.990 --> 01:02:54.660
So what we're doing here
is that, again, we're

01:02:54.660 --> 01:02:57.090
collecting now
the whole spectrum

01:02:57.090 --> 01:02:59.970
of the sun in the form
the heat by absorbing it

01:02:59.970 --> 01:03:01.380
with this absorber.

01:03:01.380 --> 01:03:02.940
And then we're
transferring this heat

01:03:02.940 --> 01:03:05.070
to a high enough
temperature such

01:03:05.070 --> 01:03:10.200
that we can target the ideal
wavelengths the PV cell is

01:03:10.200 --> 01:03:12.240
actually using these photons.

01:03:12.240 --> 01:03:15.120
And using this kind of
design of this emitter,

01:03:15.120 --> 01:03:19.240
such that then we can get
efficient power generation.

01:03:19.240 --> 01:03:21.335
And so you would think,
well, exactly how

01:03:21.335 --> 01:03:22.460
do you go about doing this?

01:03:22.460 --> 01:03:24.668
And the reason why we're
thinking about this approach

01:03:24.668 --> 01:03:26.380
is that, in fact,
theoretically, you

01:03:26.380 --> 01:03:29.800
can get very, very high
efficiencies-- over about 80%

01:03:29.800 --> 01:03:30.910
or so.

01:03:30.910 --> 01:03:33.370
So the key is in
the materials design

01:03:33.370 --> 01:03:35.800
and ultimately, in the
design of this kind

01:03:35.800 --> 01:03:38.950
of intermediate converter--
this selective absorber emitter.

01:03:38.950 --> 01:03:41.770
And that of also how
you design the PV cell

01:03:41.770 --> 01:03:43.570
to match the
temperatures that you

01:03:43.570 --> 01:03:45.820
need to thermally
emit to what, we

01:03:45.820 --> 01:03:48.510
call, the band gap
of the PV cell.

01:03:48.510 --> 01:03:50.260
So to give you a little
bit of perspective

01:03:50.260 --> 01:03:53.170
though, because we're looking
at particular wavelengths,

01:03:53.170 --> 01:03:55.810
in fact, this emission--
this thermal mission

01:03:55.810 --> 01:03:57.400
has to be at very
high temperatures.

01:03:57.400 --> 01:04:01.780
Usually, on the order of
about 1,000 degrees Celsius.

01:04:01.780 --> 01:04:03.070
So how do we do this?

01:04:03.070 --> 01:04:05.010
So I just gave a schematic
of the technology

01:04:05.010 --> 01:04:06.490
that we're really focused on.

01:04:06.490 --> 01:04:08.610
We're looking at this
planar setup, just here.

01:04:08.610 --> 01:04:10.440
So the sun comes in here.

01:04:10.440 --> 01:04:13.680
We can concentrate the sun so
we can get it hotter and hotter.

01:04:13.680 --> 01:04:16.380
This absorber emitter could be a
higher and higher temperatures,

01:04:16.380 --> 01:04:17.980
because that's what we need.

01:04:17.980 --> 01:04:21.960
And so we absorb this total
spectrum of the solar radiation

01:04:21.960 --> 01:04:24.732
using these carbon nanotubes.

01:04:24.732 --> 01:04:26.190
It turns out the
reason we're using

01:04:26.190 --> 01:04:27.940
these kind of carbon
nanotubes structures,

01:04:27.940 --> 01:04:29.550
is they're very good absorbers.

01:04:29.550 --> 01:04:31.830
They're almost near the
ideal perfect absorber

01:04:31.830 --> 01:04:33.500
of a black body absorber.

01:04:33.500 --> 01:04:36.090
So you could see that
absorbtivity is close to 1,

01:04:36.090 --> 01:04:37.150
in this case.

01:04:37.150 --> 01:04:39.523
So we're really trying to
absorb as much as possible.

01:04:39.523 --> 01:04:40.940
And meanwhile, as
I said, then you

01:04:40.940 --> 01:04:43.680
can get it high enough
temperature so that then you

01:04:43.680 --> 01:04:47.940
can try to tailor the emission
close to the band gap of the PV

01:04:47.940 --> 01:04:48.572
cell.

01:04:48.572 --> 01:04:51.030
And in this case, we've been
working with our collaborators

01:04:51.030 --> 01:04:53.640
where they've been designing
these one-dimensional photonic

01:04:53.640 --> 01:04:56.850
crystals which consist of
these different layered stacks

01:04:56.850 --> 01:04:59.490
of silicon and
silicon dioxide films.

01:04:59.490 --> 01:05:01.860
And that way, we can get
constructive interference such

01:05:01.860 --> 01:05:06.000
that you can emit close to
this ideal kind of a band

01:05:06.000 --> 01:05:08.050
gap of the PV cell.

01:05:08.050 --> 01:05:09.790
And of course, there
are tricks to this.

01:05:09.790 --> 01:05:11.957
And I'll just-- won't go
on too many the subtleties.

01:05:11.957 --> 01:05:14.050
But in fact, you
can see here that we

01:05:14.050 --> 01:05:16.510
placed the carbon
nanotubes selectively

01:05:16.510 --> 01:05:20.320
in this particular area
of the total area--

01:05:20.320 --> 01:05:21.610
the active area.

01:05:21.610 --> 01:05:23.950
And the reason we do
this is, in fact, you

01:05:23.950 --> 01:05:26.200
may recall from
some maybe physics

01:05:26.200 --> 01:05:28.690
lectures that we have
very good absorbtivity,

01:05:28.690 --> 01:05:30.910
you also have great emissivity.

01:05:30.910 --> 01:05:34.360
And we have to high emissivity,
you lose all the heat too.

01:05:34.360 --> 01:05:37.540
So we've actually done a
geometric type configuration

01:05:37.540 --> 01:05:39.520
to help us with
this problem where

01:05:39.520 --> 01:05:43.690
if we now change that
area ratio between what's

01:05:43.690 --> 01:05:46.960
the active area of this
emitter compared to that

01:05:46.960 --> 01:05:49.840
of the absorber and we
coat the rest of this

01:05:49.840 --> 01:05:52.480
with a very low emissivity
material, essentially,

01:05:52.480 --> 01:05:54.950
what kind of change the
energy balance of the system.

01:05:54.950 --> 01:05:57.130
So we can really
maximize absorption

01:05:57.130 --> 01:06:00.250
if we get to higher and
higher levels of input power

01:06:00.250 --> 01:06:02.200
from the solar lights.

01:06:02.200 --> 01:06:04.360
We have to concentrate
it at higher levels.

01:06:04.360 --> 01:06:06.190
And this way, then we
can actually really

01:06:06.190 --> 01:06:09.580
improve the efficiency
of the absorption process

01:06:09.580 --> 01:06:12.052
while minimizing some
of this emission.

01:06:12.052 --> 01:06:13.510
So these are the
kinds of materials

01:06:13.510 --> 01:06:14.750
that we're working with.

01:06:14.750 --> 01:06:19.360
And so far, we've demonstrated
three to four times higher

01:06:19.360 --> 01:06:21.700
efficiency that
has been reported.

01:06:21.700 --> 01:06:24.070
It's about 3% to 4%
efficiency overall,

01:06:24.070 --> 01:06:25.960
which is relatively low still.

01:06:25.960 --> 01:06:28.720
But with our work, we've
shown that if we just

01:06:28.720 --> 01:06:31.250
make the system slightly
bigger, then in fact,

01:06:31.250 --> 01:06:34.480
you can reach 20% conversion
efficiencies, which

01:06:34.480 --> 01:06:36.385
make them comparable
to solar cells, which

01:06:36.385 --> 01:06:38.260
is the one that everyone's
talking about now.

01:06:38.260 --> 01:06:40.600
And there's, again, the
theoretical potential

01:06:40.600 --> 01:06:42.360
is much higher.

01:06:42.360 --> 01:06:45.290
So I thought I'd just show you
a video of some of these images

01:06:45.290 --> 01:06:51.320
that were taken from our
media hope and the MIT

01:06:51.320 --> 01:06:53.120
mechanical design
department John Frita.

01:06:53.120 --> 01:06:55.250
Just give you a
little perspective

01:06:55.250 --> 01:06:58.280
of what the system looks
like when it's operating.

01:07:03.472 --> 01:07:05.284
I lost my cursor.

01:07:05.284 --> 01:07:07.140
Let me play this.

01:07:07.140 --> 01:07:08.200
OK, here we go.

01:07:08.200 --> 01:07:11.520
So this is actually looking
front down onto the absorber.

01:07:11.520 --> 01:07:14.070
This is another example of these
carbon nanotubes absorbers.

01:07:14.070 --> 01:07:15.270
And this is your PV cell.

01:07:15.270 --> 01:07:18.180
We're placing it on top in
this planar or system here.

01:07:21.075 --> 01:07:22.900
It sopped playing.

01:07:22.900 --> 01:07:23.400
Whoops.

01:07:29.710 --> 01:07:33.250
I'm not sure why it stopped.

01:07:33.250 --> 01:07:36.520
Oh, thank you.

01:07:36.520 --> 01:07:37.565
OK, so here's absorbers.

01:07:37.565 --> 01:07:39.910
So this is kind of
looking in from the--

01:07:39.910 --> 01:07:41.380
from the perspective of the sun.

01:07:41.380 --> 01:07:43.660
And here is another example.

01:07:43.660 --> 01:07:46.580
And you can see us placing
it close to the PV cell.

01:07:46.580 --> 01:07:48.970
And here is when we
start to open the shutter

01:07:48.970 --> 01:07:55.330
to have our simulated solar
spectrum onto our device.

01:07:55.330 --> 01:07:57.760
And you can see now
that it's glowing

01:07:57.760 --> 01:08:00.580
hot, which indicate that it's
at very high temperatures.

01:08:00.580 --> 01:08:03.710
And that's where it's
emitting to the PV cell.

01:08:03.710 --> 01:08:05.892
And so just gives you a
little bit of perspective

01:08:05.892 --> 01:08:07.600
as to the types of
experiments that we're

01:08:07.600 --> 01:08:10.570
doing to help
realize and develop

01:08:10.570 --> 01:08:13.210
materials that can
help us kind of realize

01:08:13.210 --> 01:08:15.520
the potential of
this technology.

01:08:15.520 --> 01:08:16.872
So thank you very much.

01:08:16.872 --> 01:08:20.316
[APPLAUSE]

01:08:38.040 --> 01:08:41.399
OK, how's everybody doing?

01:08:41.399 --> 01:08:42.450
Good?

01:08:42.450 --> 01:08:43.109
OK.

01:08:43.109 --> 01:08:45.210
Hi, I'm Jeff.

01:08:45.210 --> 01:08:47.279
I am here to tell
you about a project

01:08:47.279 --> 01:08:51.330
that we're working on that's
trying to make solar PV 3D.

01:08:51.330 --> 01:08:54.240
And I thought I'd start by
handing some things out.

01:08:54.240 --> 01:08:57.029
And actually, I thought you
might be interested in what

01:08:57.029 --> 01:08:59.404
a solar cell-- what the active
part of the solar cell is.

01:08:59.404 --> 01:09:01.154
What's the part that's
doing all the work?

01:09:01.154 --> 01:09:02.340
So I have that here.

01:09:02.340 --> 01:09:04.950
And this way, you don't
have to take apart and break

01:09:04.950 --> 01:09:06.630
your Little Suns.

01:09:06.630 --> 01:09:07.500
But this is it.

01:09:07.500 --> 01:09:11.333
This is what's inside
of your solar cell.

01:09:11.333 --> 01:09:12.750
This is what's
doing all the work.

01:09:12.750 --> 01:09:15.498
And then everything else is
just sort of encapsulation.

01:09:15.498 --> 01:09:17.040
And you can see that
I just broke it.

01:09:17.040 --> 01:09:17.857
And that's fine.

01:09:17.857 --> 01:09:18.899
I want you to break this.

01:09:18.899 --> 01:09:19.979
I'm going to hand this around.

01:09:19.979 --> 01:09:21.390
Breaking things
is a great thing.

01:09:21.390 --> 01:09:23.279
Touch it, feel it, break it.

01:09:23.279 --> 01:09:24.570
I tell my students--

01:09:24.570 --> 01:09:26.819
I tell my kids and my
students, you learn best

01:09:26.819 --> 01:09:28.439
by picking yourself up.

01:09:28.439 --> 01:09:30.672
So break stuff, make mistakes.

01:09:30.672 --> 01:09:32.130
I'm passing this
around just so you

01:09:32.130 --> 01:09:34.920
can feel what the solar
cell actually is like.

01:09:34.920 --> 01:09:37.740
That's actually mostly
silicon, which is just sand

01:09:37.740 --> 01:09:41.609
that we've put a lot of energy
into to make into silicon.

01:09:41.609 --> 01:09:43.109
So that's what
we're all the cost

01:09:43.109 --> 01:09:45.000
is in terms of material
that's in there.

01:09:45.000 --> 01:09:47.410
And then here's what the
final solar cell looks like.

01:09:47.410 --> 01:09:48.899
So I'm going to
hand this out too.

01:09:48.899 --> 01:09:51.600
You'll notice this weighs a lot
more because it's encapsulated,

01:09:51.600 --> 01:09:53.520
which is something you
need to do to give it

01:09:53.520 --> 01:09:57.210
the 0-year lifetime that you get
if you go to, say, Home Depot.

01:10:00.690 --> 01:10:02.250
First of all, can
anybody guess where

01:10:02.250 --> 01:10:04.655
I bought that solar panel?

01:10:04.655 --> 01:10:05.280
That one their.

01:10:05.280 --> 01:10:08.160
Best price you get for
those solar panels.

01:10:08.160 --> 01:10:08.820
Any guess?

01:10:08.820 --> 01:10:10.230
eBay, you bet.

01:10:10.230 --> 01:10:11.060
You bet.

01:10:11.060 --> 01:10:13.850
If you want to buy solar panels
that you can break or make

01:10:13.850 --> 01:10:16.410
into functional
panels, go to eBay.

01:10:16.410 --> 01:10:17.940
It's better prices than China.

01:10:17.940 --> 01:10:21.360
This is actually-- actually,
they come from China and then--

01:10:21.360 --> 01:10:22.987
anyway.

01:10:22.987 --> 01:10:24.570
This is what has
when you encapsulate.

01:10:24.570 --> 01:10:26.850
What do you notice about
these panels though?

01:10:26.850 --> 01:10:29.580
There's one thing I
want you to notice.

01:10:29.580 --> 01:10:32.150
There's a hint in the title.

01:10:32.150 --> 01:10:34.210
They're flat.

01:10:34.210 --> 01:10:36.795
But that's not optimal.

01:10:36.795 --> 01:10:38.170
In fact, if you
just look around,

01:10:38.170 --> 01:10:40.810
nature does not collect
sunlight in a flat manner.

01:10:40.810 --> 01:10:42.130
The tops of trees are not flat.

01:10:42.130 --> 01:10:44.080
Now, that's not necessarily
the best example

01:10:44.080 --> 01:10:48.580
because trees are designing and
optimizing around many factors,

01:10:48.580 --> 01:10:51.640
like survival, water
distribution, weight

01:10:51.640 --> 01:10:52.550
and so forth.

01:10:52.550 --> 01:10:54.592
But solar cells--
solar collection--

01:10:54.592 --> 01:10:56.050
you can pass this
around, break it.

01:10:56.050 --> 01:10:56.925
Do whatever you want.

01:10:56.925 --> 01:10:58.210
Take it home.

01:10:58.210 --> 01:11:00.340
Seriously.

01:11:00.340 --> 01:11:02.170
They're flat and they
don't need to be.

01:11:02.170 --> 01:11:04.240
And that's not optimal.

01:11:04.240 --> 01:11:05.980
So how do you go
three dimensional?

01:11:05.980 --> 01:11:06.963
What would happen?

01:11:06.963 --> 01:11:08.380
Well that's the
question we asked.

01:11:08.380 --> 01:11:10.338
And you wouldn't have
asked this a little while

01:11:10.338 --> 01:11:12.940
ago because the materials cost
dominated over the installation

01:11:12.940 --> 01:11:13.440
costs.

01:11:13.440 --> 01:11:14.870
That's no longer true.

01:11:14.870 --> 01:11:16.630
So now you can ask
questions like,

01:11:16.630 --> 01:11:18.520
what happens if we
waste a little material

01:11:18.520 --> 01:11:20.990
but go 3D, for example?

01:11:20.990 --> 01:11:24.670
So it turns out that it's
very easy to compute.

01:11:24.670 --> 01:11:27.820
If you had, say, five flat
panels like that laid out flat

01:11:27.820 --> 01:11:29.980
and you want to compute
how much energy you

01:11:29.980 --> 01:11:32.290
get from that in
the day depending

01:11:32.290 --> 01:11:35.320
on when and where you are in the
world, that's easy to compute.

01:11:35.320 --> 01:11:37.420
But what if you just fold it up?

01:11:37.420 --> 01:11:38.230
That's easy.

01:11:38.230 --> 01:11:40.480
What if you just fold
them up and make a cube?

01:11:40.480 --> 01:11:42.250
That's actually not
easy to compute.

01:11:42.250 --> 01:11:44.153
And really, people
haven't thought about this

01:11:44.153 --> 01:11:45.820
because again, the
materials costs would

01:11:45.820 --> 01:11:47.200
have killed you 10 years ago.

01:11:47.200 --> 01:11:48.320
Won't anymore.

01:11:48.320 --> 01:11:51.700
So the question is, first of
all, what is the best 3D shape

01:11:51.700 --> 01:11:53.680
for a solar panel if you go 3D?

01:11:53.680 --> 01:11:55.722
And that was the first
question we wanted to ask.

01:11:55.722 --> 01:11:58.360
And we found all sorts
of beautiful designs

01:11:58.360 --> 01:12:00.850
that we thought, well, what
if we just try some of these?

01:12:00.850 --> 01:12:03.940
But then we thought, let's
be a little smarter about it.

01:12:03.940 --> 01:12:06.730
We're engineers, so
let's engineer this.

01:12:06.730 --> 01:12:08.770
And what we did is
we built actually

01:12:08.770 --> 01:12:11.320
a really cool
simulation that's based

01:12:11.320 --> 01:12:12.743
on the properties of evolution.

01:12:12.743 --> 01:12:15.160
So it actually using something
called a genetic algorithm.

01:12:15.160 --> 01:12:18.160
It can take any random
assortment-- any random 3D

01:12:18.160 --> 01:12:21.130
shape, and you can tell it
where you are and when you are.

01:12:21.130 --> 01:12:24.100
And it'll give you
an emergent order.

01:12:24.100 --> 01:12:27.100
And it'll tell you what
the optimal structure

01:12:27.100 --> 01:12:31.280
for that given location should
be if you were to go 3D.

01:12:31.280 --> 01:12:32.980
How do you maximize the power?

01:12:32.980 --> 01:12:35.990
So that was kind of cool.

01:12:35.990 --> 01:12:38.680
And then we said, well, OK,
that's just a simulation.

01:12:38.680 --> 01:12:40.060
How about let's build it.

01:12:40.060 --> 01:12:44.200
And so as Olafur mentioned,
we don't actually

01:12:44.200 --> 01:12:48.290
think too much about design.

01:12:48.290 --> 01:12:51.070
So we first built this.

01:12:51.070 --> 01:12:54.217
And we said, well, this
looks like pretty good to us.

01:12:54.217 --> 01:12:56.050
And then we went to our
architecture friends

01:12:56.050 --> 01:12:57.370
and they said, no, no, no.

01:12:57.370 --> 01:12:58.620
That doesn't look good at all.

01:12:58.620 --> 01:12:59.770
You need to do this.

01:12:59.770 --> 01:13:02.020
And then we said, well, we
can't buy solar panels that

01:13:02.020 --> 01:13:03.112
look like this.

01:13:03.112 --> 01:13:05.320
So we sort of compromised
and we had some iterations.

01:13:05.320 --> 01:13:08.320
And we were now-- and please,
take these, break them.

01:13:08.320 --> 01:13:09.070
Do what you want.

01:13:09.070 --> 01:13:12.760
These are just these-- there
are just to touch and feel it.

01:13:12.760 --> 01:13:14.290
This is 3D PV.

01:13:14.290 --> 01:13:18.400
So we built these, we put them
on the rooftop of our building

01:13:18.400 --> 01:13:19.690
and we measured data.

01:13:19.690 --> 01:13:22.150
And here's the sun going
across one of these 3D panels.

01:13:22.150 --> 01:13:24.670
You can see sometimes
you're wasting panels.

01:13:24.670 --> 01:13:27.310
And here, you're absorbing, and
then it changes during the day.

01:13:27.310 --> 01:13:29.230
But one thing that's
really interesting,

01:13:29.230 --> 01:13:31.300
well, we put these on the
rooftop of our building

01:13:31.300 --> 01:13:32.680
to do the testing.

01:13:32.680 --> 01:13:35.380
And I'll tell you, I just
moved here from California.

01:13:35.380 --> 01:13:39.700
And all the simulations
were for sun only.

01:13:39.700 --> 01:13:43.750
Because it's easy to do sun
only, it's hard to do clouds.

01:13:43.750 --> 01:13:47.800
And we just needed one
sunny day, just one.

01:13:47.800 --> 01:13:50.200
And it was really frustrating
because it took two weeks

01:13:50.200 --> 01:13:51.670
to get that in Boston.

01:13:51.670 --> 01:13:53.560
And I'm looking at
the California weather

01:13:53.560 --> 01:13:56.145
on my phone every day, all sun.

01:13:56.145 --> 01:13:57.520
But actually, it
was really cool.

01:13:57.520 --> 01:14:00.310
Because what happened is
the bad weather of Boston

01:14:00.310 --> 01:14:01.780
actually helped
us learn something

01:14:01.780 --> 01:14:04.420
really cool about going 3D
that we had not predicted,

01:14:04.420 --> 01:14:07.450
which is that it's actually
better on cloudy days.

01:14:07.450 --> 01:14:10.630
Because when you go 3D you take
advantage of scattered light--

01:14:10.630 --> 01:14:11.710
of diffuse light.

01:14:11.710 --> 01:14:13.780
Can't do that when you're 2D.

01:14:13.780 --> 01:14:15.590
So that was really
cool actually.

01:14:15.590 --> 01:14:18.910
And I thank the Boston weather.

01:14:18.910 --> 01:14:21.370
So anyway we tested it and
some cool things happen.

01:14:21.370 --> 01:14:25.480
So if you look at the power
you can generate versus height,

01:14:25.480 --> 01:14:26.570
it's linear with height.

01:14:26.570 --> 01:14:28.542
So if you have a
confined base area,

01:14:28.542 --> 01:14:30.250
you know that you can
just keep going up.

01:14:30.250 --> 01:14:31.708
That makes sense
because now you're

01:14:31.708 --> 01:14:35.640
collecting light when the sun
is shining kind of on the side.

01:14:35.640 --> 01:14:38.140
And so you go higher up, you're
going to collect more light.

01:14:38.140 --> 01:14:40.140
What's also interesting
is the worst time of day

01:14:40.140 --> 01:14:41.890
is noon for the same reason.

01:14:41.890 --> 01:14:44.080
Right when the sun
is overhead, you

01:14:44.080 --> 01:14:47.440
do worse than as the sun kind
of goes down around the edges.

01:14:47.440 --> 01:14:50.080
But what's cool-- so
this is a flat panel

01:14:50.080 --> 01:14:52.960
and this is the power
you get during the day.

01:14:52.960 --> 01:14:55.280
That's the typical
power curve for a day.

01:14:55.280 --> 01:14:57.490
And if you look at
that, you got about 5,

01:14:57.490 --> 01:15:00.130
5 and 1/2 hours of
usable electricity.

01:15:00.130 --> 01:15:03.760
If you dual access
track a solar cell,

01:15:03.760 --> 01:15:05.620
you can get up to
eight because you

01:15:05.620 --> 01:15:07.210
can go much higher
because you're now

01:15:07.210 --> 01:15:08.980
tracking the sun each day.

01:15:08.980 --> 01:15:10.990
But we don't do that
because it's moving parts

01:15:10.990 --> 01:15:13.760
on your rooftop and that breaks
and it costs a lot of money.

01:15:13.760 --> 01:15:15.400
So it's not cost effective.

01:15:15.400 --> 01:15:18.160
With 3D, built into
the structure--

01:15:18.160 --> 01:15:19.870
because you've wasted
a little material.

01:15:19.870 --> 01:15:22.675
Built into the structure,
you have the eight hours.

01:15:22.675 --> 01:15:24.550
Because now, you're
always sort of generating

01:15:24.550 --> 01:15:28.367
a constant amount of power
no matter where the sun is.

01:15:28.367 --> 01:15:29.950
And we can actually
optimize for that.

01:15:29.950 --> 01:15:31.575
We didn't even try
to optimize for that

01:15:31.575 --> 01:15:32.840
in this particular case.

01:15:32.840 --> 01:15:36.160
So this is sort of what
we've been excited about.

01:15:36.160 --> 01:15:38.980
And we talked to
different people

01:15:38.980 --> 01:15:42.420
about where we could try to
work with this kind of thing.

01:15:42.420 --> 01:15:44.920
And one of the first interesting
opportunities that came out

01:15:44.920 --> 01:15:45.420
was--

01:15:45.420 --> 01:15:46.837
by the way, we
still have not been

01:15:46.837 --> 01:15:48.190
able to build a panel like this.

01:15:48.190 --> 01:15:50.140
But we're thinking about it--

01:15:50.140 --> 01:15:51.560
is e-bikes.

01:15:51.560 --> 01:15:54.310
So there's this nice
architecture's rendition

01:15:54.310 --> 01:15:58.060
of a charging station where
you have area confined--

01:15:58.060 --> 01:16:00.370
you have a limitation
in your area.

01:16:00.370 --> 01:16:01.810
You don't want to
put solar panels

01:16:01.810 --> 01:16:03.520
across this beautiful square.

01:16:03.520 --> 01:16:05.140
But maybe if you
put a tower that's

01:16:05.140 --> 01:16:07.600
been optimized in
the right way, you

01:16:07.600 --> 01:16:09.970
could charge say
30-40 bikes a day.

01:16:09.970 --> 01:16:11.050
Now, we think, e-bikes.

01:16:11.050 --> 01:16:12.790
Who uses e-bikes, right?

01:16:12.790 --> 01:16:15.330
Well 120 million
of them in China.

01:16:15.330 --> 01:16:18.130
So there are places, not the
US, where e-bikes are used.

01:16:18.130 --> 01:16:21.190
And there are some universities,
like the University

01:16:21.190 --> 01:16:23.500
of Washington where
actually they've

01:16:23.500 --> 01:16:27.370
shown that if you force
e-bikes onto students,

01:16:27.370 --> 01:16:30.070
you get much less traffic
problems in your campus.

01:16:30.070 --> 01:16:31.672
Much less parking problems too.

01:16:31.672 --> 01:16:33.380
So that's one area
that we're looking at.

01:16:33.380 --> 01:16:34.300
We're looking at other designs.

01:16:34.300 --> 01:16:37.060
And then we thought-- the last
thing I'll say and I'll stop,

01:16:37.060 --> 01:16:40.698
is we thought, well, these
designs are kind of hard.

01:16:40.698 --> 01:16:42.490
We've made some that
actually are foldable.

01:16:42.490 --> 01:16:45.970
So you ship them flat and you
crank them and you can go 3D.

01:16:45.970 --> 01:16:47.830
But it's still hard
because you need

01:16:47.830 --> 01:16:52.705
to have a base
that can handle it.

01:16:52.705 --> 01:16:53.830
You need to build the base.

01:16:53.830 --> 01:16:54.910
You need to build
the base and you

01:16:54.910 --> 01:16:55.870
need to build the structure.

01:16:55.870 --> 01:16:57.830
And it needs to be
weather resistant and all

01:16:57.830 --> 01:16:58.870
of these things.

01:16:58.870 --> 01:17:01.330
And so then we started
thinking, well, wait a second.

01:17:01.330 --> 01:17:04.240
The world has a 3D
landscape already.

01:17:04.240 --> 01:17:05.350
It's called houses.

01:17:05.350 --> 01:17:06.843
It's called buildings.

01:17:06.843 --> 01:17:08.260
So we started
thinking, well, what

01:17:08.260 --> 01:17:12.040
if we couldn't optimize
the structure itself

01:17:12.040 --> 01:17:12.800
as we did here?

01:17:12.800 --> 01:17:15.310
But what if we could
take the landscape

01:17:15.310 --> 01:17:18.220
that we have available to
us and optimize around that?

01:17:18.220 --> 01:17:20.110
So we've been looking
at things like, we

01:17:20.110 --> 01:17:23.410
can take all the
rooftops from Google

01:17:23.410 --> 01:17:26.500
and we can actually
3D print them.

01:17:26.500 --> 01:17:28.030
So we can 3D print
a neighborhood.

01:17:28.030 --> 01:17:29.770
And then we can
think about how--

01:17:29.770 --> 01:17:33.670
let's say, if a city
block in Cambridge

01:17:33.670 --> 01:17:37.810
all calls Home Depot today
and says, I want solar panels,

01:17:37.810 --> 01:17:40.360
every house will get their
panels perfectly aligned

01:17:40.360 --> 01:17:42.160
for the house.

01:17:42.160 --> 01:17:43.600
But the question
is, what happens

01:17:43.600 --> 01:17:45.530
if the houses work together?

01:17:45.530 --> 01:17:47.260
What are the collective effects?

01:17:47.260 --> 01:17:49.540
Could a neighborhood do
better by working together.

01:17:49.540 --> 01:17:50.748
And the answer is absolutely.

01:17:50.748 --> 01:17:52.330
So we've done the simulations.

01:17:52.330 --> 01:17:54.445
And I have a fun
little tw0-house

01:17:54.445 --> 01:17:56.320
demo here that I don't
have time to show you.

01:17:56.320 --> 01:17:58.695
But you guys should come up
and play with that afterwards

01:17:58.695 --> 01:18:00.610
and break it.

01:18:00.610 --> 01:18:02.410
Touch it, feel it,
measure currents

01:18:02.410 --> 01:18:05.440
and you'll see that by
going 3D, you can actually

01:18:05.440 --> 01:18:07.090
open up possibilities.

01:18:07.090 --> 01:18:09.550
Where you never would have
installed panels before,

01:18:09.550 --> 01:18:11.350
you can now install
panels because you're

01:18:11.350 --> 01:18:14.830
using the neighboring house
to help you get sunlight.

01:18:14.830 --> 01:18:16.137
Mirrors are cheap.

01:18:16.137 --> 01:18:18.220
Whether anyone wants to
put mirrors on their house

01:18:18.220 --> 01:18:19.053
is another question.

01:18:19.053 --> 01:18:22.990
But anyway, OK, so
I'll stop there.

01:18:22.990 --> 01:18:25.900
And I think we take
questions now, right?

01:18:25.900 --> 01:18:26.800
What do we do?

01:18:26.800 --> 01:18:28.520
We Sit down.

01:18:28.520 --> 01:18:29.020
We sit down.

01:18:29.020 --> 01:18:30.010
OK, thank you.

01:18:30.010 --> 01:18:31.984
Yeah.

01:18:31.984 --> 01:18:33.950
Oh, I was over time.

01:18:33.950 --> 01:18:35.138
You didn't see that.

01:18:35.138 --> 01:18:40.120
[APPLAUSE]

01:18:40.120 --> 01:18:42.600
OK, guys, I was thinking
about innovations.

01:18:42.600 --> 01:18:44.790
And innovations
are made by people.

01:18:44.790 --> 01:18:47.680
They don't just come
out by themselves.

01:18:47.680 --> 01:18:49.982
So I have two people right here.

01:18:49.982 --> 01:18:51.690
So if you would like
to share something--

01:18:51.690 --> 01:18:54.060
Why-- You're are in mechanical
engineering, both of you,

01:18:54.060 --> 01:18:54.560
right?

01:18:54.560 --> 01:18:55.640
I'm material science.

01:18:55.640 --> 01:18:56.300
I'm sorry.

01:18:56.300 --> 01:18:57.420
Recovering physicist.

01:18:57.420 --> 01:18:59.310
Right.

01:18:59.310 --> 01:19:04.230
Yeah, so why are you interested
in this kind of solar energy

01:19:04.230 --> 01:19:04.800
projects?

01:19:04.800 --> 01:19:08.040
And is it more interesting
than other things you are doing

01:19:08.040 --> 01:19:09.600
or how it compares?

01:19:09.600 --> 01:19:12.870
And so why?

01:19:12.870 --> 01:19:14.370
You want me to
answer that first?

01:19:14.370 --> 01:19:15.570
I'd be happy to.

01:19:15.570 --> 01:19:16.070
OK.

01:19:16.070 --> 01:19:18.195
Are you asking where the
ideas come from or are you

01:19:18.195 --> 01:19:19.200
asking why I am--

01:19:19.200 --> 01:19:20.610
What motivates
you, why you think

01:19:20.610 --> 01:19:23.070
it's more interesting than
something else in material

01:19:23.070 --> 01:19:23.640
science?

01:19:23.640 --> 01:19:26.370
There are different problems
in material science.

01:19:26.370 --> 01:19:28.770
This is true.

01:19:28.770 --> 01:19:31.620
So what drives me, what keeps
me up at night is the following.

01:19:34.620 --> 01:19:37.500
So if you look at sort of
clean energy technology,

01:19:37.500 --> 01:19:38.890
and clean water fits into that.

01:19:38.890 --> 01:19:41.057
I'd like to say water is
the only energy problem you

01:19:41.057 --> 01:19:42.780
can actually die from.

01:19:42.780 --> 01:19:44.730
But anyway, because
they're closely coupled.

01:19:44.730 --> 01:19:50.550
But these are problems that
actually the solution to which

01:19:50.550 --> 01:19:54.190
will be based on a choice of
material or choice of design.

01:19:54.190 --> 01:19:56.100
Now, what's
interesting about that

01:19:56.100 --> 01:19:58.875
is that's very different
than previous revolutions

01:19:58.875 --> 01:19:59.500
that we've had.

01:19:59.500 --> 01:20:02.100
So how many of you
know how many steps

01:20:02.100 --> 01:20:04.200
go into making a
chip in your phone?

01:20:06.972 --> 01:20:08.680
How many elements are
even in your phone?

01:20:08.680 --> 01:20:09.735
Does anybody know?

01:20:09.735 --> 01:20:11.110
Elements from the
periodic table.

01:20:11.110 --> 01:20:12.440
Take a guess.

01:20:12.440 --> 01:20:12.940
30?

01:20:12.940 --> 01:20:13.440
Good guess.

01:20:13.440 --> 01:20:13.940
How many?

01:20:13.940 --> 01:20:14.830
It's actually higher.

01:20:14.830 --> 01:20:15.820
43.

01:20:15.820 --> 01:20:16.473
43.

01:20:16.473 --> 01:20:17.140
Excellent guess.

01:20:17.140 --> 01:20:18.910
It's actually 62.

01:20:18.910 --> 01:20:20.560
62 elements are in this phone.

01:20:20.560 --> 01:20:22.887
There are only about 90
that you have to work with.

01:20:22.887 --> 01:20:24.970
But the thing is that
what's interesting about it,

01:20:24.970 --> 01:20:28.060
if you ask how many
steps are there process--

01:20:28.060 --> 01:20:32.290
how many steps are there to
make a chip in the phone?

01:20:32.290 --> 01:20:32.950
Any guesses?

01:20:35.720 --> 01:20:37.540
It's almost 1,000.

01:20:37.540 --> 01:20:38.710
It's around 1,000.

01:20:38.710 --> 01:20:41.620
But of those how many
have a choice in material?

01:20:41.620 --> 01:20:45.010
How many have had a choice of
material for the last 50 years?

01:20:45.010 --> 01:20:46.870
It's about 10.

01:20:46.870 --> 01:20:49.810
So this revolution here
that we all know and love,

01:20:49.810 --> 01:20:51.850
this is a processing revolution.

01:20:51.850 --> 01:20:53.980
This is not a
materials revolution.

01:20:53.980 --> 01:20:54.990
This is not.

01:20:54.990 --> 01:20:57.220
It's also a design
revolution, clearly.

01:20:57.220 --> 01:21:00.910
But the future of energy will
be a materials revolution.

01:21:00.910 --> 01:21:02.980
Because all the
bottlenecks in energy

01:21:02.980 --> 01:21:05.522
are essentially based
on a choice of material.

01:21:05.522 --> 01:21:07.480
That's how we're going
to get the efficiency up

01:21:07.480 --> 01:21:09.520
and the costs down.

01:21:09.520 --> 01:21:11.310
And so that,
combined with design,

01:21:11.310 --> 01:21:13.060
is what's going to be
the next revolution.

01:21:13.060 --> 01:21:14.518
That's what gets
me really excited.

01:21:14.518 --> 01:21:18.547
As a material scientist,
I get excited.

01:21:18.547 --> 01:21:19.630
As a mechanical engineer--

01:21:19.630 --> 01:21:20.130
[LAUGHTER]

01:21:20.130 --> 01:21:21.190
Uh-oh.

01:21:21.190 --> 01:21:23.890
I would agree with Jeff here.

01:21:23.890 --> 01:21:25.270
Because I think
the opportunities

01:21:25.270 --> 01:21:29.320
are at this intersection of
materials and engineering.

01:21:29.320 --> 01:21:32.500
We, as you probably
saw from the slides

01:21:32.500 --> 01:21:34.780
that I showed, that indeed
we do take advantage

01:21:34.780 --> 01:21:36.010
of a lot of the materials.

01:21:36.010 --> 01:21:38.230
And a lot of these
materials, we don't actually

01:21:38.230 --> 01:21:40.090
create or invent ourselves.

01:21:40.090 --> 01:21:42.790
We rely on material scientists
to help us kind of come up

01:21:42.790 --> 01:21:43.570
with them.

01:21:43.570 --> 01:21:45.820
And then we get
excited about them

01:21:45.820 --> 01:21:48.860
because now we can
realize new properties.

01:21:48.860 --> 01:21:50.710
And I think that's
really the opportunity.

01:21:50.710 --> 01:21:53.440
To be able to tune the
spectral properties.

01:21:53.440 --> 01:21:54.970
To be able to
collect wavelengths

01:21:54.970 --> 01:21:57.760
in certain at
particular wavelengths

01:21:57.760 --> 01:21:59.950
and not collect them and others.

01:21:59.950 --> 01:22:03.100
To be able to now effectively
transport the heat.

01:22:03.100 --> 01:22:06.520
Say, have better thermal
conductors or better insulators

01:22:06.520 --> 01:22:08.950
in certain regions, which
are critical in these kinds

01:22:08.950 --> 01:22:12.070
of devices are, I think,
the opportunities.

01:22:12.070 --> 01:22:14.230
And then being able to
then manipulate them

01:22:14.230 --> 01:22:16.060
and integrate them into device.

01:22:16.060 --> 01:22:18.490
So it's really this
interplay, I think,

01:22:18.490 --> 01:22:20.680
that is really exciting
about-- especially

01:22:20.680 --> 01:22:22.960
now that we are facing
these energy problems.

01:22:22.960 --> 01:22:26.740
And now, we're in a
well-positioned time

01:22:26.740 --> 01:22:28.815
in being able to
now control them

01:22:28.815 --> 01:22:30.190
in a way-- control
the materials,

01:22:30.190 --> 01:22:31.460
create them in a way.

01:22:31.460 --> 01:22:34.855
And I think that's where
the opportunities are too.

01:22:34.855 --> 01:22:37.330
OK, staying on the
people subject,

01:22:37.330 --> 01:22:40.090
you guys don't actually do work.

01:22:40.090 --> 01:22:42.220
Your students do
it-- your postdocs.

01:22:42.220 --> 01:22:44.560
So when new students
come, does it

01:22:44.560 --> 01:22:46.930
feel like more people are
now interested in doing

01:22:46.930 --> 01:22:48.940
energy kind of science or less?

01:22:48.940 --> 01:22:51.880
Is it a lot of interest
in new generation?

01:22:51.880 --> 01:22:55.660
Could I just say, the phrase
we don't actually do work

01:22:55.660 --> 01:22:57.012
is a little troubling.

01:22:57.012 --> 01:22:58.700
[LAUGHTER]

01:22:58.700 --> 01:23:00.370
I didn't mean it.

01:23:00.370 --> 01:23:03.693
But anyway, yeah, we're
not in the lab as much.

01:23:03.693 --> 01:23:04.610
Let's put it that way.

01:23:04.610 --> 01:23:06.520
It's a collective effort.

01:23:06.520 --> 01:23:08.690
But yes, the students are
the ones in the trenches.

01:23:08.690 --> 01:23:11.680
And they do-- yeah, they
deserve all the credit.

01:23:11.680 --> 01:23:16.243
So there is an enormous
amount of energy in energy

01:23:16.243 --> 01:23:17.410
on the part of the students.

01:23:17.410 --> 01:23:19.448
In fact, I think
really if you want

01:23:19.448 --> 01:23:21.490
to look at what the problem
is, is the problem is

01:23:21.490 --> 01:23:22.615
there isn't enough funding.

01:23:22.615 --> 01:23:24.310
There aren't enough resources.

01:23:24.310 --> 01:23:29.740
So there is much more pent
up demand and excitement

01:23:29.740 --> 01:23:32.350
on trying to solve these
energy problems that I

01:23:32.350 --> 01:23:34.570
see than there are spots
available for students

01:23:34.570 --> 01:23:35.860
to work on them.

01:23:35.860 --> 01:23:38.020
That's really the issue here.

01:23:38.020 --> 01:23:39.460
And this is a long-term problem.

01:23:39.460 --> 01:23:40.720
That's part of the issue.

01:23:40.720 --> 01:23:43.780
This is nothing-- this will
not be solved in 18 months.

01:23:43.780 --> 01:23:46.430
This will not be solved
in three to five years.

01:23:46.430 --> 01:23:50.350
So all of a sudden, the
investment part of this

01:23:50.350 --> 01:23:51.970
starts to feel uneasy.

01:23:51.970 --> 01:23:54.220
So VCs get a little uneasy
when you talk about that.

01:23:54.220 --> 01:23:56.770
But if you want to be honest
about it, it's long term.

01:23:56.770 --> 01:23:58.760
You should look at this
as a long-term problem.

01:23:58.760 --> 01:23:59.975
So how do we invest in that?

01:23:59.975 --> 01:24:01.600
Well, usually, it's
the government that

01:24:01.600 --> 01:24:03.460
invests in long-term problems.

01:24:03.460 --> 01:24:06.700
Government funding for
energy is very, very little.

01:24:06.700 --> 01:24:07.300
Very little.

01:24:09.880 --> 01:24:12.910
So indeed, I think we do get
a lot of interest in energy.

01:24:12.910 --> 01:24:15.700
Because I think there's also
been a lot of media about it

01:24:15.700 --> 01:24:16.480
more and more.

01:24:16.480 --> 01:24:18.760
And specifically,
solar has gotten

01:24:18.760 --> 01:24:21.100
a lot of renewed interest.

01:24:21.100 --> 01:24:22.795
But a lot of students
come in and they

01:24:22.795 --> 01:24:23.920
say they want to do energy.

01:24:23.920 --> 01:24:25.630
And the reality is,
I think, we have been

01:24:25.630 --> 01:24:27.470
doing energy for a long time.

01:24:27.470 --> 01:24:32.560
It's just maybe not in
that same words maybe.

01:24:32.560 --> 01:24:35.350
And I think a lot of the
fundamental principles

01:24:35.350 --> 01:24:37.330
that some of the governing
physics that we've

01:24:37.330 --> 01:24:39.910
been talking about
for decades now

01:24:39.910 --> 01:24:45.600
can still be kind of used in the
same sense for energy problems.

01:24:45.600 --> 01:24:48.800
And so I think there are still--

01:24:48.800 --> 01:24:51.490
and certainly as Jeff
already said, that right now,

01:24:51.490 --> 01:24:52.670
funding is still low.

01:24:52.670 --> 01:24:54.970
And as a professor
where you're always

01:24:54.970 --> 01:24:57.940
trying to get funding
to write grants,

01:24:57.940 --> 01:25:01.180
you always try to maneuver
in a way to sell your ideas,

01:25:01.180 --> 01:25:02.810
whether it's energy right now.

01:25:02.810 --> 01:25:04.360
But the idea is
that hopefully, we

01:25:04.360 --> 01:25:07.240
can create new capabilities,
new characterization

01:25:07.240 --> 01:25:10.420
tools and new ways to
fabricate materials

01:25:10.420 --> 01:25:15.340
so that we can understand and
develop new insights into kind

01:25:15.340 --> 01:25:18.340
of fundamental principles that
we haven't discovered before.

01:25:18.340 --> 01:25:21.100
And then really to take it and
address these really bigger

01:25:21.100 --> 01:25:22.000
energy challenges.

01:25:22.000 --> 01:25:25.040
There aren't solutions
near-term necessarily.

01:25:25.040 --> 01:25:28.720
And so I kind of see that
as an opportunity in the way

01:25:28.720 --> 01:25:31.600
we kind of don't think
about more fundamental kind

01:25:31.600 --> 01:25:36.640
of science problems that we
can now also further pursue.

01:25:36.640 --> 01:25:41.200
And eventually, maybe help
us in the longer term.

01:25:41.200 --> 01:25:43.720
Speaking about longer
term-- and I completely

01:25:43.720 --> 01:25:44.890
agree that it takes time.

01:25:44.890 --> 01:25:46.420
All the innovation takes time.

01:25:46.420 --> 01:25:47.560
But it's also uneven.

01:25:47.560 --> 01:25:50.510
Sometimes it moves fast,
sometimes for a long time,

01:25:50.510 --> 01:25:52.930
it seems to be not
moving where you want it.

01:25:52.930 --> 01:25:57.010
So how do you feel photovoltaics
and general solar energy

01:25:57.010 --> 01:25:57.510
science?

01:25:57.510 --> 01:25:58.840
At what stage is it now?

01:25:58.840 --> 01:26:01.510
Is it going to leap
somewhere in the near future?

01:26:01.510 --> 01:26:02.980
Or is it going to--

01:26:02.980 --> 01:26:05.710
maybe it will be
nice type of leap,

01:26:05.710 --> 01:26:07.810
like in terms of
design or delivery

01:26:07.810 --> 01:26:11.080
to places where it's needed.

01:26:11.080 --> 01:26:14.200
First of all, I think we
should go to questions soon.

01:26:14.200 --> 01:26:17.530
But second of all, just a
very quick answer to that.

01:26:17.530 --> 01:26:22.900
You can't predict when
breakthroughs will occur.

01:26:22.900 --> 01:26:25.810
What you can't predict
is if you don't support

01:26:25.810 --> 01:26:28.480
an environment that has the
potential to breakthroughs,

01:26:28.480 --> 01:26:30.970
then they won't occur.

01:26:30.970 --> 01:26:32.300
So that's for sure.

01:26:32.300 --> 01:26:36.130
But innovation,
ideas, creativity,

01:26:36.130 --> 01:26:38.530
that usually happens at
intersections that you cannot

01:26:38.530 --> 01:26:39.640
predict.

01:26:39.640 --> 01:26:42.560
If you tour the Pixar
building-- or it

01:26:42.560 --> 01:26:43.810
used to be the Pixar building.

01:26:43.810 --> 01:26:46.810
Now it's like it's the Disney
building in Emeryville,

01:26:46.810 --> 01:26:48.790
California, they'll
tell you the story

01:26:48.790 --> 01:26:52.060
about how Steve Jobs
insisted on one line--

01:26:52.060 --> 01:26:54.113
one bathroom in on one floor.

01:26:54.113 --> 01:26:55.780
There's a 1,000 people
in this building.

01:26:55.780 --> 01:26:56.680
One bathroom.

01:26:56.680 --> 01:26:59.343
He insisted for four months
that there could only

01:26:59.343 --> 01:27:00.010
be one bathroom.

01:27:00.010 --> 01:27:00.510
Why?

01:27:00.510 --> 01:27:03.190
The best ideas happen
while waiting in line

01:27:03.190 --> 01:27:06.790
next to somebody you don't know.

01:27:06.790 --> 01:27:08.620
And they tell the
story passionately.

01:27:08.620 --> 01:27:10.210
And then they say
they compromised

01:27:10.210 --> 01:27:12.400
because they built this
beautiful atrium for people

01:27:12.400 --> 01:27:14.260
to intersect.

01:27:14.260 --> 01:27:15.460
But you just don't know.

01:27:15.460 --> 01:27:17.890
And so you have to create
an environment that

01:27:17.890 --> 01:27:21.010
allows for those intersections
to happen so that you

01:27:21.010 --> 01:27:23.800
can get those breakthroughs.

01:27:23.800 --> 01:27:24.490
Yeah.

01:27:24.490 --> 01:27:25.090
Oh, sorry.

01:27:25.090 --> 01:27:27.790
I'm calling on people and
I shouldn't be doing that.

01:27:27.790 --> 01:27:30.200
[INAUDIBLE] Let's
take questions.

01:27:30.200 --> 01:27:33.340
In the first presentation, how
hot does the intermediate layer

01:27:33.340 --> 01:27:34.090
get?

01:27:34.090 --> 01:27:35.890
[INAUDIBLE]

01:27:35.890 --> 01:27:37.242
My presentation?

01:27:37.242 --> 01:27:39.366
How hot does the
intermediate layer get?

01:27:39.366 --> 01:27:40.680
This is my presentation?

01:27:40.680 --> 01:27:43.010
Oh, it's going to be
about 1,000 degrees,

01:27:43.010 --> 01:27:46.470
even for a kind of, what they
call, low-band gap PV cell.

01:27:46.470 --> 01:27:47.720
Is that Fahrenheit or Celsius?

01:27:47.720 --> 01:27:48.630
Celsius.

01:27:48.630 --> 01:27:50.780
So it is very high temperature.

01:27:50.780 --> 01:27:52.460
Because it's a thermal
emission process,

01:27:52.460 --> 01:27:55.460
so it really has to really
get to high temperatures.

01:27:55.460 --> 01:27:57.091
And that is the challenge.

01:28:01.510 --> 01:28:08.870
When you were talking about the
inefficiency of photovoltaic

01:28:08.870 --> 01:28:13.430
[INAUDIBLE] related to
the wavelengths that

01:28:13.430 --> 01:28:20.860
are not being absorved, and then
went on to the 3D presentation.

01:28:20.860 --> 01:28:23.560
I'm [INAUDIBLE]
thinking that you

