nanomaterials and [INAUDIBLE]

01:28:30.750 --> 01:28:33.080
surfaces and things
like that, where

01:28:33.080 --> 01:28:39.130
[INAUDIBLE] inside
the cores [INAUDIBLE]

01:28:39.130 --> 01:28:43.770
wavelengths penetrate
[INAUDIBLE]..

01:28:43.770 --> 01:28:49.650
Is there any work in that
direction [INAUDIBLE]??

01:28:49.650 --> 01:28:51.090
Is that a question for--

01:28:51.090 --> 01:28:51.780
I don't care.

01:28:51.780 --> 01:28:52.530
It doesn't matter.

01:28:52.530 --> 01:28:53.140
[INAUDIBLE]

01:28:53.140 --> 01:28:58.550
[INAUDIBLE]

01:28:58.550 --> 01:29:00.308
I think there's a lot of--

01:29:00.308 --> 01:29:01.850
there are a lot of
interesting things

01:29:01.850 --> 01:29:04.160
you can do by
nanostructuring materials.

01:29:04.160 --> 01:29:07.250
And nano has a
little bit of in--

01:29:07.250 --> 01:29:08.250
a way, a bad rap.

01:29:08.250 --> 01:29:10.610
It got it got, I think,
a little overhyped.

01:29:10.610 --> 01:29:15.163
And of course, Spider-Man--
well, anyway, Incredible Hulk.

01:29:15.163 --> 01:29:17.330
We've got all this like
nano in Hollywood and stuff.

01:29:20.617 --> 01:29:22.700
But the fact of the matter
is that we can actually

01:29:22.700 --> 01:29:25.220
design materials down to
the scale of the atom.

01:29:25.220 --> 01:29:29.497
Pretty controllable and in some
ways, depending, at low costs.

01:29:29.497 --> 01:29:31.580
And that has opened up all
kinds of opportunities.

01:29:31.580 --> 01:29:33.920
Actually, solar cells
are always textured

01:29:33.920 --> 01:29:35.610
at that scale on the surface.

01:29:35.610 --> 01:29:38.480
You do that on purpose,
because you collect more light.

01:29:38.480 --> 01:29:40.670
So at that scale
solar cells always

01:29:40.670 --> 01:29:42.548
have that kind of texturing.

01:29:42.548 --> 01:29:44.090
Evelyn, I don't know
if you want to--

01:29:44.090 --> 01:29:45.560
Well, certainly, I
think you're referring

01:29:45.560 --> 01:29:47.540
to-- and people I've been
studying like butterfly wings,

01:29:47.540 --> 01:29:48.420
like you said.

01:29:48.420 --> 01:29:50.750
These are kind of natural
photonic structures.

01:29:50.750 --> 01:29:52.310
And so we're exactly doing that.

01:29:52.310 --> 01:29:55.542
We're trying to create now
artificial photonic structures.

01:29:55.542 --> 01:29:56.250
I'm not doing it.

01:29:56.250 --> 01:29:57.042
My collaborator is.

01:29:57.042 --> 01:29:59.510
But they're really designing
it such that in a way

01:29:59.510 --> 01:30:01.370
that they can
efficiently kind of

01:30:01.370 --> 01:30:06.650
suppress particular
wavelengths and absorb and emit

01:30:06.650 --> 01:30:08.520
particular wavelengths,
things like that.

01:30:08.520 --> 01:30:11.470
So certainly, you could do
that [INAUDIBLE] structure.

01:30:11.470 --> 01:30:13.323
And certainly, that
is an area that

01:30:13.323 --> 01:30:14.615
has received a lot of interest.

01:30:18.071 --> 01:30:20.150
Jeff, it's broken.

01:30:20.150 --> 01:30:21.050
Love it.

01:30:21.050 --> 01:30:22.670
[LAUGHTER]

01:30:22.670 --> 01:30:25.160
Mission accomplished.

01:30:25.160 --> 01:30:26.150
That's good.

01:30:26.150 --> 01:30:28.110
Somebody learned something
when they did this.

01:30:28.110 --> 01:30:30.260
And that's all I care about
