01:30:38.720 --> 01:30:48.027
[INAUDIBLE]

01:30:48.027 --> 01:30:49.110
For these kinds of things?

01:30:49.110 --> 01:30:49.530
Actually, yeah.

01:30:49.530 --> 01:30:51.363
So all of our-- we have
a whole neighborhood

01:30:51.363 --> 01:30:53.660
we printed that's
from Cambridge.

01:30:53.660 --> 01:30:57.470
And there are two
houses over there.

01:30:57.470 --> 01:31:01.430
And so now, we have actually
thought about this--

01:31:01.430 --> 01:31:06.030
about 3D printing the cells
directly into the material.

01:31:06.030 --> 01:31:09.270
There are some tricky things
you have to do because--

01:31:09.270 --> 01:31:11.450
so these are silicon.

01:31:11.450 --> 01:31:13.880
I'm guessing the ones
in Little Sun are

01:31:13.880 --> 01:31:15.740
either [INAUDIBLE] or silicon.

01:31:15.740 --> 01:31:16.450
I'm not sure.

01:31:19.010 --> 01:31:22.340
But there are polymer-based
or organic-based materials

01:31:22.340 --> 01:31:24.808
that you can 3D
print more easily.

01:31:24.808 --> 01:31:26.350
You're not going to
3D print silicon.

01:31:29.000 --> 01:31:31.022
So that's something
we've thought about.

01:31:31.022 --> 01:31:32.480
The problem there
is, then you have

01:31:32.480 --> 01:31:35.480
this issue that those
types of solar cells

01:31:35.480 --> 01:31:39.510
tend to be lower efficiency and
they tend to degrade over time.

01:31:39.510 --> 01:31:41.940
And so you have packaging
problems and so forth.

01:31:41.940 --> 01:31:43.690
But I think it's a
very interesting idea

01:31:43.690 --> 01:31:49.310
to directly 3D print the
active layer into the design.

01:31:57.460 --> 01:32:00.070
This is [INAUDIBLE].

01:32:00.070 --> 01:32:03.930
In terms of the [INAUDIBLE]
size or [INAUDIBLE]..

01:32:11.080 --> 01:32:13.510
So in this case,
what we've created

01:32:13.510 --> 01:32:16.990
is kind of a layered
stack of films.

01:32:16.990 --> 01:32:19.660
And certainly, there are other
types of photonic crystal type

01:32:19.660 --> 01:32:21.365
structures that you can make.

01:32:21.365 --> 01:32:22.990
And our collaborators
have been working

01:32:22.990 --> 01:32:26.962
on these two-dimensional
photonic crystals, which

01:32:26.962 --> 01:32:28.420
is I think what
you're referring to

01:32:28.420 --> 01:32:30.600
with these kind of
poor geometries.

01:32:30.600 --> 01:32:32.590
[INAUDIBLE] geometry
[INAUDIBLE]..

01:32:32.590 --> 01:32:34.050
Oh.

01:32:34.050 --> 01:32:36.220
[INAUDIBLE]

01:32:36.220 --> 01:32:40.400
OK, so right now, you're
talking about the absorber?

01:32:40.400 --> 01:32:40.900
OK, sorry.

01:32:40.900 --> 01:32:41.850
I was confused.

01:32:45.520 --> 01:32:48.880
Our active area is
about one centimeter.

01:32:48.880 --> 01:32:50.980
And so what we're
trying to do is

01:32:50.980 --> 01:32:54.010
we're working with nanotubes
on the order of 100

01:32:54.010 --> 01:32:55.510
nanometers in diameter each.

01:32:55.510 --> 01:32:58.130
But of course, we're trying
to pattern into an area.

01:32:58.130 --> 01:33:00.670
And so when we
shrink the area, we

01:33:00.670 --> 01:33:04.060
can get to much
higher concentrations

01:33:04.060 --> 01:33:06.430
and also get higher
efficiencies.

01:33:06.430 --> 01:33:08.260
So to the areas
that we've shrunk,

01:33:08.260 --> 01:33:13.660
we've gone down to about an
area ratio of about seven,

01:33:13.660 --> 01:33:14.410
I believe.

01:33:14.410 --> 01:33:18.700
So it's about over one
centimeter squared area.

01:33:18.700 --> 01:33:21.530
So that's a kind of area
that we're looking at.

01:33:21.530 --> 01:33:24.940
But we're kind of not
necessarily, I guess--

01:33:24.940 --> 01:33:26.710
as you get smaller and
smaller it's really

01:33:26.710 --> 01:33:29.290
a matter of how
much input power you

01:33:29.290 --> 01:33:32.380
have to get to get a
higher thermal efficiency.

01:33:32.380 --> 01:33:34.720
Because of the fact that
you have to still get

01:33:34.720 --> 01:33:37.060
to those 1,000
degree temperatures

01:33:37.060 --> 01:33:39.670
to emit this particular--
at the wavelengths

01:33:39.670 --> 01:33:41.770
that you want for
the solar cell.

01:33:41.770 --> 01:33:44.860
So it's really a matter
of how much concentration

01:33:44.860 --> 01:33:46.750
you want really in
a practical system.

01:33:46.750 --> 01:33:49.690
Because we know actually one of
the big challenges with solar

01:33:49.690 --> 01:33:52.000
is a lot of these
systems are concentrated.

01:33:52.000 --> 01:33:57.130
And the optics really
dominate the cost.

01:33:57.130 --> 01:33:59.680
And to be realistic,
what we're looking at

01:33:59.680 --> 01:34:00.700
is a really small area.

01:34:00.700 --> 01:34:03.100
It's really kind of what
we can do in the lab.

01:34:03.100 --> 01:34:05.410
And in these kind
of thermal systems,

01:34:05.410 --> 01:34:07.360
as you get to bigger
and bigger areas,

01:34:07.360 --> 01:34:09.280
you minimize a lot
of the losses--

01:34:09.280 --> 01:34:11.680
the parasitic losses that you
would have because now you

01:34:11.680 --> 01:34:14.820
have a much more active
area compared to the losses,

01:34:14.820 --> 01:34:17.850
say, on the edges or
something like that.

01:34:17.850 --> 01:34:20.325
So then hopefully, also
when we have more of that,

01:34:20.325 --> 01:34:21.700
then we can also
tailor it better

01:34:21.700 --> 01:34:23.590
so we don't have to go to as
high concentrations either.

01:34:23.590 --> 01:34:25.450
So then we can
deal with smaller--

01:34:25.450 --> 01:34:27.910
if we design it with
these carbon nanotubes,

01:34:27.910 --> 01:34:30.670
we can think about how we
design the area of that.

01:34:39.860 --> 01:34:41.210
It's not really a question.

