01:39:48.440 --> 01:39:52.360
So a problem that
really is interesting

01:39:52.360 --> 01:39:58.720
is originated with
this comment that--

01:39:58.720 --> 01:39:59.800
oh.

01:39:59.800 --> 01:40:00.300
OK.

01:40:05.470 --> 01:40:05.970
Andrew.

01:40:12.010 --> 01:40:14.860
Where's are-- click here.

01:40:14.860 --> 01:40:15.360
OK.

01:40:18.160 --> 01:40:19.720
Good.

01:40:19.720 --> 01:40:22.293
Paul Farmer was at a
conference several years ago.

01:40:22.293 --> 01:40:23.960
And it was a conference
similar to this.

01:40:23.960 --> 01:40:25.713
We were talking about
scaling up and how

01:40:25.713 --> 01:40:27.130
do you reach out
to people and how

01:40:27.130 --> 01:40:29.800
do you mass produce things
and make them really cheap?

01:40:29.800 --> 01:40:32.800
And Paul stood up
and he said, wait.

01:40:32.800 --> 01:40:36.340
Market forces rarely serve
the needs of my clients.

01:40:36.340 --> 01:40:40.760
And Paul is a pioneer
in health care.

01:40:40.760 --> 01:40:43.540
And by that, he meant it
in the developing world,

01:40:43.540 --> 01:40:45.670
if you're really ill,
you don't want to hear,

01:40:45.670 --> 01:40:48.160
I have a super cheap pill.

01:40:48.160 --> 01:40:50.410
You want to hear, I
have a life saving pill.

01:40:50.410 --> 01:40:52.090
And it's important
to you and I want

01:40:52.090 --> 01:40:54.010
to put the best
things for my health.

01:40:54.010 --> 01:40:58.510
So Paul really got me thinking
about the market and market

01:40:58.510 --> 01:40:59.360
forces.

01:40:59.360 --> 01:41:02.860
How do we really rethink the
problem of scale thinking

01:41:02.860 --> 01:41:05.090
about new materials?

01:41:05.090 --> 01:41:07.240
So just three
points really quick.

01:41:07.240 --> 01:41:10.480
The first is to go soft.

01:41:10.480 --> 01:41:12.730
How can we use less material?

01:41:12.730 --> 01:41:15.130
How can we have a
smaller carbon footprint?

01:41:15.130 --> 01:41:16.720
What kinds of
materials can we use?

01:41:16.720 --> 01:41:21.010
Because materials equal
energy, as Jeff said.

01:41:21.010 --> 01:41:24.010
The energy that goes into making
materials, into heating them,

01:41:24.010 --> 01:41:25.090
into forming them.

01:41:25.090 --> 01:41:28.230
And into to shipping them
all around the world.

01:41:28.230 --> 01:41:31.180
So if you think about the world
and you think about the way

01:41:31.180 --> 01:41:35.020
that we're doing things, it's
really like since World War II.

01:41:35.020 --> 01:41:38.290
We centralized productions.

01:41:38.290 --> 01:41:42.070
We make one thing and
we make it really well.

01:41:42.070 --> 01:41:43.520
And we make a lot of it.

01:41:43.520 --> 01:41:44.620
And then it gets cheaper.

01:41:44.620 --> 01:41:46.075
And then we spend
a lot of carbon

01:41:46.075 --> 01:41:47.450
shipping it all
around the world.

01:41:47.450 --> 01:41:49.510
So we're trying
to think about how

01:41:49.510 --> 01:41:52.540
we can kind of aggregate scales
and how we can sort of change

01:41:52.540 --> 01:41:54.290
this paradigm.

01:41:54.290 --> 01:41:58.360
So what I'm working with in
the Portable Light Project

01:41:58.360 --> 01:42:01.600
is a flexible solar
material, which you see here.

01:42:01.600 --> 01:42:03.280
It's a SIGS material.

01:42:03.280 --> 01:42:04.810
It's a market-based material.

01:42:04.810 --> 01:42:06.760
You can buy it today.

01:42:06.760 --> 01:42:10.000
And we're really trying to
take all most of the hard parts

01:42:10.000 --> 01:42:12.010
of a lighting kit
and remove them

01:42:12.010 --> 01:42:16.570
so that we can just take this
kit and get it into shape,

01:42:16.570 --> 01:42:17.680
fold it up.

01:42:17.680 --> 01:42:20.140
And then take this
very bright LED light,

01:42:20.140 --> 01:42:23.320
which is actually a
little almost too bright,

01:42:23.320 --> 01:42:24.490
and create a lantern.

01:42:24.490 --> 01:42:25.660
Like a soft lantern.

01:42:25.660 --> 01:42:26.910
So that's the premise.

01:42:26.910 --> 01:42:29.260
It's very simple.

01:42:29.260 --> 01:42:33.520
And we're thinking about
that and developing that.

01:42:33.520 --> 01:42:35.080
So it's a kit.

01:42:35.080 --> 01:42:37.390
It's an adaptable kit.

01:42:37.390 --> 01:42:39.040
And so those are
the parts inside.

01:42:39.040 --> 01:42:41.530
The LED, the flexible panel.

01:42:41.530 --> 01:42:42.820
A little battery pack.

01:42:42.820 --> 01:42:45.620
Of course, not
everything is soft.

01:42:45.620 --> 01:42:47.920
You can see the hard
battery underneath here.

01:42:47.920 --> 01:42:49.150
We can scale this.

01:42:49.150 --> 01:42:51.220
We can add panels.

01:42:51.220 --> 01:42:53.170
We can charge multiple things.

01:42:53.170 --> 01:42:55.000
We start creating some economy.

01:42:55.000 --> 01:42:58.150
And that leads to the kind
of second principle, which

01:42:58.150 --> 01:43:00.220
is that people have
a lot of knowledge.

01:43:00.220 --> 01:43:02.710
And in so many cultures
around the world,

01:43:02.710 --> 01:43:06.560
people have a very direct
connection to textiles.

01:43:06.560 --> 01:43:09.940
And so we found that by
dematerializing solar energy

01:43:09.940 --> 01:43:12.640
and trying to turn it as much
as possible into a problem

01:43:12.640 --> 01:43:15.280
about textiles and
reflectivity, we

01:43:15.280 --> 01:43:17.600
can really draw on
local knowledge.

01:43:17.600 --> 01:43:20.170
Which is good because it
creates jobs for people

01:43:20.170 --> 01:43:21.130
and that's good.

01:43:21.130 --> 01:43:23.230
But even more than
that, it creates a sense

01:43:23.230 --> 01:43:24.970
of stewardship for energy.

01:43:24.970 --> 01:43:27.250
When people are invested
in making something--

01:43:27.250 --> 01:43:31.120
and this pattern was actually
made by women in a favela group

01:43:31.120 --> 01:43:32.740
called Copa Hoca.

01:43:32.740 --> 01:43:35.600
When people make something,
they feel like they own it.

01:43:35.600 --> 01:43:38.150
That's the cool
thing about making.

01:43:38.150 --> 01:43:40.880
So in the different places where
a portable light has worked,

01:43:40.880 --> 01:43:42.740
we work with these
different skill sets.

01:43:42.740 --> 01:43:46.030
This is a weaver in the
Sierra Madre mountains

01:43:46.030 --> 01:43:49.210
who actually made a little
kit which looks like this.

01:43:49.210 --> 01:43:53.090
That's actually the lead
over there on the right.

01:43:53.090 --> 01:43:55.270
And then sometimes you
don't have a single producer

01:43:55.270 --> 01:43:58.822
making something, because that's
not the best way or the way

01:43:58.822 --> 01:43:59.530
that people work.

01:43:59.530 --> 01:44:01.370
Because people like
to work together.

01:44:01.370 --> 01:44:03.370
So we've developed
a kind of a model

01:44:03.370 --> 01:44:06.790
where a one group-- one
community-- here the women

01:44:06.790 --> 01:44:09.640
of Copa Roca in Rio de Janeiro.

01:44:09.640 --> 01:44:12.010
And then here are another
set of benefactors,

01:44:12.010 --> 01:44:15.020
completely in the North
of Brazil in the Amazon.

01:44:15.020 --> 01:44:19.270
So you have two groups that
benefit in making things.

01:44:19.270 --> 01:44:21.820
And then last, that kind
of adaptable principal.

01:44:21.820 --> 01:44:24.160
Like, be as flexible
as possible.

01:44:24.160 --> 01:44:26.290
When you have a
flexible solar material,

01:44:26.290 --> 01:44:28.720
can the whole kit be adaptable?

01:44:28.720 --> 01:44:32.260
Can you actually use something
like this as a lantern?

01:44:32.260 --> 01:44:35.600
Can you then be able to take
it in a different position,

01:44:35.600 --> 01:44:38.710
walk with it, take it outside
and use it in different areas?

01:44:38.710 --> 01:44:42.290
Because more often than
not, people need two things.

01:44:42.290 --> 01:44:44.050
They need an ambient
lighting system

01:44:44.050 --> 01:44:46.330
and they need a task
lighting system.

01:44:46.330 --> 01:44:48.050
So we're trying to
think about that.

01:44:48.050 --> 01:44:51.850
So we are developing
really a kind

01:44:51.850 --> 01:44:55.060
of a shapeshifting
technology, if you will.

01:44:55.060 --> 01:44:57.460
This is sort of the
opposite of a brand.

01:44:57.460 --> 01:44:59.290
It's kind of above a brand.

01:44:59.290 --> 01:45:03.610
Where you actually don't
see the technology and you

01:45:03.610 --> 01:45:05.680
see glimpses of the
same kinds of things.

01:45:05.680 --> 01:45:09.130
You might recognize one or
two panels in these kits.

01:45:09.130 --> 01:45:11.350
But different textile
configurations

01:45:11.350 --> 01:45:14.350
that change the shape
and adapt it to the skill

01:45:14.350 --> 01:45:18.980
sets in local cultures and also,
the need of local cultures.

01:45:18.980 --> 01:45:25.430
So we can, for example, look at
what this looks like in Mexico,

01:45:25.430 --> 01:45:26.630
in Kenya.

01:45:26.630 --> 01:45:28.850
You have tailors
who have designers.

01:45:28.850 --> 01:45:30.530
So they're putting that in bags.

01:45:30.530 --> 01:45:33.860
That's our flat bag,
flexible light and power.

01:45:33.860 --> 01:45:39.440
In Nicaragua, a little bit
different kind of design.

01:45:39.440 --> 01:45:40.430
Different kind of use.

01:45:40.430 --> 01:45:42.950
People charge as they walk
up and down the beach.

01:45:42.950 --> 01:45:44.930
And then at night, they
protect turtle eggs

01:45:44.930 --> 01:45:47.720
and they get paid by
conservation agencies

01:45:47.720 --> 01:45:48.280
to do that.

01:45:48.280 --> 01:45:50.090
So that's another
source of income.

01:45:50.090 --> 01:45:52.730
And in Brazil, the
development that we're

01:45:52.730 --> 01:45:55.190
moving to of actually
using natural materials

01:45:55.190 --> 01:45:58.890
and using disposable
degradable materials.

01:45:58.890 --> 01:46:01.850
So I'm going to just show you
a very short video that's going

01:46:01.850 --> 01:46:04.250
to take my last minute up.

01:46:04.250 --> 01:46:07.275
And it's from the Portable
Light Project in Brazil.

01:46:07.275 --> 01:46:08.150
And it was actually--

01:46:08.150 --> 01:46:10.595
I think you'll like this
because it was actually--

01:46:10.595 --> 01:46:14.030
it's sort of the opposite
of a super produced video.

01:46:14.030 --> 01:46:16.550
And then it was
shot in large part

01:46:16.550 --> 01:46:21.620
with handheld cell phone video
footage that was actually

01:46:21.620 --> 01:46:23.000
powered by portable light.

01:46:23.000 --> 01:46:25.410
And there was a little bit
that was done by our partners.

01:46:25.410 --> 01:46:27.470
It's in Portuguese, so
I hope you won't mind.

01:46:27.470 --> 01:46:29.150
I think you'll understand it.

01:46:29.150 --> 01:46:33.030
So this shows the context
of Portable Light in Brazil,

01:46:33.030 --> 01:46:35.900
where we are nationalizing
this technology

01:46:35.900 --> 01:46:39.140
and trying to put it in
many different small river

01:46:39.140 --> 01:46:41.340
towns in the Amazon.

01:46:41.340 --> 01:46:43.620
So I'll play the video now.

01:46:43.620 --> 01:46:45.920
There should be some sound.

01:46:45.920 --> 01:46:47.600
Yeah.

01:46:47.600 --> 01:46:49.400
So this was their
idea, put music to it.

01:46:49.400 --> 01:46:51.710
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

01:46:51.710 --> 01:46:56.690
- [SPEAKING PORTUGUESE]

01:46:56.690 --> 01:46:59.450
So we're working mostly
in the Rio [INAUDIBLE]..

01:46:59.450 --> 01:47:01.030
This is a typical village there.

01:47:07.490 --> 01:47:17.760
- [SPEAKING PORTUGUESE]

01:47:17.760 --> 01:47:19.920
All of these towns are
Portable Light towns.

01:47:24.730 --> 01:48:09.887
- [SPEAKING PORTUGUESE]

01:48:09.887 --> 01:48:10.470
[END PLAYBACK]

01:48:10.470 --> 01:48:11.280
All right, that's it.

01:48:11.280 --> 01:48:11.720
Thank you.

01:48:11.720 --> 01:48:12.470
I'll stop it here.

01:48:17.778 --> 01:48:22.260
[APPLAUSE]

01:48:29.250 --> 01:48:31.920
Well, there is
benefits going last.

01:48:31.920 --> 01:48:33.780
I've had such
great introductions

01:48:33.780 --> 01:48:36.300
for this technology.

01:48:36.300 --> 01:48:39.750
And what my group has done
over the past couple of years

01:48:39.750 --> 01:48:41.940
is actually decided
to make solar cells

01:48:41.940 --> 01:48:43.740
on paper substrates.

01:48:43.740 --> 01:48:45.210
And you may wonder why that is.

01:48:45.210 --> 01:48:49.050
We saw the great prop that
Jeff was passing around.

01:48:49.050 --> 01:48:53.910
And it had a lot of weight in it
because of the two glass panels

01:48:53.910 --> 01:48:56.010
that encapsulate the solar cell.

01:48:56.010 --> 01:48:58.500
So obviously, a paper
will be lighter weight.

01:48:58.500 --> 01:48:59.850
It will also be a lot cheaper.

01:48:59.850 --> 01:49:02.130
If you think about
design, the real reason

01:49:02.130 --> 01:49:05.830
that solar is not adopted more
is it's just too expensive.

01:49:05.830 --> 01:49:07.530
And so for solar
to be attractive,

01:49:07.530 --> 01:49:11.040
you need to be able to hit
a price point of about $140

01:49:11.040 --> 01:49:12.450
per meter squared.

01:49:12.450 --> 01:49:15.490
And a meter squared
of glass cost $40.

01:49:15.490 --> 01:49:18.600
So you've already used
up $80 of your budget.

01:49:18.600 --> 01:49:21.420
So I need some other substrate.

01:49:21.420 --> 01:49:23.550
I could think about
using plastic.

01:49:23.550 --> 01:49:25.860
But in many cases,
plastic for electronics

01:49:25.860 --> 01:49:28.110
is actually more
expensive than glass.

01:49:28.110 --> 01:49:30.210
Because you can't
use the plastic

01:49:30.210 --> 01:49:32.040
that's rough like Coke bottles.

01:49:32.040 --> 01:49:34.080
You need to use special
material from DuPont,

01:49:34.080 --> 01:49:35.430
which is ultra thin.

01:49:35.430 --> 01:49:38.790
When they made a Kindle
using a plastic display,

01:49:38.790 --> 01:49:41.130
it ended up costing $1,200.

01:49:41.130 --> 01:49:44.550
So we decided to go with paper.

01:49:44.550 --> 01:49:46.800
And this requires us to
have processes that work

01:49:46.800 --> 01:49:50.520
with the rough substrates
that represent paper and also

01:49:50.520 --> 01:49:52.270
the low temperatures.

01:49:52.270 --> 01:49:56.910
So what you see here are
actually a 3D solar cell.

01:49:56.910 --> 01:49:59.940
This one's in the form
of a paper airplane.

01:49:59.940 --> 01:50:01.440
And there's a YouTube
video if you'd

01:50:01.440 --> 01:50:05.880
like to look at it that shows
us folding it up and running it.

01:50:05.880 --> 01:50:08.610
We like to think about paper
as maybe being delicate.

01:50:08.610 --> 01:50:13.515
But we can actually run paper
solar cells under water.

01:50:13.515 --> 01:50:15.890
And one of the wonderful things
about this technology, it

01:50:15.890 --> 01:50:18.930
really is an intersection
of disciplines.

01:50:18.930 --> 01:50:20.770
I'm in chemical engineering.

01:50:20.770 --> 01:50:24.480
So I really think mostly
about molecules and processes.

01:50:24.480 --> 01:50:27.330
And my collaborator in
electrical engineering,

01:50:27.330 --> 01:50:30.180
Vladimir Bulavich
is really an expert

01:50:30.180 --> 01:50:35.410
in organic devices
and their properties.

01:50:35.410 --> 01:50:37.140
So we put those two together.

01:50:37.140 --> 01:50:40.380
We had a fantastic
group of students.

01:50:40.380 --> 01:50:44.970
Myles Clark Bar won the Student
Lemelson prize for inventors.

01:50:44.970 --> 01:50:48.660
Christopher Voice is now a
Gates Fellow at Cambridge.

01:50:48.660 --> 01:50:53.940
And Sungyak Bihn is
a professor in Korea.

01:50:53.940 --> 01:50:55.150
And it goes on and on.

01:50:55.150 --> 01:50:57.600
So really a lot of
people contributed

01:50:57.600 --> 01:51:01.260
to this particular effort.

01:51:01.260 --> 01:51:03.840
So how is it that you can
make a solar cell on paper?

01:51:03.840 --> 01:51:06.042
Well, you need low
temperature processes.

01:51:06.042 --> 01:51:08.250
And we call the one that we
invented in our lab vapor

01:51:08.250 --> 01:51:09.210
printing.

01:51:09.210 --> 01:51:11.370
And it's analogous
to things that people

01:51:11.370 --> 01:51:13.560
use to build cell
phones and laptops,

01:51:13.560 --> 01:51:15.630
except we're doing
organic materials.

01:51:15.630 --> 01:51:18.030
And we paid particular
attention to the chemistry

01:51:18.030 --> 01:51:20.310
so that we could do
it at low temperature.

01:51:20.310 --> 01:51:21.850
And we get many things at once.

01:51:21.850 --> 01:51:24.240
We put the paper in the reactor.

01:51:24.240 --> 01:51:27.510
And we basically
sublimate or turn

01:51:27.510 --> 01:51:31.150
into gas vapor reactants
or solid reactants.

01:51:31.150 --> 01:51:33.840
And we put a mask in
front of the paper.

01:51:33.840 --> 01:51:37.180
And we get exactly the
design that we want.

01:51:37.180 --> 01:51:40.800
And if you look at one of the
masked areas at the top here,

01:51:40.800 --> 01:51:42.690
you look at the
close up, you can

01:51:42.690 --> 01:51:47.440
see the uncoated paper has
this rough fibrous structure.

01:51:47.440 --> 01:51:51.030
And when we have the vapor
printing, what you see

01:51:51.030 --> 01:51:53.320
is that you still
see that structure.

01:51:53.320 --> 01:51:56.040
So you're basically shrink
wrapping each of those fibers,

01:51:56.040 --> 01:51:58.840
making a very adherent surface.

01:51:58.840 --> 01:52:00.990
So this gives you
durability in what

01:52:00.990 --> 01:52:04.520
you're doing so that your
device doesn't delaminate.

01:52:04.520 --> 01:52:06.270
And what we vapor
printed here is actually

01:52:06.270 --> 01:52:07.737
a transparent electrode.

01:52:07.737 --> 01:52:10.320
So it's going to be able to let
the light into the active part

01:52:10.320 --> 01:52:12.370
of the device.

01:52:12.370 --> 01:52:15.120
The other great thing about
doing something on paper

01:52:15.120 --> 01:52:17.500
is that you can do what's
called roll to roll processing.

01:52:17.500 --> 01:52:20.550
And so this is analogous
to how newspapers are made.

01:52:20.550 --> 01:52:22.500
And it's a much
more economical way

01:52:22.500 --> 01:52:26.010
to do solar cell manufacture.

01:52:26.010 --> 01:52:28.380
You can imagine that
you really can't roll

01:52:28.380 --> 01:52:30.270
to roll process silicon wafers.

01:52:30.270 --> 01:52:33.240
And that's one of
the benefits of going

01:52:33.240 --> 01:52:35.282
to flexible substrates.

01:52:35.282 --> 01:52:36.240
So here's how it works.

01:52:36.240 --> 01:52:37.490
We actually have a paper mask.

01:52:37.490 --> 01:52:40.230
This was made with a
conventional stencil cutter

01:52:40.230 --> 01:52:42.540
like you would use
for a hobby shop.

01:52:42.540 --> 01:52:47.190
And you put that mask on top
of a piece of tracing paper,

01:52:47.190 --> 01:52:50.040
it goes upside down
in the reactor.

01:52:50.040 --> 01:52:55.020
And out comes your piece of
tracing paper with 250 bricks.

01:52:55.020 --> 01:52:58.710
And these are electrically
conductive bricks.

01:52:58.710 --> 01:53:04.560
And we can do this on a variety
of papers and also textiles.

01:53:04.560 --> 01:53:07.830
There's a t-shirt fabric,
stretchable rubber.

01:53:07.830 --> 01:53:11.280
So we can also make
stretchable solar cells.

01:53:11.280 --> 01:53:13.440
And so it's your
choice of design

01:53:13.440 --> 01:53:16.060
of what substrate you
would like to use.

01:53:16.060 --> 01:53:18.630
And so you can think
about applications

01:53:18.630 --> 01:53:20.520
which are not solar fields.

01:53:20.520 --> 01:53:23.760
And I think you saw many of
those kinds of applications

01:53:23.760 --> 01:53:25.320
tonight.

01:53:25.320 --> 01:53:26.880
And so to complete the device--

01:53:26.880 --> 01:53:28.830
I just showed you one
layer-- basically,

01:53:28.830 --> 01:53:31.560
you have to do this
several more times.

01:53:31.560 --> 01:53:33.720
And the second layer
is actually the layer

01:53:33.720 --> 01:53:36.150
that absorbs the sun's light.

01:53:36.150 --> 01:53:38.880
And this actually
is a blue car paint.

01:53:38.880 --> 01:53:40.590
So it's very cheap.

01:53:40.590 --> 01:53:45.870
It contains copper,
the cathode is silver.

01:53:45.870 --> 01:53:48.180
And the way you
put them together

01:53:48.180 --> 01:53:51.135
actually allows you to hook
up solar cells in series

01:53:51.135 --> 01:53:53.040
to get the voltage
that you want.

01:53:53.040 --> 01:53:55.380
We're doing 250 cells.

01:53:55.380 --> 01:53:58.500
The other two things that are
nice about this particular way

01:53:58.500 --> 01:54:01.470
of fabricating cells are
that all the elements

01:54:01.470 --> 01:54:06.330
that are in this device are
things that are Earth abundant.

01:54:06.330 --> 01:54:08.100
So you can find
carbon everywhere

01:54:08.100 --> 01:54:09.820
and sulfur everywhere.

01:54:09.820 --> 01:54:11.220
We don't have
things like indium,

01:54:11.220 --> 01:54:13.770
which are many conventional
solar cells, which

01:54:13.770 --> 01:54:15.000
are a rare Earth metals.

01:54:15.000 --> 01:54:18.600
And also make the
cells more expensive.

01:54:18.600 --> 01:54:21.340
And when you put this all
together and you test it,

01:54:21.340 --> 01:54:23.160
you can actually
get out 50 volts

01:54:23.160 --> 01:54:27.250
from this seven centimeter
square piece of paper.

01:54:27.250 --> 01:54:31.600
And you can use this
to drive devices.

01:54:31.600 --> 01:54:33.940
So I want to show you
a couple of movies.

01:54:33.940 --> 01:54:37.980
This is a smaller piece
of that paper solar cell.

01:54:37.980 --> 01:54:39.900
And it was encapsulated
in plastic.

01:54:46.020 --> 01:54:48.640
And we'll light it
from the bottom.

01:54:48.640 --> 01:54:50.610
This is AM 1.5 light.

01:54:50.610 --> 01:54:54.580
That's typically what's
used to simulate the sun.

01:54:54.580 --> 01:54:59.150
And you can see that you can
in fact drive this LED clock.

01:54:59.150 --> 01:55:00.400
And this is being done in air.

01:55:00.400 --> 01:55:03.120
So one of the knocks
against organic solar cells

01:55:03.120 --> 01:55:05.640
is they tend to be nondurable.

01:55:05.640 --> 01:55:07.860
So this is the cheapest
role to role reactor

01:55:07.860 --> 01:55:11.370
we have in our lab-- or our
little Laserjet printer.

01:55:11.370 --> 01:55:13.230
And we put it through--

01:55:13.230 --> 01:55:15.330
the cell through there
to show that it survives

01:55:15.330 --> 01:55:17.308
the flexing and thermal cycles.

01:55:17.308 --> 01:55:19.600
We wanted to print something--
we're not very creative.

01:55:19.600 --> 01:55:20.790
We just put MIT on there.

01:55:20.790 --> 01:55:22.800
That's what happens.

01:55:22.800 --> 01:55:26.290
But after it goes through
that process, it still works.

01:55:26.290 --> 01:55:29.850
And I have demos
with me here that

01:55:29.850 --> 01:55:32.700
have been around for
a year and a half now.

01:55:32.700 --> 01:55:35.100
And the vapor process
just as much pure.

01:55:35.100 --> 01:55:40.290
And so some of the degradation
mechanisms are gone.

01:55:40.290 --> 01:55:43.590
Not only can we
flex solar cells,

01:55:43.590 --> 01:55:46.500
we actually can fold them.

01:55:59.200 --> 01:56:02.640
So here, you see this particular
cell's putting out 26 volts.

01:56:02.640 --> 01:56:03.960
This again is in air.

01:56:03.960 --> 01:56:07.890
And you'll see the hands of
Myles Clark Bar actually coming

01:56:07.890 --> 01:56:10.440
in and folding up
the solar cell.

01:56:10.440 --> 01:56:12.415
And the volt meter
shows the voltage drops.

01:56:12.415 --> 01:56:14.040
And we expect that
because there's less

01:56:14.040 --> 01:56:15.870
cross-section with the light.

01:56:15.870 --> 01:56:19.020
But as you expand the
solar cell back out,

01:56:19.020 --> 01:56:22.390
you can see that the
voltage again increases.

01:56:22.390 --> 01:56:24.330
And I think this
form factor naturally

01:56:24.330 --> 01:56:26.430
you'd think about
foldable blinds

01:56:26.430 --> 01:56:28.900
that you could have
as indoor solar cells,

01:56:28.900 --> 01:56:30.750
perhaps with
batteries on the head

01:56:30.750 --> 01:56:34.347
or directly connected
to a lighting system.

01:56:34.347 --> 01:56:36.930
And that way, you would get rid
of all the transmission losses

01:56:36.930 --> 01:56:39.540
that you have in traditional--

01:56:39.540 --> 01:56:43.560
a traditional lighting
system in a house.

01:56:43.560 --> 01:56:47.580
And the final movie
here is the solar cell.

01:56:47.580 --> 01:56:49.180
This one looks a
little bit different.

01:56:49.180 --> 01:56:53.310
So in the first movie, you saw
that we used plastic lamination

01:56:53.310 --> 01:56:55.530
like you would have
for driver's license.

01:56:55.530 --> 01:56:58.440
In the second movie, we
had no lamination at all.

01:56:58.440 --> 01:57:02.580
In this one, we
do another method

01:57:02.580 --> 01:57:05.820
from our lab that puts
on a hydrophobic coating.

01:57:05.820 --> 01:57:09.510
And here, you can actually
see that the solar cell can

01:57:09.510 --> 01:57:13.620
go underwater and it still
retains its papery quality.

01:57:13.620 --> 01:57:15.310
It has two axis bending.

01:57:15.310 --> 01:57:18.150
So if you have plastic, it'll
just roll up into a cylinder.

01:57:18.150 --> 01:57:22.170
But paper and textiles actually
have to access bending.

01:57:22.170 --> 01:57:24.840
So this particular
piece of solar cell,

01:57:24.840 --> 01:57:27.150
you're handling
fairly roughly in air

01:57:27.150 --> 01:57:28.440
and putting it under water.

01:57:28.440 --> 01:57:32.580
And it continues to put out
the same amount of voltage

01:57:32.580 --> 01:57:35.160
that you have here.

01:57:35.160 --> 01:57:38.100
And you can see that this
really is much more lightweight

01:57:38.100 --> 01:57:43.110
and much more flexible than the
traditional solar cells that

01:57:43.110 --> 01:57:44.190
exist.

01:57:44.190 --> 01:57:48.090
And we're really excited about
improving the efficiency.

01:57:48.090 --> 01:57:51.570
Currently, this cell would
be about 1% efficient.

01:57:51.570 --> 01:57:54.600
We hope by the end of this
year, we'll have 5% efficiency.

01:57:54.600 --> 01:57:58.260
And that will really start to
be interesting for designing

01:57:58.260 --> 01:58:02.520
novel applications in solar.

01:58:02.520 --> 01:58:04.350
And some of those
are pictured here.

01:58:04.350 --> 01:58:08.670
What if you had wallpaper
that was actually solar cells?

01:58:08.670 --> 01:58:12.390
You had a jacket you could wear
to charge your mobile device.

01:58:12.390 --> 01:58:16.620
We've actually created a
solar cell on a postage stamp

01:58:16.620 --> 01:58:18.720
just as a demonstration of size.

01:58:18.720 --> 01:58:21.090
This one was 2% efficient.

01:58:21.090 --> 01:58:24.510
Curtains, I think, Sheila's
had some designs and thoughts

01:58:24.510 --> 01:58:25.290
about that.

01:58:25.290 --> 01:58:27.090
If you just want to
put them on your roof,

01:58:27.090 --> 01:58:28.830
it's much more
lightweight and you

01:58:28.830 --> 01:58:32.370
don't have to do structural
changes to your building

01:58:32.370 --> 01:58:35.430
to be able to support the heavy
weight of the solar cells.

01:58:35.430 --> 01:58:38.700
Or you can take the power
with you with the tents.

01:58:38.700 --> 01:58:41.580
So I hope that we're able
to do this in a large area

01:58:41.580 --> 01:58:43.980
with the roll to
roll processing.

01:58:43.980 --> 01:58:48.090
We've been working on this
for about five years now.

01:58:48.090 --> 01:58:51.270
And I hope that will
really be at scale

01:58:51.270 --> 01:58:54.760
that this can be commercial in
another three to five years.

01:58:54.760 --> 01:58:57.990
So it does take
continued investment.

01:58:57.990 --> 01:59:01.170
And I'd be happy if you
have any ideas about where

01:59:01.170 --> 01:59:03.990
these solar cells on
paper could be used.

01:59:03.990 --> 01:59:05.070
I'd love to hear them.

01:59:05.070 --> 01:59:06.347
Thank you very much.

01:59:06.347 --> 01:59:10.083
[APPLAUSE]

01:59:12.430 --> 01:59:14.690
OK, well, that was
really exciting.

01:59:14.690 --> 01:59:17.560
Thank you both for
your presentations.

01:59:17.560 --> 01:59:21.460
Since both of you are
looking at a soft version--

01:59:21.460 --> 01:59:22.540
you've called it soft.

01:59:22.540 --> 01:59:24.040
In your case, clearly
it's foldable,

01:59:24.040 --> 01:59:26.560
it's all these other things.

01:59:26.560 --> 01:59:28.810
What have you found?

01:59:28.810 --> 01:59:31.480
How have you seen this as
a paradigm shift in solar?

01:59:31.480 --> 01:59:34.750
This idea of softness
and flexibility.

01:59:34.750 --> 01:59:37.025
What are the--
beyond the attributes

01:59:37.025 --> 01:59:38.650
that you started to
outline, how do you

01:59:38.650 --> 01:59:41.200
see this as something
that is remarkably

01:59:41.200 --> 01:59:43.510
different than the
rooftop solar array

01:59:43.510 --> 01:59:45.400
that we've seen previously?

01:59:45.400 --> 01:59:47.140
I mean, even for
the rooftop array,

01:59:47.140 --> 01:59:50.560
just lowers the barrier
to implementation.

01:59:50.560 --> 01:59:52.660
You think about
installing it yourself.

01:59:52.660 --> 01:59:56.230
You could staple it to the roof.

01:59:56.230 --> 01:59:59.420
You don't need a professional
installer to do that.

01:59:59.420 --> 02:00:02.350
And I think the mobility of
something that's lightweight

02:00:02.350 --> 02:00:05.680
is also clearly valuable.

02:00:05.680 --> 02:00:09.880
For me, I think what's really
cool about the new materials is

02:00:09.880 --> 02:00:11.920
the Earth abundant.

02:00:11.920 --> 02:00:16.210
And the fact that
you could actually

02:00:16.210 --> 02:00:18.520
recapture some of
those materials

02:00:18.520 --> 02:00:20.110
at the end of their
life lifecycle.

02:00:20.110 --> 02:00:22.900
You might be able to take that
t-shirt and be able to then

02:00:22.900 --> 02:00:26.170
extract some of the
ITO or whatever it is--

02:00:26.170 --> 02:00:28.810
the different solar
layers and recapture that.

02:00:28.810 --> 02:00:31.180
So I think that's a
complete revolution

02:00:31.180 --> 02:00:32.760
in terms of infrastructure.

02:00:32.760 --> 02:00:34.390
We think of
infrastructure as hard.

02:00:34.390 --> 02:00:37.660
We think about technologies
having a single form.

02:00:37.660 --> 02:00:42.100
And really, since, I think,
the early period of the wars,

02:00:42.100 --> 02:00:43.970
we've been thinking about that.

02:00:43.970 --> 02:00:45.580
So the idea that it
can be adaptable,

02:00:45.580 --> 02:00:48.800
that it could exist on different
substrates is super exciting.

02:00:48.800 --> 02:00:51.400
Well, I think people have
been obsessed with efficiency

02:00:51.400 --> 02:00:53.965
in solar panels for a
long time, which we've

02:00:53.965 --> 02:00:55.090
been talking about tonight.

02:00:55.090 --> 02:00:56.715
And so there's always
this drive to get

02:00:56.715 --> 02:01:00.732
to a more efficient thin
film or in this case, paper.

02:01:00.732 --> 02:01:02.440
So I think what I've
heard you talk about

02:01:02.440 --> 02:01:05.200
in the past is this
idea of generous excess

02:01:05.200 --> 02:01:08.260
where you have a material
that has less of an output

02:01:08.260 --> 02:01:09.785
but has more applications.

02:01:09.785 --> 02:01:12.160
So it's less tied to a specific
solar angle or something.

02:01:12.160 --> 02:01:14.140
Yeah, I think Jeff was
also talking about that.

02:01:14.140 --> 02:01:14.890
Yeah, exactly.

02:01:14.890 --> 02:01:20.050
As material costs become
less and you have a low heat

02:01:20.050 --> 02:01:23.050
way of printing
fairly large areas

02:01:23.050 --> 02:01:26.710
or even smaller areas in a
very cost effective way, then

02:01:26.710 --> 02:01:31.630
I think you really do change
the idea of efficiency.

02:01:31.630 --> 02:01:34.060
And so if something
is easy to make

02:01:34.060 --> 02:01:36.850
and has a low
environmental footprint,

02:01:36.850 --> 02:01:39.970
it doesn't have to
last 20 to 25 years.

02:01:39.970 --> 02:01:42.248
It could, but it
doesn't have to.

02:01:42.248 --> 02:01:44.290
Because we can dispose of
it just in the same way

02:01:44.290 --> 02:01:47.980
we drop a leaf on the ground and
a tree will grow another leaf.

02:01:47.980 --> 02:01:50.900
So it's a pretty radical new
way of thinking of things.

02:01:50.900 --> 02:01:51.400
Yeah.

02:01:51.400 --> 02:01:55.450
I do think the solar industry
currently is very conservative.

02:01:55.450 --> 02:01:58.630
And all of these criteria
for lasting really

02:01:58.630 --> 02:02:01.240
go with being out
in a solar field.

02:02:01.240 --> 02:02:04.910
So they want stability
that last 20 years.

02:02:04.910 --> 02:02:08.440
But if you really want a solar
cell that perhaps integrates

02:02:08.440 --> 02:02:11.380
directly with your cell
phone to charge it,

02:02:11.380 --> 02:02:13.940
no one is keeping their
cell phone 20 years.

02:02:13.940 --> 02:02:16.660
And so there's
absolutely no reason

02:02:16.660 --> 02:02:18.550
that that should be
a barrier to entry

02:02:18.550 --> 02:02:20.020
for these new technologies.

02:02:20.020 --> 02:02:20.800
It's true.

02:02:20.800 --> 02:02:24.400
We're being asked--
innovators and creators are

02:02:24.400 --> 02:02:26.980
being asked to compare
new solar materials

02:02:26.980 --> 02:02:29.290
or emerging solar
materials with mainstream

02:02:29.290 --> 02:02:31.210
electrical technologies.

02:02:31.210 --> 02:02:33.673
And so 25 years, all
this kind of stuff.

02:02:33.673 --> 02:02:35.590
And if you think about
it, that wasn't the way

02:02:35.590 --> 02:02:38.410
that even mainstream
electricity developed.

02:02:38.410 --> 02:02:43.160
Edison went to the capital
and-- various different capitals

02:02:43.160 --> 02:02:43.660
in Europe.

02:02:43.660 --> 02:02:45.010
He went to Berlin.

02:02:45.010 --> 02:02:47.620
And he asked people
whether they would

02:02:47.620 --> 02:02:49.660
like to be the first
European capital

02:02:49.660 --> 02:02:53.440
to have his AC
distribution system.

02:02:53.440 --> 02:02:54.940
And they said, what do you mean?

02:02:54.940 --> 02:02:56.902
We're going to put
poles in the ground

02:02:56.902 --> 02:02:59.110
and then you're going to
have wires going in between?

02:02:59.110 --> 02:03:00.568
And that's going
to be electricity?

02:03:00.568 --> 02:03:01.540
And he said, yes.

02:03:01.540 --> 02:03:03.130
And they said, forget it.

02:03:03.130 --> 02:03:06.700
So his first application
was actually a DC generator

02:03:06.700 --> 02:03:07.690
on board a ship.

02:03:07.690 --> 02:03:09.160
A very small scale--

02:03:09.160 --> 02:03:12.220
what we would call a
niche application today.

02:03:12.220 --> 02:03:15.490
But that's really how mainstream
electricity and our grid

02:03:15.490 --> 02:03:17.230
that we know today got started.

02:03:17.230 --> 02:03:19.480
Through a series of
these very inventive

02:03:19.480 --> 02:03:21.590
and kind of corner markets.

02:03:21.590 --> 02:03:23.050
And I think it's
very interesting

02:03:23.050 --> 02:03:25.480
that these mobile
applications, you're

02:03:25.480 --> 02:03:27.723
seeing them insert
in rural areas

02:03:27.723 --> 02:03:29.140
because then you
don't really have

02:03:29.140 --> 02:03:31.430
to compete against grid power.

02:03:31.430 --> 02:03:34.450
And I think that
that is essential.

02:03:34.450 --> 02:03:37.030
And also transportation
that works in these areas

02:03:37.030 --> 02:03:38.360
are very limited.

02:03:38.360 --> 02:03:41.860
And so being lightweight is
really a critical factor.

02:03:41.860 --> 02:03:45.100
Being foldable, you don't want
to take this huge glass panel

02:03:45.100 --> 02:03:48.490
and try to get it up some
Rocky Mountain road somewhere.

02:03:48.490 --> 02:03:50.740
Because breakability, even
though it's good for grant

02:03:50.740 --> 02:03:57.270
getting and mayors, it can
cause trouble out in the field.

02:03:57.270 --> 02:03:58.190
Glare.

02:03:58.190 --> 02:03:58.690
Glare.

02:03:58.690 --> 02:03:59.620
Glare.

02:03:59.620 --> 02:04:01.150
Oh, glare.

02:04:01.150 --> 02:04:02.140
Sorry.

02:04:02.140 --> 02:04:03.380
Her device works too well.

02:04:03.380 --> 02:04:03.880
Yeah.

02:04:06.370 --> 02:04:09.340
I'll just ask one more
question, then I'll open it up.

02:04:09.340 --> 02:04:11.830
So you kind of are getting at
it with the Edison comment.

02:04:11.830 --> 02:04:13.570
But we've always
thought about technology

02:04:13.570 --> 02:04:16.240
as something that's had to
be scaled to an incredibly

02:04:16.240 --> 02:04:18.345
large degree for it
to be cost effective

02:04:18.345 --> 02:04:19.970
and distributable
and all these things.

02:04:19.970 --> 02:04:22.420
But it seems like in
both cases, what you're

02:04:22.420 --> 02:04:25.360
doing is interesting
in that it actually

02:04:25.360 --> 02:04:27.910
doesn't have to scale in the
conventional way we think of.

02:04:27.910 --> 02:04:30.040
You're talking about
engaging local communities.

02:04:30.040 --> 02:04:32.200
You're talking about
something that can be--

02:04:32.200 --> 02:04:33.700
we can imagine a
future where this

02:04:33.700 --> 02:04:37.690
could be something that
happens, if not in the home,

02:04:37.690 --> 02:04:40.107
near a home.

02:04:40.107 --> 02:04:41.440
Because it's a clean technology.

02:04:41.440 --> 02:04:42.270
Because it's a clean technology.

02:04:42.270 --> 02:04:44.680
So it doesn't have to be out
in the outskirts of the city

02:04:44.680 --> 02:04:46.450
sequestered where
it can be polluting.

02:04:46.450 --> 02:04:48.850
And we live in the city
where it's not polluting.

02:04:48.850 --> 02:04:49.780
Right.

02:04:49.780 --> 02:04:52.360
So I was just wondering--

02:04:52.360 --> 02:04:56.920
I mean, it seems like this is,
again, like a paradigm shift.

02:04:56.920 --> 02:05:01.270
And so in your eyes, is it
necessary to bring these ideas

02:05:01.270 --> 02:05:02.650
to a large scale?

02:05:02.650 --> 02:05:04.510
Or what is exciting
about them that they're

02:05:04.510 --> 02:05:07.290
nimble at these smaller scales?

02:05:07.290 --> 02:05:10.780
I actually think one
of the exciting things

02:05:10.780 --> 02:05:14.020
about our process is
that it's scalable

02:05:14.020 --> 02:05:16.030
and that you could do
it in these large roll

02:05:16.030 --> 02:05:17.290
to roll reactors.

02:05:17.290 --> 02:05:21.610
And that just the reams of
rolls of paper could come out.

02:05:21.610 --> 02:05:24.700
And then they're very easily
transportable and usable

02:05:24.700 --> 02:05:28.210
by people without
professional installation.

02:05:28.210 --> 02:05:31.010
Or you can just take it
wherever you want it.

02:05:31.010 --> 02:05:34.270
So I like the scale and
the mobility together.

02:05:34.270 --> 02:05:37.090
I think that's the
interesting intersection.

02:05:37.090 --> 02:05:39.590
I agree.

02:05:39.590 --> 02:05:40.090
All right.

02:05:43.090 --> 02:05:43.590
Question.

02:05:43.590 --> 02:05:48.580
Does it matter the
location if you're

02:05:48.580 --> 02:05:52.860
printing some sort of
cells on your jacket?

02:05:52.860 --> 02:05:59.075
[INAUDIBLE] Will it be better
for the people in Boston

02:05:59.075 --> 02:06:01.630
to be wearing these
jackets [INAUDIBLE]??

02:06:06.330 --> 02:06:09.090
Well of course,
different materials

02:06:09.090 --> 02:06:12.510
have different what's
called solar apertures.

02:06:12.510 --> 02:06:15.150
And I think Jeff talked
a little bit about this.

02:06:15.150 --> 02:06:20.190
But with glass-based silicon
monocrystalline cells,

02:06:20.190 --> 02:06:22.180
they have a relatively
narrow aperture,

02:06:22.180 --> 02:06:24.990
which means that you need to
have light directly fall on

02:06:24.990 --> 02:06:27.510
them more or less
within a range.

02:06:27.510 --> 02:06:31.900
Organic solar, and to
some extent, these panels,

02:06:31.900 --> 02:06:33.200
which are inorganic--

02:06:33.200 --> 02:06:35.550
CIGS-- have a broader aperture.

02:06:35.550 --> 02:06:39.180
So you can talk about light
that accrues over the whole day.

02:06:39.180 --> 02:06:43.290
In general, on the
organic and something

02:06:43.290 --> 02:06:46.380
that's flexible like
this allows you to not

02:06:46.380 --> 02:06:49.690
have to walk at a 45
degree angle, for example--

02:06:49.690 --> 02:06:51.210
which might be
painful and awkward--

02:06:51.210 --> 02:06:54.000
in order to collect energy
with something on your back.

02:06:54.000 --> 02:06:56.850
So you want to be able
to catch incident light.

02:06:56.850 --> 02:06:59.670
That said you still need to
have your solar panel in light.

02:06:59.670 --> 02:07:01.500
So you would need to
be committed to spend

02:07:01.500 --> 02:07:03.360
a fairly long time outdoors.

02:07:03.360 --> 02:07:07.890
Say five to six hours in
order to collect power

02:07:07.890 --> 02:07:10.800
to charge a cell phone
or to run a light--

02:07:10.800 --> 02:07:13.800
an LED lights say for
six hours or on low,

02:07:13.800 --> 02:07:15.760
let's say, for a 24 hours.

02:07:15.760 --> 02:07:20.895
So you need to be out there
with your jacket in the sun.

02:07:20.895 --> 02:07:24.800
Does it matter if
I'm north or south.

02:07:24.800 --> 02:07:26.620
Of course.

02:07:26.620 --> 02:07:28.410
The principle of
the solar panels

02:07:28.410 --> 02:07:33.230
that it needs to see sunlight
and it needs to convert

02:07:33.230 --> 02:07:35.185
that sunlight into electricity.

02:07:39.070 --> 02:07:46.090
The solar factor that immersed
in water, how does [INAUDIBLE]??

02:07:46.090 --> 02:07:48.490
So it has on it
a very thin layer

02:07:48.490 --> 02:07:51.130
of a fluoropolymer,
which is hydrophobic.

02:07:51.130 --> 02:07:55.270
So the device itself actually
never sees the water.

02:07:55.270 --> 02:07:58.600
It had almost the same principle
as that heavy plastic that's

02:07:58.600 --> 02:08:00.850
on it, but just that
a much thinner level

02:08:00.850 --> 02:08:03.130
so it still behaves like paper.

02:08:10.420 --> 02:08:13.790
So [INAUDIBLE] I was working
on designing that [INAUDIBLE]

02:08:13.790 --> 02:08:26.850
your team for a solar
light [INAUDIBLE]

02:08:26.850 --> 02:08:30.340
because of their
glass-like behavior

02:08:30.340 --> 02:08:33.660
and because of the
connection to them.

02:08:33.660 --> 02:08:36.700
[INAUDIBLE] when I look
products that you're working on,

02:08:36.700 --> 02:08:41.580
I wish [INAUDIBLE] that the
solar cell that you're holding

02:08:41.580 --> 02:08:47.600
is as tough [INAUDIBLE] if
you're putting it on your roof

02:08:47.600 --> 02:08:49.675
that you can drive a
nail straight through it.

02:08:49.675 --> 02:08:51.930
Maybe you loose a cell
or two, but at the end

02:08:51.930 --> 02:08:56.090
of the day, [INAUDIBLE]

02:08:56.090 --> 02:08:57.670
That is true with this.

02:08:57.670 --> 02:09:00.810
For example, you can
put punch holes in this

02:09:00.810 --> 02:09:02.220
and it will still operate.

02:09:02.220 --> 02:09:04.890
You can also partially shade
and it can still operate.

02:09:04.890 --> 02:09:07.920
So it has a lot of
benefits that way.

02:09:07.920 --> 02:09:09.690
And we just try to
use it structurally,

02:09:09.690 --> 02:09:11.370
so it's sort of part
of the structure

02:09:11.370 --> 02:09:15.780
that the textile
acts in tension.

02:09:15.780 --> 02:09:17.872
And then we compress
this and so we sort of

02:09:17.872 --> 02:09:19.330
try to build the
structure with it.

02:09:19.330 --> 02:09:19.770
Yeah.

02:09:19.770 --> 02:09:21.312
I mean, I think you
saw in the videos

02:09:21.312 --> 02:09:22.757
I had different sized cells.

02:09:22.757 --> 02:09:24.090
They were all made the same way.

02:09:24.090 --> 02:09:26.040
We actually use
scissors to cut them

02:09:26.040 --> 02:09:29.040
into different
shapes for testing.

02:09:29.040 --> 02:09:31.830
I did have one
incident where I was

02:09:31.830 --> 02:09:33.540
talking to a large
group at MIT and I

02:09:33.540 --> 02:09:34.860
passed around one of these.

02:09:34.860 --> 02:09:38.040
And someone actually tore
off the corner of the cell

02:09:38.040 --> 02:09:39.480
to keep for themselves.

02:09:39.480 --> 02:09:42.270
But the rest still worked
because they're really--

02:09:42.270 --> 02:09:44.620
the way they're
connected in series.

02:09:44.620 --> 02:09:47.130
But if you had a lot of this,
there's really a lot of things

02:09:47.130 --> 02:09:52.750
that you can do in terms of
the foldability of the cells.

02:09:52.750 --> 02:09:56.100
And if they're really cheap
enough and you do destroy them,

02:09:56.100 --> 02:09:57.690
it's easy to replace.

02:09:57.690 --> 02:09:59.180
I have a question
for you, Karen.

02:09:59.180 --> 02:10:03.390
On those the 50 volts
or those other metrics,

02:10:03.390 --> 02:10:05.800
are those like open
circuit voltage?

02:10:05.800 --> 02:10:08.190
Or if you apply
to load or if you

02:10:08.190 --> 02:10:11.910
looked at the sweet spot of
powerpoint tracking, what

02:10:11.910 --> 02:10:14.760
would be the kind of
typical yield in terms

02:10:14.760 --> 02:10:16.350
of current in voltage?

02:10:16.350 --> 02:10:18.100
Because that's important
for applications.

02:10:18.100 --> 02:10:18.600
Right.

02:10:18.600 --> 02:10:25.140
So the voltage there was the
VOC for everything in series.

02:10:25.140 --> 02:10:30.420
And I can't remember the current
number off the top of my head.

02:10:30.420 --> 02:10:34.220
We used it to drive
an LED display, which

02:10:34.220 --> 02:10:37.140
needs a relatively low current.

02:10:37.140 --> 02:10:40.140
We actually don't have enough
current to drive and LED

02:10:40.140 --> 02:10:40.787
on right now.

02:10:40.787 --> 02:10:42.870
So we really need to be
more efficient to do that.

02:10:42.870 --> 02:10:43.830
But at 5%, you should--

02:10:43.830 --> 02:10:45.070
Absolutely, yeah.

02:10:45.070 --> 02:10:45.570
Cool.

02:10:51.850 --> 02:10:55.360
I thought your projects that you
described just were fantastic,

02:10:55.360 --> 02:10:57.820
as well as the one
we heard earlier.

02:10:57.820 --> 02:11:01.620
Jeff had talked about 3D.

02:11:01.620 --> 02:11:03.980
And he showed some
pretty [INAUDIBLE]

02:11:03.980 --> 02:11:07.390
designs, but mentioned that it
was difficult right now to get

02:11:07.390 --> 02:11:12.730
into the shapes that
[INAUDIBLE] your paper cells

02:11:12.730 --> 02:11:15.340
[INAUDIBLE] the bill.

02:11:15.340 --> 02:11:19.330
Is there any collaboration or
any potential for maybe making

02:11:19.330 --> 02:11:22.580
this [INAUDIBLE] he
was talking to put--

02:11:22.580 --> 02:11:23.720
Yeah.

02:11:23.720 --> 02:11:24.230
I think so.

02:11:24.230 --> 02:11:26.272
I think that would be a
tremendous collaboration.

02:11:26.272 --> 02:11:28.620
And also because of
the ability to fold,

02:11:28.620 --> 02:11:30.870
you don't need a
static 3D shape.

02:11:30.870 --> 02:11:33.110
So you could
actually transform it

02:11:33.110 --> 02:11:35.180
to take best
advantage of the sun

02:11:35.180 --> 02:11:37.520
as it tracks across the sky.

02:11:37.520 --> 02:11:40.550
I also think it's just
sometimes just a neat challenge.

02:11:40.550 --> 02:11:42.980
Since I'm kind of
living in the world of,

02:11:42.980 --> 02:11:44.310
let's do something now.

02:11:44.310 --> 02:11:47.210
I'm an architect, so
we do do research.

02:11:47.210 --> 02:11:49.550
But we generally, get really
excited about making things

02:11:49.550 --> 02:11:51.800
and building things and
seeing things out in the world

02:11:51.800 --> 02:11:52.140
working.

02:11:52.140 --> 02:11:53.930
And so one thing that
you can also think about

02:11:53.930 --> 02:11:55.400
as a designer is,
just how can you

02:11:55.400 --> 02:11:58.920
work with the design
of the cell as it is?

02:11:58.920 --> 02:12:00.500
Do you really need to change it?

02:12:00.500 --> 02:12:03.870
Or can you find a way to
kind of work with that?

02:12:03.870 --> 02:12:07.400
So one of the things about
the silicon and the hard cells

02:12:07.400 --> 02:12:08.880
is that they tend to be square.

02:12:08.880 --> 02:12:12.020
That's because they're made
out of a log of silicon

02:12:12.020 --> 02:12:13.250
and then that's cut.

02:12:13.250 --> 02:12:15.540
And then actually, there's
quite a bit of waste

02:12:15.540 --> 02:12:17.930
to go from circle to a square.

02:12:17.930 --> 02:12:19.030
Then there are defects.

02:12:19.030 --> 02:12:20.550
So you have to check for those.

02:12:20.550 --> 02:12:24.110
And so those square
glass cells are sometimes

02:12:24.110 --> 02:12:27.350
difficult to work with in
terms of design intent.

02:12:27.350 --> 02:12:27.900
Yeah.

02:12:27.900 --> 02:12:29.440
I think what's really
interesting about what

02:12:29.440 --> 02:12:30.200
Sheila's doing--

02:12:30.200 --> 02:12:31.820
I think there's
Clayton Christensen

02:12:31.820 --> 02:12:33.385
has the innovator's dilemma.

02:12:33.385 --> 02:12:37.010
It says what can you do with
parts that already exist?

02:12:37.010 --> 02:12:39.980
And I think we don't know
really how people will interact

02:12:39.980 --> 02:12:42.800
with the solar or how
much they'll pay for it

02:12:42.800 --> 02:12:44.990
or what uses
they'll find for it.

02:12:44.990 --> 02:12:47.750
And I think when they
do, the next generations

02:12:47.750 --> 02:12:50.990
of technologies to come in are
even cheaper or more flexible

02:12:50.990 --> 02:12:53.810
can just insert into
those modes of operation.

02:12:58.000 --> 02:13:05.090
How far away from [INAUDIBLE]
I think just [INAUDIBLE]

02:13:05.090 --> 02:13:12.670
how far from having regular car
driving on solar [INAUDIBLE]??

02:13:12.670 --> 02:13:14.920
Well, today, there
are cars that have

02:13:14.920 --> 02:13:18.970
flexible solar panels, actually
the same material but larger.

02:13:18.970 --> 02:13:21.100
You see that integrated
into a curved roof.

02:13:21.100 --> 02:13:24.850
And typically they're
going to power some subset

02:13:24.850 --> 02:13:29.050
of applications in the car.

02:13:29.050 --> 02:13:30.643
For example, air conditioning.

02:13:30.643 --> 02:13:32.560
You can have a direct
drive solar panel, which

02:13:32.560 --> 02:13:34.000
means you don't need a battery.

02:13:34.000 --> 02:13:36.250
But when the sun
hits that car, it'll

02:13:36.250 --> 02:13:39.040
circulate a fan which will help
make the air conditioning more

02:13:39.040 --> 02:13:40.510
effective in the car.

02:13:40.510 --> 02:13:44.260
Or there can be a certain
DC load in the car--

02:13:44.260 --> 02:13:46.660
car is a DC platform--
that will be taken up--

02:13:46.660 --> 02:13:48.580
let's say the dashboard--

02:13:48.580 --> 02:13:50.500
by that particular power source.

02:13:53.170 --> 02:13:55.780
The other thing I would
say is that, again,

02:13:55.780 --> 02:14:00.490
we in the electricity grid,
look for total everything.

02:14:00.490 --> 02:14:02.750
Black or white,
like the whole car.

02:14:02.750 --> 02:14:03.760
The whole city.

02:14:03.760 --> 02:14:04.930
The whole world.

02:14:04.930 --> 02:14:07.090
And what we're
going to need to see

02:14:07.090 --> 02:14:08.800
is that there are
going to be steps.

02:14:08.800 --> 02:14:10.900
There are going to be
multiple power sources.

02:14:10.900 --> 02:14:12.370
There's going to
be a transition--

02:14:12.370 --> 02:14:14.200
an energy transition,
it's called--

02:14:14.200 --> 02:14:17.260
as we start developing
some things off grid,

02:14:17.260 --> 02:14:18.940
but not everything off grid.

02:14:18.940 --> 02:14:21.340
And so we'll have to
get used to these ideas

02:14:21.340 --> 02:14:24.100
of multiple sources,
partial powering,

02:14:24.100 --> 02:14:27.790
objects that we plug-in, objects
that we take with us, objects

02:14:27.790 --> 02:14:29.660
that we charge in our lives.

02:14:29.660 --> 02:14:31.840
So there's going to be
much more of a plurality I

02:14:31.840 --> 02:14:34.522
think of energy sources.

02:14:34.522 --> 02:14:35.980
We have time for
one more question?

02:14:41.330 --> 02:14:51.020
Along these lines [INAUDIBLE]

02:14:51.020 --> 02:14:52.360
I've thought a lot.

02:14:52.360 --> 02:14:55.690
And I've worked with Jeff on
a similar project that right

02:14:55.690 --> 02:14:57.410
now is not in the public realm.

02:14:57.410 --> 02:14:59.800
But a very interesting
project about taking sunlight

02:14:59.800 --> 02:15:02.620
and bringing it into buildings.

02:15:02.620 --> 02:15:05.150
But I think 3D is
really interesting.

02:15:05.150 --> 02:15:09.190
And I think that the
algorithm that he has that

02:15:09.190 --> 02:15:12.760
takes inputs and then generates
a series of responses to that,

02:15:12.760 --> 02:15:15.670
throwing out the ones that--
the ancestors that don't work

02:15:15.670 --> 02:15:18.440
and continuing on
is very interesting.

02:15:18.440 --> 02:15:20.620
So I think we could
be much more creative.

02:15:20.620 --> 02:15:24.907
We're stuck in what
Jimmy Carter did in 1978.

02:15:24.907 --> 02:15:26.740
He had all the good
intentions in the world.

02:15:26.740 --> 02:15:29.290
But he put a solar panel
in the White House.

02:15:29.290 --> 02:15:30.280
Put it like flat on.

02:15:30.280 --> 02:15:31.280
He said that's it.

02:15:31.280 --> 02:15:32.590
And we've just accepted that.

02:15:32.590 --> 02:15:36.058
That that should be what solar
panels do, how they operate.

02:15:36.058 --> 02:15:36.850
That they're glass.

02:15:36.850 --> 02:15:37.540
That they're hard.

02:15:37.540 --> 02:15:40.082
That they have to have direct
sunlight, et cetera, et cetera.

02:15:40.082 --> 02:15:43.090
And this materials
revolution that we've

02:15:43.090 --> 02:15:46.540
touched on in this
panel is, I think,

02:15:46.540 --> 02:15:48.670
going to change a lot of that.

02:15:48.670 --> 02:15:51.490
And I think the
investment in solar right

02:15:51.490 --> 02:15:54.400
now would be very difficult
to get a company funded

02:15:54.400 --> 02:15:56.200
in the solar area.

02:15:56.200 --> 02:16:01.060
Green energy has been viewed as
being a very poor investment.

02:16:01.060 --> 02:16:03.130
There were bankruptcies
like Solyndra.

02:16:03.130 --> 02:16:09.100
There's been huge consolidations
in the solar producers really

02:16:09.100 --> 02:16:14.090
driving each other the prices
below what can be sustained.

02:16:14.090 --> 02:16:17.200
And so you have huge
companies going bankrupt.

02:16:17.200 --> 02:16:19.240
And I think it really
hurts the technology.

02:16:19.240 --> 02:16:21.700
And someone really
needs to sustain it.

02:16:21.700 --> 02:16:24.790
I first did solar energy
when I was a graduate student

02:16:24.790 --> 02:16:26.090
25 years ago.

02:16:26.090 --> 02:16:28.715
And after my thesis, I
didn't do it for 20 years.

02:16:28.715 --> 02:16:30.340
And that's true of
a lot of scientists.

02:16:30.340 --> 02:16:33.760
And all the momentum from 25
years ago was generally lost.

02:16:33.760 --> 02:16:36.370
Most of those people
did not pick up again.

02:16:36.370 --> 02:16:38.799
And so it would be
unfortunate if we lost

02:16:38.799 --> 02:16:40.530
this round of momentum as well.

02:16:46.100 --> 02:16:49.120
Please join me in making
our [INAUDIBLE] panelists

02:16:49.120 --> 02:16:50.563
and [INAUDIBLE].

02:16:50.563 --> 02:16:55.860
[APPLAUSE]

02:16:55.860 --> 02:16:58.700
So thank you all for joining
us for tonight's program.

02:16:58.700 --> 02:17:02.480
I hope you found it as sort
of inspiring and fascinating

02:17:02.480 --> 02:17:03.330
as I did.

02:17:03.330 --> 02:17:07.309
We heard from a number of
people about their very varied

02:17:07.309 --> 02:17:07.879
projects.

02:17:07.879 --> 02:17:12.170
Their very different approaches
to using solar technology

02:17:12.170 --> 02:17:15.260
to developing technologies
to harness solar power.

02:17:15.260 --> 02:17:20.059
And I think we might all
sort of think on our own

02:17:20.059 --> 02:17:24.139
what new projects we might
want to pursue or support,

02:17:24.139 --> 02:17:28.685
how solar maybe isn't what
we always thought it was.

02:17:28.685 --> 02:17:31.100
It actually takes
many shapes and forms

02:17:31.100 --> 02:17:34.040
and has some pretty wonderful
applications for people,

02:17:34.040 --> 02:17:37.139
not just in our own community,
but around the world.

02:17:37.139 --> 02:17:40.459
Thank you very much to our
panelists and moderators.

02:17:40.459 --> 02:17:41.330
Thank you very much.

02:17:41.330 --> 02:17:44.750
And thank you all for
joining us this evening.

02:17:44.750 --> 02:17:45.770
Have a good night.

02:17:45.770 --> 02:17:47.620
[APPLAUSE]

